\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT:MARKET TALK: Thai 2Q GDP Growth Above View; THB Rises\b0\par\par\uc2 1,525 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2010\par 05:10\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0310 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0256 GMT misstated the USD/THB's level at that point in time. The correct version is as follows:) Thailand's 2Q GDP growth beats expectations, +0.2% seasonally-adjusted on-quarter despite adverse impact from political unrest, while +9.1% on-year thanks largely to continued robust performance in exports. Median forecast of five economists projected 1.3% contraction seasonally-adjusted on-quarter, poll of 11 economists tipped 7.9% on-year growth. Thailand's 1H GDP +10.6% on-year, reaffirms PM Abhisit's recent view full-year growth could top 8.0%. Stronger-than-expected economic growth drags USD/THB lower to 31.47 vs 31.50 early, likely to push stocks higher when market opens at 0300 GMT. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0309 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Morgan Stanley\uc2  says Hong Kong's underlying inflation to maintain steady uptrend due to higher food prices, private rents. Notes July underlying CPI +1.9% on year vs June's +1.7%. Says private housing rent component of CPI had been gaining sequentially for 8 months, and resumed positive on-year growth for 3 months, and consumer demand recovery will allow upside in pricing in discretionary items. Tips Hong Kong headline CPI +2.8% for 2010 and +3% in 2011 as "cost pressures from the property sector are feeding through the economy, meeting with vocal demand for higher wages and planned hiked in public transport fees." Official forecasts CPI +2.3% for 2010. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0308 GMT [Dow Jones] PREVIEW: Taiwan July industrial output likely +20.7% on year, easing off June's +24.3%, according to Dow Jones poll of four economists. Anticipated slowdown would be in line with recent Taiwan data, highlight uncertainties about global recovery in 2H. July export orders (indicator for shipments in 1-2 months) issued Friday came in +18.2% on year, slower than expected, also down on month. Taiwan government last week forecast 2H GDP to grow 4.03% on year, more than three times slower than 1H's +13.1%. Data due 0800 GMT. (jr.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0307 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/THB drops to 31.47 vs 31.50 early on hefty selling by interbank, offshore players after release of robust 2Q growth data. Despite adverse effect from worst political unrest in recent Thai history, 2Q GDP +0.2% seasonally adjusted on quarter, +9.1% on year thanks largely to solid export performance. But suspected \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  buying at around 31.47-31.50 in substantial amounts this morning helped curbing downside, say 2 dealers; "without the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's effort to slow the baht's rise, we would have seen the pair slip much deeper," says Bangkok-based dealer. Pace of THB's rise depends mainly on BOT's willingness to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with next pair support pegged at 31.40, then 31.30. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] LME metals starting this week as they finished last, with low volume, fairly directionless trade. LME 3-month copper at $7,265/ton, up $13 with 386 lots traded; Sentiment supported by inventory drawdowns with SHFE copper stocks down 3,499 tons last week, LME warehouse stocks dropping 1,800 tons Friday to 9-month low of 401,725 tons. Technically \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  says contract could test support at $7,119 ahead of a run at $7,614 next month. "Bigger picture, it would take a downside overshoot beyond $6,885 before we would believe the recent fall is something other than a correction." LME 3-month zinc at $2,055/ton, down $3 with 207 lots done; LME 3-month aluminum at $2,053/ton, up $13 with 375 lots done. (james.campbell@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0305 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last down 1.2% at 449.78 after touching 448.96 earlier, but traders say volumes thinnest seen for more than 2 years; about 9 million shares valued at VND235 billion traded so far. Investors exiting market amid continued concerns that inflows will be weak because of high interest rates, also on EPS dilution concerns with several companies seeking to raise capital with new share issues. "Investor sentiment is also weak because some brokerage firms have forecast that prices will fall further this week," says trader. Among decliners, Itaco (ITA.VH) down 4.6% at VND18,900, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) down 2.8% at VND35,000, \uc2 PetroVietnam Finance\uc2  (PVF.VH) down 2.9% at VND20,500. Trader tips immediate support at 447. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0256 GMT [Dow Jones] Thailand's 2Q GDP growth beats expectations, +0.2% seasonally-adjusted on-quarter despite adverse impact from political unrest, while +9.1% on-year thanks largely to continued robust performance in exports. Median forecast of five economists projected 1.3% contraction seasonally-adjusted on-quarter, poll of 11 economists tipped 7.9% on-year growth. Thailand's 1H GDP +10.6% on-year, reaffirms PM Abhisit's recent view full-year growth could top 8.0%. Stronger-than-expected economic growth drags USD/THB lower to 31.40 vs 31.50 early, likely to push stocks higher when market opens at 0300 GMT. (phisanu.phromchanya@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY, EUR/JPY fall on short-term players' reaction to Kyodo report that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  PM Kan didn't talk about \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when he spoke to BOJ governor Shirakawa on the phone earlier today. Kenichi Nishii, senior dealer at \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ, says players who had expected \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  or monetary easing were disappointed by news which "may encourage them to try the downside later in the day" by pushing USD/JPY below 85.00. USD/JPY, EUR/JPY down about 15 sen respectively after report. USD/JPY support at 85.00 for now vs last 85.33; EUR/JPY support at 108.00 vs 108.54. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0241 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mixed, with 5-, 10-year bonds falling moderately on profit taking, but demand for 20-year bonds remains strong. Benchmark 5-year bond's yield at 0.8991% vs 0.8965% Friday close, 10-year yield rises to 1.2420% vs near record low of 1.2350% at Friday's close. 20-year yield down to 1.6365% vs 1.6445%. "Shorter-dated bonds yields are rising on stock strength," trader at foreign bank says; "there was a wave of bond selling when Taiex hit 8000. But 20-year bonds still get good demand from long-term investors like life insurers." Adds traders to closely monitor stock market for cues. 5-year yield tipped in 0.88%-0.91% band. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up after former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor Li Yang says China not likely to hike interest rates or banks' reserve requirement ratio. Shanghai exchange government bond index 126.65 vs 126.61 last close. However, trader at Shanghai-based foreign bank notes liquidity concerns over upcoming big bond sales; Central Huijin Investment plans to sell up to CNY54 billion worth of bonds tomorrow. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Now that AUD/USD has pushed back off morning lows, cross is stuck in technical 'no man's land' with fundamental factors set to drive next move. AUD/USD opened lower on hung parliament in Australia, falling to 0.8840, but has pared those losses late morning to hit 0.8909 recently. From there, CMC Markets tips support at 0.8850, adding "if the AUD can hold this level it remains in position to run back past 90 cents when sentiment turns for the better." Still, Bank of America-Merrill Lynch strategists don't expect sentiment to turn, targeting cross at 0.8000 by year end. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.4% at 9141.01 midday after falling to intraday low of 9098.37 on comments from Japan's finance minister that he hasn't heard if PM Naoto Kan and BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa to meet this week to discuss yen's rise. But Nikkei may be supported above 9100 in afternoon, says strategist at Japanese asset management firm, after Japan government says just before midday break that Kan held talks with Shirakawa by phone Monday. Kan, Shirakawa agreed to closely watch developments in financial markets, but did not discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kyodo News says. Meanwhile, Sharp (6753.TO) off 2.6% at Y831 due to ongoing concern about LCD demand weakness; \uc2 Daiichi Sankyo\uc2  (4568.TO) off 1.8% at Y1,634 after \uc2 UBS\uc2  cuts rating to Sell. Shippers outperforming on Nikkei report that they may raise earnings outlook for this FY; \uc2 Kawasaki Kisen\uc2  (9107.TO) up 0.6% at Y342. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com) [ 08-22-10 2310ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100823e68n00025\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
