\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:JPY Up On News PM,BOJ Gov Didn't Talk  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\b0\par\par\uc2 1,424 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 23 August 2010\par 04:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0255 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY, EUR/JPY fall on short-term players' reaction to Kyodo report that \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  PM Kan didn't talk about \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FX intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  when he spoke to BOJ governor Shirakawa on the phone earlier today. Kenichi Nishii, senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ, says players who had expected \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  or monetary easing were disappointed by news which "may encourage them to try the downside later in the day" by pushing USD/JPY below 85.00. USD/JPY, EUR/JPY down about 15 sen respectively after report. USD/JPY support at 85.00 for now vs last 85.33; EUR/JPY support at 108.00 vs 108.54. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0241 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mixed, with 5-, 10-year bonds falling moderately on profit taking, but demand for 20-year bonds remains strong. Benchmark 5-year bond's yield at 0.8991% vs 0.8965% Friday close, 10-year yield rises to 1.2420% vs near record low of 1.2350% at Friday's close. 20-year yield down to 1.6365% vs 1.6445%. "Shorter-dated bonds yields are rising on stock strength," trader at foreign bank says; "there was a wave of bond selling when Taiex hit 8000. But 20-year bonds still get good demand from long-term investors like life insurers." Adds traders to closely monitor stock market for cues. 5-year yield tipped in 0.88%-0.91% band. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0234 GMT [Dow Jones] China government bonds up after former \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  advisor Li Yang says China not likely to hike interest rates or banks' reserve requirement ratio. Shanghai exchange government bond index 126.65 vs 126.61 last close. However, trader at Shanghai-based foreign bank notes liquidity concerns over upcoming big bond sales; Central Huijin Investment plans to sell up to CNY54 billion worth of bonds tomorrow. (wynne.wang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Now that AUD/USD has pushed back off morning lows, cross is stuck in technical 'no man's land' with fundamental factors set to drive next move. AUD/USD opened lower on hung parliament in Australia, falling to 0.8840, but has pared those losses late morning to hit 0.8909 recently. From there, CMC Markets tips support at 0.8850, adding "if the AUD can hold this level it remains in position to run back past 90 cents when sentiment turns for the better." Still, Bank of America-Merrill Lynch strategists don't expect sentiment to turn, targeting cross at 0.8000 by year end. (geoffrey.rogow@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0229 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 0.4% at 9141.01 midday after falling to intraday low of 9098.37 on comments from Japan's finance minister that he hasn't heard if PM Naoto Kan and BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa to meet this week to discuss yen's rise. But Nikkei may be supported above 9100 in afternoon, says strategist at Japanese asset management firm, after Japan government says just before midday break that Kan held talks with Shirakawa by phone Monday. Kan, Shirakawa agreed to closely watch developments in financial markets, but did not discuss \b \highlight22\uc2 forex intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Kyodo News says. Meanwhile, Sharp (6753.TO) off 2.6% at Y831 due to ongoing concern about LCD demand weakness; \uc2 Daiichi Sankyo\uc2  (4568.TO) off 1.8% at Y1,634 after \uc2 UBS\uc2  cuts rating to Sell. Shippers outperforming on Nikkei report that they may raise earnings outlook for this FY; \uc2 Kawasaki Kisen\uc2  (9107.TO) up 0.6% at Y342. (ayai.tomisawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0227 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW marginally lower at 1,181.3 vs 1,183.0 late Friday in Seoul, with KRW in choppy trade, tracking Kospi's just as choppy moves (last +0.3%), traders say. Exporters selling USD near 1,183 also capping upside for pair, says local bank trader. But adds, "I don't really have a clue as to which way I should be betting on," amid such choppy market moves, with neither local stocks nor Chinese shares (Shanghai Composite last +0.5%) providing clear one-sided momentum for KRW. Tips pair in 1,178-1,185 range for rest of day, with downside likely cushioned by chance of USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0217 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares slightly higher early, with earnings-related gains in banks offsetting Wall Street-induced weakness in oil firms. Shanghai Composite Index up 0.4% at 2653.33, with immediate resistance pegged at 2700. "The financial sector is looking strong today while some property developers are still up despite Beijing's tough talk over the weekend," says Zhang Li at Huatai Securities. China vice premier Li Keqiang reiterates Saturday that Beijing must continue to crack down on property speculation, boost supply of cheap public housing. But Zhang says investors remain confident in sector's long-term profitability. Among gainers, \uc2 China Vanke\uc2  (000002.SZ) up 0.4% at CNY8.68, \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) up 1.6% at CNY14.31. Shenzhen index last up 0.7% at 1124.69. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0216 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.1% at 21,001.26, still within 416-point range (20,881-21,297) index held in over past 7 sessions, remaining in consolidation mode given lack of fresh cues. Volume modest at HK$4.98 billion in opening minutes. "In our view, concerns over the pace of global economic growth will weigh on the performance of the equity market in the near term," says Daiwa. Blue chip gainers outnumber losers but only 1 stock rising more than 2%; \uc2 Cathay Pacific\uc2  (0293.HK) +2.9% at HK$20.00 likely helped by recent decline in oil prices; Sinopec (0386.HK) down 0.9% at HK$6.30 after 1H results, already worst-performing blue chip. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely lower, weighed by weak IDR, falls in several Asian markets such as Shanghai and Japan, traders say; tip support at 3100 after index ended up 0.4% Friday at all-time high of 3117.720. "Overall, it looks like that the market may consolidate after recent sharp gains and amid an absence of fresh leads," says trader at Lautandhana Securities. Car maker Astra (ASII.JK), heavyweight Telkom (TLKM.JK) may lead falls on profit-taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Virgin Blue's decision to pull out of the NZ market to be credit positive for \uc2 Air NZ\uc2  (AIR.NZ), Australia's Qantas (QAN.AU), says \uc2 Moody's\uc2 ; "overall, Virgin's decision should be good new for Qantas but even better news for \uc2 Air New Zealand\uc2 , which relies heavily on its domestic base." Adds Qantas to gain within NZ through subsidiary Jetstar but may lose some market share in Australia; "Virgin's move to go after Qantas in the Australian carrier's home base has a less certain outcome." Says Virgin's exit will ease what had been cut-rate competition in NZ, support other 2 carriers' profitability. (lucy.craymer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD weaker early at 31.9400 vs last close 31.9350 in tight range trading. So far most trades happening around 31.94-31.95, says local dealer; adds local unit to strengthen toward 31.90 in session especially if Taiwan shares touch 8000 (Taiex now up 0.8% to 7990.65). Cues to come mostly from shares, international USD moves in the near term, dealer says. (jr.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY open higher, taking cues from USD's strength against major rivals; pair last at 6.7970, vs late Friday's 6.7902, expected to move in 6.7900-6.8000 range for most of session. "Currently, either a sharp rise or a big drop in the yuan's value is unlikely," says Shanghai-based trader at local bank, citing China's easing economic growth, rising pressure on Beijing's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy. USD/CNY central parity at 6.7989 vs Friday's 6.7884. "In the near future, the dollar's performance in the global markets will continue to dominate the dollar/yuan trade in China," trader says. (rose.yu@dowjones.com) [ 08-22-10 2255ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100823e68n0001t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
