\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Seen Mixed; Worries Persist\b0\par\par\uc2 2,931 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2010\par 07:04\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par SNAPSHOT: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European bourses seen lower, govt debt mixed; major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  little changed; oil futures up, spot gold marginally lower\f28 \par \par \uc2 -Germany Resumes Campaign Against EU Bailouts\par \par \uc2 -ECB Honohan: Detect No Change In German Solid Backing For EU Debt\par \par \uc2 -SAP Says No Plans To Acquire Stake In Software AG For Now\par \par \uc2 -Celesio May Sell \uc2 Anzag\uc2  Stake To Alliance Boots- Newspaper\par \par \uc2 -Fed's Bullard: Lower Interest Paid On Reserves A Dead-End Policy\par \par \uc2 -H-P, Dell Post Profit Increases\par \par \uc2 -H-P's Lesjak: Stock Price Attractive For Share Buybacks\par \par \uc2 -US FAA Set To Levy Big Fine Against \uc2 American Airlines\uc2 -Sources\par \par \uc2 -Intel seals $7.7B takeover of McAfee\par \par \uc2 -UK Research Tax Incentive May Be On Chopping Block -Report\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Potash Corp\uc2  CEO To Get Windfall If BHP Bid Wins -Report\par \par \uc2 -Fed's Bullard: Fed 'Thinking' About Quantitative Easing Affects Mkts\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 1400 GMT US Jul Mass Layoffs\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES: \par ======== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are tipping mostly lower on Friday, reflecting muddled views of the U.S. economy's chances of sustaining a meaningful recovery. European stock markets are likely to start a bit lower with government bonds mostly mixed. The euro and sterling are little changed, while oil futures are higher and spot gold is just a bit lower.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par STOCKS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 European stock markets are likely to start slightly lower on Friday, while investors rehash Thursday's dreadful US economic data to gauge how much risk to venture.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Friday's opening, IG Markets is calling the the FTSE down 5 points, the DAX off 19 and the CAC down 11.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Armando Guglielmetti, senior strategist at InvestNews.ch in Switzerland, says traders still rate market sentiment as neutral, while company results continue to validate the view that the U.S. influence in the global economy is declining.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The German Bundesbank lifted its 2010 growth forecast for Germany to 3% from 1.9% and added that the risk of a double-dip recession in the U.S. is low, according to its monthly report for August.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors might differ with that optimistic outlook for the U.S., given the poor data it delivered.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Thursday's "news on the U.S. economy has been nothing but awful," said Paul Ashworth, senior U.S. economist at the Capital Economics consultancy in London.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The jobs situation continues to hang over the stock market like a dark cloud, essentially deflating the good news delivered earlier this week from a better-than-expected July report on industrial production," said Frederic Dickson, chief market strategist for \f28 \uc2 \f2 D.A. Davidson & Co\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In London Thursday, "the biggest fallers were the usual suspects when risk aversion takes hold with the banks and resource stocks taking a beating as recovery fears weighed on commodity prices," said Michael Hewson, a CMC Markets analyst.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. stock futures are slightly lower on Friday, after markets slid with Intel and \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  both weighing heavily on the Dow Jones Industrial Average on Thursday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Some investors worried that Intel is paying too steep a price for McAfee, whose shares it will buy for $48 each, a 60% premium to its close on Wednesday. Still, the lavish premium helped boost other stocks in the computer-security software field. McAfee rival \f28 \uc2 \f2 Symantec\f28 \uc2 \f2  jumped 78 cents, or 6.2%, to 13.37.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Otherwise, dreadful economic data on the labor market and factory sector soured hopes for a mere slowdown. Investors also worry that falling wages in the U.S. will curb consumer demand.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Everybody's worried we're tiptoeing or teetering closer to the edge of recession than we'd like," said Bernie Williams, vice president of private investment management at \f28 \uc2 \f2 USAA\f28 \uc2 \f2 . The slide in the Philadelphia Fed factory data spurred worries that the broader and closely watched Institute for Supply Management's manufacturing indexes could cease showing expansion, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Asian stock markets are mostly lower Friday after Wall Street tumbled on data indicating the U.S. jobs market remains bleak, while the yen's strength continued to drag exporters' stocks in Tokyo.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Weak U.S. data once again spoiled the mood dragging most global risk assets weaker," said RBC Capital markets in a note to clients.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The market is increasingly concerned about the yen's rise, and has priced in hopes that the BOJ will have an emergency meeting soon," said Shinichiro Matsushita, market analyst at Daiwa Securities.\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Most major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  pairs are little changed, though the yen lost some ground after Japanese Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said Friday that the country may need to act promptly on economic stimulus and that he was watching the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market with "great interest."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Minister of Economy and Fiscal Policy Satoshi Arai also said Friday that recent yen strength was a little too excessive.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In general, there's a lack of conviction" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, as poor U.S. data has investors wondering whether further deterioration in the U.S. economy is ahead, said Alvise Marino, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are unwilling to pile into positions, until they see where the economy is going," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Talk of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stem its \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's rise may have limited the losses the dollar saw after the dismaying data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York, said, "Everybody will think about the possibility of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," noting there has been no clear signal from Japanese authorities on future action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling, now little changed, is likely to trade in ranges against the dollar and euro on Friday but does have good underpinning from recent UK data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BONDS: \par ===== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed to slightly higher on Friday while investors size up Germany's commitment to the euro-zone bailout program as well as the latest U.S. data.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A member of the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's governing council said Friday he is not concerned about statements from German authorities this week suggesting they will continue to push for concessions on debt cutting from their counterparts elsewhere in Europe.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Just because people make statements doesn't mean there's not a solid backing" for previous efforts to shore up confidence in European financial markets, said Patrick Honohan, who is also governor of Ireland's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "I detect no change in the solid backing (from Germany) for the measures taken in May to underpin the sovereign debt situation," Honohan told reporters during a trip to Japan.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The latest weak U.S. numbers again highlight the fragility of the U.S. economic recovery, dragging on equities and boosting safe-haven assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 While the outlook for the U.S. economy remains downbeat, growth in the UK is also likely to slow as a tighter fiscal policy begins to bite. Expectations that the Bank of England's monetary policy will remain accommodative should support gilts, analysts say.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The MPC looks unlikely to hike rates anytime soon as it would be mindful of the impact of the spending review on the economy," says Anthony O'Brien at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. Treasurys are slightly higher on Friday, after markets rallied Thursday, led by the 30-year bond, as investors sought safe assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The economic recovery is most certainly threatened," said Dan Greenhaus, chief economic and bond strategist at Miller Tabak & Co in New York. "While a full-blown second recession is not the base-case scenario, the probability of it occurring is probably a bit higher than many have been estimating."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-dated Treasurys outperformed short-dated peers as they provide higher yields at a time when bond yields have fallen across the board. A slowing economy also reduces the risk of inflation, a major threat to bonds' fixed return over time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Treasurys continue their rally on expectations that yields certainly can move lower with little in the way of economic or inflation data to discourage them from doing so," said Christopher Sullivan, who oversees $1.6 billion as chief investment officer at United Nations Federal Credit Union in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In Tokyo on Friday, prices of Japan's government bonds rose as worries about the U.S. economy sent U.S. Treasury yields and Japanese stocks lower, boosting demand for safe-haven bonds.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Speculation that the Bank of Japan will take additional monetary easing steps also prompted investors to buy bonds, traders said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 10-year JGB yield may be in a 0.870%-0.950% range next week with a downward bias if global economic uncertainty persists, dragging on Treasury yields, says RBS strategist Akito Fukunaga.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par ENERGY: \par ======= \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Oil prices are rebounding Friday on hopes the Japanese government would ease monetary policy to stimulate its economy, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 New York's main contract, light, sweet crude for delivery in September, was up 14 cents to 74.57 dollars a barrel in morning trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Brent North Sea crude for October delivery advanced 24 cents to 75.54 dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hopes that the Japanese government would \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to curtail the appreciating yen were galvanising crude markets, said Victor Shum, senior principal of Purvin and Gertz energy consultants in Singapore.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan is the world's third-largest energy consuming nation after the United States and China.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The markets are reacting to the expectations that the Japanese government will try to stimulate the economy with further monetary easing," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par METALS: \par ====== \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Gold is showing little sign of slipping after advancing 1.4% this week and 4.3% in August so far. "It's a lack of selling combined with some sporadic buying as people look forward to September when some seasonal strength should come into the physical market," says Darren Heathcote, head of trading at Investec in Sydney. Spot gold was at $1,231.90/oz, down 50 cents from the New York close. [ 20-08-10 0504GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 London Metal Exchange metals are a tad higher after falling sharply Thursday due to the poor U.S. data. "Copper closed right on the moving average support and could look very vulnerable below this level, while aluminium stopped just short of the next support at $2,045 before recovering to $2,065," Sucden Financial says. LME 3-month copper is at $7,315/ton, up $15 from the London kerb. Aluminum is at $2,082/ton, up $16.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 20-08-10 0504GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10271.21  -144.33  -1.39% \par Nasdaq     2178.95   -36.75  -1.66% \par S&P 500    1075.63   -18.53  -1.69% \par FTSE 100   5211.29   -91.58  -1.73% \par Xetra DAX  6075.13  -111.18  -1.80% \par CAC40      3572.40   -75.53  -2.07% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9222.74  -139.94 -1.49% \par Hang Seng    20912.15 -160.31 -0.76% \par S&P/ASX 200   4411.70  -67.32 -1.50% \par Taiwan Index  7924.51   -4.43 -0.06% \par S.Korea Kospi 1769.17  -10.47 -0.59% \par Shanghai Comp 2653.52  -34.46 -1.28% \par Bombay SenSex 18413.10 -41.84 -0.23% \par Dow Future    10217.50 -23.50  -0.2% \par NASDAQ Future 1817.88  -2.13   -0.1% \par S&P Future    1069.35  -1.95   -0.2% \par Above are as of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      85.40-44      +0.02% \par Range        85.53   85.20 \par EUR/USD      1.2811-15    -0.10% \par Range        1.2822  1.2793 \par AUD/USD    