\par\par \b \uc2 CORRECT: WORLD  \highlight22\uc2 FOREX \highlight\uc2 : Euro Sinks While Yen, Swiss Franc Benefit\b0\par\par\uc2 643 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2010\par 12:26\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 ("=WORLD \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 : Euro Sinks While Yen, Swiss Franc Benefit," at 0935 GMT, misspelled the French President's name in the 12th paragraph. The correct version follows:)\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings\par \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES\par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--A new wave of anti-risk sentiment has pushed the euro lower and helped the yen and the Swiss franc higher Friday.\par \par \uc2 However, major \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  pairs continue to trade in fairly narrow ranges with investors showing little appetite for taking large positions.\par \par \uc2 The dollar itself was mixed after Thursday's news that U.S. jobless claims had risen much more than expected last week and that the Philadelphia Fed index fell sharply this month instead of rising as forecast.\par \par \uc2 The fear of a double dip recession in the U.S. sent investors heading for safe haven assets, with most global equity markets tumbling and U.S. Treasurys staging a rally.\par \par \uc2 In Europe, most stock markets were about 0.5% lower and the price of the 10-year German government bond futures contract climbed to a record high.\par \par \uc2 The dollar initially fell Thursday as the disappointing U.S. data was released. However, the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  then rallied as fears over the global recovery took hold and the dollar itself looked a safer bet compared to high yielders.\par \par \uc2 See how the euro rose and fell against the dollar:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4461"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4461}}}\par \par \uc2 The yen and the Swiss franc were the other major beneficiaries, with both being helped by falling fears of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Despite speculation earlier this week that the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would ease monetary policy further in an effort to weaken the yen, Japanese Economics Minister Satoshi Arai said that the government has no specific plans for the Bank of Japan just now even though the yen does pose a risk to the economy.\par \par \uc2 Fears over some early monetary move also relaxed after the country's Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda appeared to play down reports that the Prime Minister Naoto Kan is meeting with Bank of Japan Governor Masaaki Shirakawa on Monday.\par \par \uc2 The Swiss franc was also seen less likely to be the victim of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  than it was before.\par \par \uc2 Although the \uc2 Swiss National Bank\uc2  has tried to prevent the euro from falling under CHF1.30 in the past, analysts said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  appears to be more concerned about recent balance sheet losses and more likely to let the franc continue rising for now.\par \par \uc2 The euro, meanwhile, was coming under additional pressure from news that French President Nicolas Sarkozy is meeting senior ministers today to discuss growth prospects and the 2011 budget.\par \par \uc2 Although credit rating agencies have affirmed the country's triple-A rating, there are fears that growth forecasts could be lowered sharply for next year. Economists at Credit Agricole reckon they could be lowered as far as 1.0-1.5% from 2.5%.\par \par \uc2 By midmorning, the euro had slipped back to $1.2762 from $1.2820 late on Thursday in New York, according to EBS.\par \par \uc2 The euro was also down at Y108.99 from Y109.40 while the dollar was up a little at Y85.39 from Y85.29.\par \par \uc2 After an initial fall, the dollar did bounce back to CHF1.0335 from CHF1.0325 while the pound fell to $1.5538 from $1.5606.\par \par \uc2 By Nicholas Hastings, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9493;\par \par \uc2 nick.hastings@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments. [ 20-08-10 1026GMT ]\par \par \uc2 1035 28038 4163 44462   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100820e68k000oh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
