\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks Fall As Data Disappoint; GE, Intel Lead Declines\b0\par\par\uc2 2,547 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 20 August 2010\par 00:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10271.21  -144.33  -1.39% \par Nasdaq     2178.95   -36.75  -1.66% \par S&P 500    1075.63   -18.53  -1.69% \par FTSE 100   5211.29   -91.58  -1.73% \par Xetra DAX  6075.13  -111.18  -1.80% \par  \par 2Y Tsy   100 9/32  +1/32 0.484% -1.2BP \par 5Y Tsy  101 22/32  +6/32 1.394% -4.2BP \par 10Y Tsy 100 14/32 +18/32 2.573% -6.5BP \par  \par USD/JPY       85.39-41     -0.05% \par Range         85.91-84.90 \par EUR/USD       1.2825-26    -0.16% \par Range         1.2902-1.2773 \par AUD/USD       0.8926-30    -0.59% \par Range         0.9018-0.8906 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   10.80/11.10% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   11.05/11.35% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   12.85/13.15% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,235.40  +$4.00 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,229.50-$1,239.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $74.43   -$0.99 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intel and \f28 \uc2 \f2 General Electric\f28 \uc2 \f2  both weighed heavily on the Dow Jones Industrial Average Thursday as U.S. stocks skidded broadly.\f28 \par \par \uc2 The Dow fell 144.33, or 1.39%, to 10271.21, its lowest close since July 21. Intel was the measure's worst performer, after it agreed to pay a hefty premium for computer-security software company McAfee. The chip giant fell 69 cents, or 3.5%, to 18.90. McAfee soared 17.08, or 57%, to 47.01.\par \par \uc2 Some investors are worried that Intel is paying too steep a price for McAfee, whose shares it will buy for $48 each, a 60% premium to its close Wednesday. Still, the lavish premium helped boost other stocks in the computer-security software field. McAfee rival \uc2 Symantec\uc2  jumped 78 cents, or 6.2%, to 13.37.\par \par \uc2 Economically sensitive \uc2 General Electric\uc2  also weighed on the Dow. The company dropped 45 cents, or 2.9%, to 15.25, after jobless claims unexpectedly jumped to their highest level since November 2009 and a survey of business conditions in the Philadelphia region dropped into negative territory.\par \par \uc2 "Everybody's worried we're tiptoeing or teetering closer to the edge of recession than we'd like," said Bernie Williams, vice president of private investment management at \uc2 USAA\uc2 . The slide in the Philadelphia Fed story spurred worries that the broader and closely watched Institute for Supply Management's manufacturing indexes could cease showing expansion, he said.\par \par \uc2 The Nasdaq Composite fell 36.75, or 1.66%, to 2178.95, its first drop in four sessions. The \uc2 Standard & Poor's\uc2  500-share index slid 18.53, or 1.69%, to 1075.63, with all of its sectors closing in the red.\par \par \uc2 Leading the measure's broad decline, financials and materials slid as the economic data darkened prospects for a robust recovery. Aluminum producer \uc2 Alcoa\uc2  fell 27 cents, or 2.5%, to 10.66. Manufacturing giant 3M shed 1.80, or 2.2%, to 81.81. Among financials, \uc2 Bank of America\uc2  dropped 30 cents, or 2.3%, to 13.02, while \uc2 J.P. Morgan Chase\uc2  declined 82 cents, or 2.2%, to 37.07.\par \par \uc2 And in an ominous sign for the broad market, the Dow Jones 20 Transportation Average, seen as an economic barometer, sank 103.18, or 2.39%, to 4216.87l. Shipping giant \uc2 FedEx\uc2  dropped 2.46, or 2.9%, to 81.58, while railroad company Union Pacific fell 1.50, or 2%, to 74.50.\par \par \uc2 China's shares closed at a more than three-month high Thursday, buoyed by expectations of strong first-half earnings by banks, although profit-taking in property developers capped the gains.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely end this week around the psychologically important 2700 level. The index has jumped 13% since the start of July, as decelerating economic growth helped ease concerns about the possibility of further tightening measures.\par \par \uc2 "The traditional industries have lagged the market and are now catching up," said Hao Guomei, an analyst at Huatai Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks rose Thursday as signs of strong demand for chip-making equipment fueled a sharp rise in \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2 's shares, while heightened speculation for \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  monetary easing steps drove up real estate and consumer lender stocks.\par \par \uc2 Real estate and consumer lender shares charged ahead after the Sankei Shimbun reported that the Bank of Japan may consider expanding its lending facility, rumors about which have been rife in the market all week.\par \par \uc2 Speculation was also strong that the BOJ may hold an emergency gathering before a reported meeting between Prime Minister Naoto Kan and BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa Monday.\par \par \uc2 "The scary prospect is that investors may prod authorities to act further by pushing share prices down in the case only half-baked measures come out," said Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities.\par \par \uc2 With investors increasingly pricing in the possibility of a BOJ easing step, share gains may last only until the prospective formal announcement is actually out, Fumiyuki Nakanishi, general manager at SMBC Friend Securities, cautioned.\par \par \uc2 "Monetary easing may briefly calm market concerns about the yen's rise, but this will not be a remedy for fundamental economic conditions," Nakanishi said, putting near-term Nikkei resistance around 9448, its present 25-day moving average.\par \par \uc2 Gold futures hit a seven-week high Thursday in U.S. trading after a flurry of disappointing economic data rejuvenated investors' recent flight to safety.\par \par \uc2 Crude oil futures prices settled at a six-week low Thursday, stung by the rash of disappointing U.S. data and the highest inventories in nearly 27 years.\par \par \uc2 Base metals generally closed lower on the London Metal Exchange Thursday after the U.S. data releases weighed on market sentiment.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar gained against the euro and most other higher-yielding \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  Thursday after deepening fears about the country's slow pace of recovery sent investors to traditional safe harbors.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Disappointing U.S. employment and manufacturing data weighed on U.S. equity markets and led to losses for \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closely tied to global growth, such as the Australian, New Zealand and Canadian dollars.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "In general, there's a lack of conviction" in \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets, as poor U.S. data has investors wondering whether further deterioration in the U.S. economy is ahead, said Alvise Marino, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are unwilling to pile into positions, until they see where the economy is going," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Low summer trading volumes may have exacerbated intraday \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  movements, analysts said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday in New York, the euro was at $1.2820 from $1.2860 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y85.29 from Y85.43 after falling as low as Y84.89. The euro was at Y109.40 from Y109.87. The U.K. pound was at $1.5606 from $1.5605. The dollar was at CHF1.0325 from CHF1.0420.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , was at 82.437 from 82.259.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Philadelphia Fed index fell to -7.7 in August compared with 5.1 in July, and economists' expectations of a 7.0 reading.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. initial unemployment claims also disappointed, rising by 12,000 to 500,000 in the week ended Aug. 14. It was the highest level since Nov. 14, and further stoked fears about the country's already weak labor market.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The rise in claims was particularly troubling because, with no seasonal factors affecting the week's results, economists had expected to see a decline. In previous weeks, analysts said data were likely distorted by seasonal adjustment factors tied to factory shutdowns by car makers and the hiring and firing of temporary workers for the 2010 Census.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Canadian, Australian and New Zealand dollars all fell following the release of the data. Regional factors such as weak wholesale sales in Canada, rising input prices in New Zealand and the news that Fitch Ratings and Standard and Poor's placed mining giant BHP Billiton's A+ rating on watch also weighed on the commodity-backed dollar bloc.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback's losses against the yen after the U.S. data were likely limited by talk of possible \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  by \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to stop the appreciation of the yen that is causing alarm among Japanese exporters, said Hidetoshi Yanagihara, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Mizuho Corporate Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Everybody will think about the possibility of the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," he said, noting there has been no clear signal from Japanese authorities on future action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Separately, the Malaysian ringgit surged to a near 13-year high and may scale new heights in the next few months after the country's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  eased \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  rules further by allowing companies to use the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to settle international trade, dealers said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Adding fresh impetus to the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's move, China started trading the Malaysian ringgit against the yuan on its domestic interbank \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market Thursday, as part of its efforts to internationalize the Chinese \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and boost cross-border trade.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys rallied Thursday, led by the 30-year bond, as investors sought safe assets after the latest U.S. data in manufacturing and employment heightened fears of an economic slowdown.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 At the peak of the buying, the two-year note's yield hit a record low of 0.472% and the benchmark 10-year note's yield touched 2.555%, the lowest level since March 2009. The 30-year bond's yield hit 3.619%, the weakest level since April 2009.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1952 GMT, the benchmark 10-year Treasury note was 18/32 higher, and its yield was down 6.5 basis points to 2.573%. The 30-year bond was 1 17/32 higher and its yield fell 8.4 basis points to 3.653%. The two-year note was up 1/32 to yield 0.484%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The main catalyst was the Philadelphia Fed August business index which unexpectedly dropped to a negative 7.7 this month from a positive 5.1 in July. That raised concern the manufacturing sector, the best-performing sector of the economy, may lose traction.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A job market report added to the anxiety. The initial jobless claims for the week ended Aug. 14 rose to the highest level since November, pointing to a weak market still saddled with a near 10% unemployment rate. [ 19-08-10 2207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Federal Reserve has acknowledged the downside risks to the economic growth earlier this month and held key interest rates near zero. The \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  also started buying Treasurys this week in an attempt to push down long-dated interest rates and spur economic growth.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "The economic recovery is most certainly threatened," said Dan Greenhaus, chief economic and bond strategist at Miller Tabak & Co in New York. "While a full-blown second recession is not the base case scenario, the probability of it occurring is probably a bit higher than many have been estimating."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Long-dated Treasurys outperformed short-dated peers as they provide higher yields at a time when bond yields have fallen across the board. A slowing economy also reduces the risks of inflation, a main threat to bonds' fixed return over time.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Treasurys continue their rally on expectations that yields certainly can move lower with little in the way of economic or inflation data to discourage them from doing so," said Christopher Sullivan, who oversees $1.6 billion as chief investment officer at United Nations Federal Credit Union in New York.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended flat Thursday after the PBOC left the yield on its three-month bills unchanged in its regular open market \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 operation\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , fueling the view that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  is satisfied with current market rates, a Shenzhen-based analyst with a local insurer said. The PBOC is likely to keep its bill yields stable at current levels in coming weeks, the trader added.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Shanghai exchange government bond index is likely to hover around 126.6 Friday.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bond yields marked fresh multi-year lows Thursday, as investors picked up the safe haven assets on the view that further monetary easing steps by the Bank of Japan would be unlikely to stem the yen's rise.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Sankei Shimbun reported Thursday without citing sources that the BOJ will consider expanding its 0.1% fixed-rate lending facility by either increasing the amount to Y30 trillion from Y20 trillion, or lengthening the duration to six months from three months.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Such steps could push JGB yields down further, widening the interest rate gap between Japan and the U.S., but analysts said the measures wouldn't be bold enough arrest the yen's recent surge.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Investors are buying JGBs because they think expanding the fund-provision steps can't stop the yen's rise. If the dollar falls below Y85.00 again, we should be ready for JGB yields to mark fresh multi-year lows down the road," said Makoto Noji, a senior market analyst at Mizuho Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In addition, some investors speculate that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may cut its key policy rate from the current 0.10%, said \f28 \uc2 \f2 Credit Suisse\f28 \uc2 \f2 's strategist Kenro Kawano.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If the BOJ eases its policy by expanding fund provision programs, then short-term JGB yields will likely fall further, making it difficult for the BOJ to keep overnight call rate around 0.10%, he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among data due Friday, consumer prices in Hong Kong likely rose faster in July than in June as rental, food and transportation costs continued to increase, while a prolonged low-interest-rate environment also added to inflationary pressure.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The city's consumer price index likely rose 3.0% in July from a year earlier, faster than June's 2.8% increase and May's 2.5% rise, according to the median forecast of eight economists.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Jobless Claims Jump To 500k In Week \par Intel To Buy McAfee For $48 A Share, Or $7.68B \par Dell Profit Rises 15%; PC Sales Up \par Fed Discount Window Borrowings Total $61.92B \par Bullard Sees Core Inflation Lower But 'Manageable' \par Transocean Says BP Withholding Data On Oil Spill \par BP To Replace Blow-out Preventer On Gulf Well \par Mastercard To Acquire Datacash For \u163\'00\'A3333M \par Japan To Lift Freeze On New Public Works Projects In FY11 -Nikkei \par =DJ Japanese Government Mulls More Stimulus Amid Yen Strength \par =Caterpillar CEO Aims For Market Leadership In China \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  Jul International Travel and Migration \par 0200  Sri Lanka    Aug \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  of Sri Lanka Monetary Policy Review announcement \par 0300  New Zealand  Jul Credit card statistics \par 0500  Japan        Jul Steel Production \par 0730  Thailand     Weekly International Reserves \par 0800  Taiwan       Jul Export Orders \par 0820  Taiwan       2Q  Balance of Payments \par 0830  Hong Kong    Jul CPI \par 0900  Malaysia     Aug International Reserves, middle of month \par 1130  India        Weekly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves \par 1400  US           Jul Mass Layoffs \par 1400  US           Jul Regional & State Employment & Unemployment \par N/A   India        Jul CPI AL/RL (Agricultural/Rural Labourers) \par N/A   India        Jun CPI UNME (Urban non-manual employees) \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 19-08-10 2207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument D