\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: JPY Impact Limited Even If BOJ Eases - Dealer\b0\par\par\uc2 1,606 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 19 August 2010\par 04:06\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0206 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/JPY may not rise much even if BOJ goes ahead and eases policy further because investors have already factored in such moves, says Mitsuru Sahara, senior dealer at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\f28 \uc2 \f2  UFJ. Notes even if BOJ eases further, JGB's already-low yields may not fall further, leaving interest rate gap between Japan, U.S. almost unchanged. Adds "I'm actually scared that the yen may extend its gains even after a BOJ action." Says if any BOJ easing fails to push down JPY, then "there would be no remaining measure but \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ." \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  daily Sankei Shimbun reports \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to consider steps to cope with rising JPY, falling stocks, including expanding 0.1% fixed-rate lending facility to Y30 trillion from current Y20 trillion. Pair last 85.61. (kosaku.narioka@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Look to sell BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) 5-year CDS at 112 bps, target 90 bps, stop at 120 bps say \uc2 Deutsche Bank\uc2  credit strategists. Says widening in BHP CDS to 112/116 bps early likely linked to hedging on loans (on back of Potash bid). "However, loan hedging activity will likely be around the short end of the CDS curve, years 2 or 3, matching the likely maturity of the loans. We think loan hedging demand on the 5-year point will be thin. We continue to see the 5-year CDS fair at 85 bps on fundamentals." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0203 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares open up, led by banks, auto makers. Shanghai Composite Index +0.5% at 2680.36, tipped to trade in 2650-2700 range for now. "Investors had been worried about the risk of bad loans for banks, but because the policy environment has remained stable and both \uc2 Everbright Bank\uc2  and Agricultural Bank are holding steady, confidence is returning," says Li Bin at Capital Securities. China Everbright (601818.SH) +0.6% at CNY3.68, AgBank (601288.SH) flat at CNY2.69, \uc2 China Merchants Bank\uc2  (600036.SH) +2.0% at CNY14.15 after reporting slightly better-than-expected 1H results. Automakers extend gain on expectations that government will spend multi billion dollars to help develop electric cars: SAIC Motor (600104.SH) +3.0% at CNY16.60, FAW (000800.SZ) +0.9% at CNY17.33. Shenzhen index +0.3% at 1141.84. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] Lead Kospi200 September futures +1.2% at 231.60, tracking firm lead from Kospi (+0.8%). \uc2 Tong Yang Securities\uc2  analyst Yoon Sun-il says strong foreign buying has allowed index to overcome initial profit-taking pressure in early session, likely to maintain upward momentum for the day; "The Nikkei's rise is supportive, and U.S. futures are holding up well. We're seeing a quick recovery in investors' sentiment." Tips resistance at 232. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0157 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to extend losses with investors set to continue to sell on concerns for weak inflows after \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's dong devaluation yesterday. "I don't see any positive factors for the stock market after such a move, and many other investors are also waiting for more clear cues on how the government will act to boost liquidity for banks before we can come back to buy shares," says Hanoi-based trader. Tips key index in 448-453 range after ending down 1.7% at 455.49 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0152 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares choppy, last flat at 7925.46 vs down 0.5% earlier, as gains in Asian shares mostly offset by profit-taking selling after Taiex fails to test key 8000 resistance in recent sessions, says Mega Securities assistant VP Alex Huang. "The market is likely to be quiet today on a lack of fresh leads," he says. Tips Taiex in tight 7880-7960 band for day. Chimei (3481.TW) +1.5% at NT$37.25. \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) +0.9% at NT$29.10. Shin Kong Financial (2888.TW) down 5.5% at NT$11.10 after company says yesterday it swung to 2Q net loss of NT$72 million on-year. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0149 GMT [Dow Jones] August HSI futures +0.5% at 21,072, suggests cash market may open modestly higher. Still, contract ended yesterday at 59-point discount to cash, consistent with Tuesday close at 42-point discount to cash, wider than August fair-value discount at below 20 points; signals negative view in market's near-term direction, amid HK market's consolidation, with HSI trapped in less than 400-point range over last 5 sessions. August open interest little changed at 92,244 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0147 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares likely to extend gains as capital inflows, local institutions' buying expected to bolster market, says KT Zmico Securities First Vice President Charoen Iamphattanatham. Large-cap stocks likely to dominate market activities today. "Capital is still flowing to the Thai stock market as seen from foreigners' net buying and the stronger baht, so I think the positive momentum will continue," he says. Foreigners net buyers of Thai shares of THB1.23 billion yesterday. Resistance tipped at 900-mark, support at 867. SET index ends +1.6% at 880.02 yesterday. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0144 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR drops to a fresh 13-year low after \uc2 Bank Negara Malaysia\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  liberalization measures, but decline should be short-lived due to the global growth slowdown and MYR's traditional poor performance in 3Q, Standard Chartered says. Reckons effect of new measures allowing wider use of MYR for international trade settlements is more of a medium-term positive for MYR, "as it should, over time, broaden the offshore use of the MYR for trade settlement." Adds, move combined with additional MYR positives including a strong 2Q GDP reading and BNM comments that \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength is not a problem right now; "from late 3Q, USD/MYR should break lower on improving seasonality and CNY appreciation." StanChart now reviewing forecasts, previously had USD/MYR at 3.15 at the end-2010. Pair last at 3.1275. (david.roman@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0142 GMT [Dow Jones] (An item at 0051 GMT misstated the year of \uc2 Downer EDI\uc2 's earnings report. The correct version is as follows:) S&P/ASX 200 down 2 points at 4472.9 after quiet trading on Wall Street, with market heavyweight BHP (BHP.AU) continuing to restrain the market due to uncertainty about its bid for Potash Corp. (POT). BHP down 1% in line with ADRs, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) down 0.6%, despite some analysts recommending a switch to Rio. Focus otherwise on earnings reports, with AMP (AMP.AU) down 3.0% after missing 1H earnings expectations, Brambles (BXB.AU) up 3.6% after beating FY earnings consensus, Downer (DOW.AU) down 2.7% on difficult 2H earnings, though FY10 earnings beat consensus. QBE (QBE.AU) up 2.7% because its shares were sold down before today's 1H results, which were mostly pre-released last month. "I think we're in a holding pattern, as investors try to work out whether we should be concerned about a (U.S.) double-dip recession or whether this is a buying opportunity, amid a shallow recovery," says BBY senior institutional trader Peter Copeland. "Bottom-up focus is currently taking center stage in Australia, but we have a wary eye on the macro picture. The U.S. economic outlook in particular is still fairly clouded." (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0140 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan 20-year government bond extends recent sharp rally, with yield testing fresh record low 1.6600% earlier (last at 1.6650% vs 1.6668% yesterday) on gains in long-end U.S. Treasurys, sustained demand from insurers, Taiex's early 0.5% loss, says local bank trader. Still, expects 20-year yield to retrace some losses near term; "the 20-year yield is too low and contains no investment value (for brokerage houses)," he says. 5-year bond lower on increased expectations \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to quicken tightening after local report Thursday says consumer goods prices likely higher near term. 5-year yield last at 0.8943% vs 0.8890% yesterday, eyed in 0.8800%-0.9000% band for day. 20-year yield eyed in 0.6500%-0.6700% band. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.1% at fresh 30-month high 1387.54 on broad-based gains, with blue chips leading advances, says dealer with local brokerage. "The better than expected second quarter GDP, the stronger ringgit (USD/MYR last 3.1280 ) are all helping to sustain interest in the local bourse," says dealer. But TA Research says since index has risen sharply in past 4 days to overbought levels with 14-day RSI reading in mid 70s, a profit-taking correction more likely; "expect selling interest to increase closer to the immediate resistance of 1392, (the 24 July 2007 pivot high) with next tougher hurdle at 1410." Among gainers, \uc2 KL Kepong\uc2  (2445.KU) +0.6% at MYR17.00 after reporting stronger 3Q earnings, CIMB (1023.KU) +0.9% and MYR7.57 on expectation of better 2Q earnings, AirAsia (5099.KU) +1.8% at MYR1.71 on robust 2Q earnings. (elffie.chew@dowjones.com) [ 08-18-10 2206ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100819e68j0001p\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
