\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:  \highlight22\uc2 Japan FX \highlight\uc2  Acct Loss Not  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Obstacle-BarCap\b0\par\par\uc2 1,725 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2010\par 06:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0421 GMT [Dow Jones] Large losses in Japanese government's special \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  account not major obstacle to any \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , says Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 . Account marks record Y31 trillion valuation loss due to surging JPY, according to Nikkei report. But Yamamoto says losses don't change house's view probability of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  has increased with JPY's recent persistent strength; says government could be prompted to conduct JPY-selling campaign "by any deterioration in the outlook for the economy and equity market on further JPY strength." Says record loss in special account wouldn't preclude possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in such circumstances as "the objective the Special \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  Account is to smooth exchange rate moves, and not primarily to seek higher returns." Moreover, says according to law governing special account, unrealized valuation losses don't have to be realized at end of each fiscal year, can be rolled over, diminishing impact.(andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0420 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY stays down in quiet trade on lower-than-expected dollar-yuan central parity (6.7895 vs 6.7979 yesterday); in OTC trade, USD/CNY at 6.7887 vs 6.7921 last close. "It seems the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  wants to widen the trading range through the fixing setting," says Shanghai-based trader at European bank. "Everyone wants to know how the PBOC came up with the fixing level," trader says. "But no one knows what is in the PBOC's basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  that it references to. I personally don't think the fixing level has much to do with any \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  basket." USD/CNY tipped 6.7850-6.7900 for session. Offshore 1-year USD/CNY NDF at 6.6770/6.6800 vs 6.6730/6.6770 late yesterday. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0414 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/TWD lower at 31.930 midday vs 31.970 yesterday, off early low of 31.750 as Taiex turns lower (down 0.1% vs +0.7% earlier), though pair still weighed by exporters' selling around 31.950, says local bank trader. Tips pair in 31.900-31.950 band in afternoon. "The downward bias for the pair remains intact," he says. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0413 GMT [Dow Jones] China shares down midday, weighed by profit-taking after recent gains. Shanghai Composite Index down 0.3% at 2663.9 after ending up 0.4% Tuesday; tipped to hover around 2660 rest of session. "The market has rallied quite a bit in the run up to \uc2 Everbright Bank\uc2 's debut; now it's consolidating before checking its direction," says Wang Junqing, analyst at Guosen Securities. Notes, favorable policy conditions for auto makers, firms in Northeast may provide some lift to index. SAIC Motor (600104.SH) +4.3% to CNY16.15 on Beijing's push to encourage energy-efficient car buying with rebates, strong sales outlook. \uc2 Heilongjiang Transportation Development\uc2  (601188.SH) +7.3% at CNY4.56, and Jilin Expressway (601518.SH) +6.8% at CNY4.56 after Premier Wen Jiabao said Tuesday China will focus on developing Northeast region. \uc2 China Everbright Bank\uc2  (601818.SH) +16.8% at CNY3.62 vs CNY3.10 IPO price. \uc2 Bank of Beijing\uc2  (601169.SH) down 0.4% at CNY13.35 after gaining 2.2% in previous two sessions. Shenzhen Composite Index down 0.4% at 1133.93. (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0405 GMT [Dow Jones] India government bonds lower as traders cut positions ahead of Friday's INR120 billion debt sale but buying interest may emerge at high yield levels, trader with state-run bank says. Benchmark 7.80% 2020 bond at 99.22 after rising to 99.49 in session, 99.43 last close. "The market has been selling off for the past two sessions so you may see some buying interest in the benchmark 10-year paper and the new 12-year paper at higher yield levels. You may see good bidding interest above 7.90% yield (7.91% last) on the benchmark bond while the 8.13% 2022 bond may attract investors around 8.03% (8.00% last)," trader says. Tips benchmark 2020 bond in 99.25-99.65 band in session. (khushita.vasant@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0359 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/PHP moving higher, rebounding from early weakness, as USD regains strength vs EUR, some other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Pair last at 45.24 in moderate volume, up from 45.17 late Tuesday, early low at 45.10 today; support 45.00, resistance 45.40. "The dollar is correcting, spurred on by gains against the euro and other regional \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," says local bank trader. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0356 GMT [Dow Jones] JPY as strong vs USD as it has been in past 15 years, spurring markets to watch for signs Tokyo might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to relieve pressure on already struggling export-oriented economy. But on \b \highlight22\uc2 forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  action, where you stand depends on where you sit. When factoring Japan's trading partners and changing prices at home, JPY sits nearly 28% below 15-year highs; real-effective exchange rate index +3.6% in 7 months through July. Against USD, meanwhile, JPY +7.5% in nominal terms. Indicates economic shock not as severe as USD/JPY rate implies. That's a good part of argument against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . But theory does little for already suffering Japanese exporters. At \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2  every time USD weakens by single yen, $350 million wiped off operating profit. More broadly, Japan's recovery already threatened by slowing export rebound, weakening domestic demand. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves making Japanese products more expensive overseas is last thing economy needs. See HEARD ON THE STREET: The No and Da Of Yen Action (james.simms@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0355 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Bank of Thailand\uc2  Governor Tarisa says recent THB appreciation vs USD result of external factors, continuing robust exports, but adds current level remains in line with regional peers; says THB not too strong, recent strength has not affected Thailand's competitiveness. Adds, \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  "has taken care of the THB when necessary." THB also being boosted by capital inflows, most of which were heading for local bond market, Tarisa said. Notes that inflows to bond market were around THB20 billion in both June, July, close to THB50 billion year-to-date. USD/THB recently steady at 31.72. Comments come ahead of release of 2Q GDP data next Monday, which some economists say could show growth of up to 8.0% on-year. (piyarat.setthasiriphaiboon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0350 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex opens higher, now +0.3% at 18,102.28, helped by most Asian markets trading modestly higher, strong close of U.S. stocks yesterday, market watchers say. Angel Securities says, "The trend deciding level for the day is 18,072/5422 levels. If Nifty trades above this level during the first half-an-hour of trade, then we may witness a further rally up to 18,117-18,186/5436-5457 levels." Alex Mathews, head of research, \uc2 Geojit BNP Paribas Financial Services\uc2 , says index still trading in tight range, managing to sustain about 18,000. "But the volumes have been really weak lately, which suggests traders are cautious and not willing to be on the benchmark right now." Believes India equities likely to continue trading sideways in absence of clear triggers. \uc2 Tata Motors\uc2  (500570.BY) +1.0% at INR1,015.55, \uc2 HDFC Bank\uc2  (500180.BY) +0.9% at INR2,142, \uc2 Bharti Airtel\uc2  (532454.BY) +0.6% at INR317.85, \uc2 Infosys Technologies\uc2  (500209.BY) +0.6% at INR2,768.20. S&P CNX Nifty +0.3% at 5432.25. (amitha.rajan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0338 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai government bonds mixed in moderate trade, with auction result largely in line with expectations, although yield a touch higher than some tipped. "There's been a bit of activity, with some buying flows coming from foreign banks. The 4 and 5 year tenors are still the most active," says Bangkok-based dealer. Government sold THB11 billion worth of bonds due December 2024 with weighted average accepted yield of 3.6105%, bid coverage ratio of 1.1X; THB5 billion worth of bonds due June 2040 with weighted average accepted yield of 3.9658%, bid coverage ratio of 2.31X. Bid/offer yields of issue due December 2015 at 2.92%/2.90% vs 2.93%/2.90% late yesterday, November 2016 at 3.08%/3.04% vs 3.05%/3.02%, June 2019 at 3.21%/3.18% vs 3.19%/3.17%. (leigh.murray@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0336 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 JP Morgan\uc2  receiving Asian IRS across curves, despite rates at historical lows, vs earlier bull-flattener trades; now says shorter-dates rates also to fall as Asian central banks likely to pause tightening in 2H with Fed signaling it may stay on hold for longer. "With inflation peaking during 3Q, Asian central banks will not be under pressure to tighten much further. We believe that the remainder of the year will be about predicting the pause." House recommends receiving 2-year TWD IRS 2 years forward at 1.53%, receiving THB 2-5-10-year butterfly spread at +30 bps, receiving KRW 2-4-10-year butterfly spread at minus 6 bps, receiving 5-year SGD IRS 5 years forward at 3.37%. (aries.poon@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0332 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares last down 2.0% at 454.05, vs 453.99 earlier, with investors selling on concerns that \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's devaluation of VND vs USD by 2.1% effective Wednesday will hurt many companies as move will raise cost of paying back USD-denominated debts. "Adding to the current concerns of a possible equity glut and high bank interest rates, today's devaluation is hurting sentiment further," says Hanoi-based trader. Volume thin with 25 million shares valued at VND640 billion traded. Among decliners, Pha Lai Thermal Power (PPC.VH) down 4.5% at VND12,900, Masan (MSN.VH) down 3.5% at VND49,700, Ocean Group (OGC.VH) down 3.6% at VND35,300. Trader tips immediate support at 450. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 08-18-10 0021ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100818e68i00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
