\par\par \b \uc2 ANALYSIS-Barriers against  \highlight22\uc2 intervention \highlight\uc2  rise with yen\b0\par\par\uc2 By Steven C. Johnson  \par \uc2 835 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 18 August 2010\par 22:34\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited  \par \par \uc2 NEW YORK, Aug 18 (Reuters) - In decades past, Japanese authorities would have wasted little time printing more yen to counter the sort of gains their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  has been racking up.\par \par \uc2 Yet even with the yen at a 15-year peak of 85 per dollar and near its record high around 80, official \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to weaken it seems less likely than ever before.\par \par \uc2 U.S. and euro zone central banks, whose financial firepower would be needed in any push to bring down the yen, appear content to let their weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  revive their own exports and flagging economies.\par \par \uc2 "Most people agree that for \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to be successful, it has to be coordinated, and that quite simply is not going to happen," said Tom Levinson, a \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at ING Commercial Banking in London. "The U.S. and others just aren't going to sign up for it."\par \par \uc2 The main reason is economic. Growth in Europe and America, while stronger than Japan's, is still sluggish, and strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  would undermine exports and push up interest rates.\par \par \uc2 A euro zone official told Reuters last week that European authorities would not welcome Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  on the yen and cast doubt on any coordinated push. [ID:nTST002429]\par \par \uc2 "If you look at the majority of the developed world, deflation is a concern, so nobody wants their \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  to strengthen," said Takuji Okubo, chief economist for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In addition, allowing Tokyo to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since 2004 could hinder efforts by the Group of 20 economies, including Japan, to convince China to let its yuan appreciate.\par \par \uc2 What's more, recent Chinese interest in Japanese government debt would dilute the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , as would \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's own pension funds, which are repatriating foreign investments as U.S. yields fall to record lows.\par \par \uc2 Japan's fragile government would essentially be locking horns with a $3 trillion-a-day \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market determined to buy the yen.\par \par \uc2 NOT AS STRONG AS IT SEEMS\par \par \uc2 Historically, U.S. and euro zone officials have been more hostile to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  manipulation than their counterparts in Japan, although they did help Japanese authorities weaken the yen in the mid 1990s, when anxiety stemming from the Mexican peso crisis drove it to an all-time high of 79.75 per dollar.\par \par \uc2 Japan's exports compete with those of China and South Korea, which \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to dampen \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strength. Slower exports would hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's weak growth and stoke deflation fears.\par \par \uc2 Yen strength also hurts corporate profits, pushing down share prices and consumer spending. Data this week showed the Japanese economy barely grew at all in the second quarter. [ID:nTOE67901S]\par \par \uc2 So far, government officials have tried to talk the yen lower. Japan's prime minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor are due to meet on Monday to discuss the situation. [ID:nTOE67F033]\par \par \uc2 Sympathy from abroad will be in short supply. "The U.S. and Europe have so many other things to worry about right now. The message to Japan on yen strength will be: 'deal with it,'" said \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  strategist Win Thin. "The Europeans have had to deal with a strong euro since 2005."\par \par \uc2 Some analysts say the yen's rise may be a lot less painful for Japanese firms than the government fears.\par \par \uc2 While the nominal dollar-yen exchange rate has depreciated some 25 percent since 2008, the real effective exchange rate remains well below the peak seen in the mid-90s.\par \par \uc2 That's because in Japan, falling prices have squeezed wage costs and helped exporters maintain market share.\par \par \uc2 Increased overseas production for Panasonic, Toyota and others also shields firms from \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk. [ID:nTOE67304R]\par \par \uc2 "The U.S., the Europeans, even the IMF would argue the yen is not inconsistent at current levels, given Japan's current account surplus, and that Japan should welcome it and become less reliant on exports to drive growth," said Chris Scicluna, deputy head of economic research at Daiwa Capital Markets.\par \par \uc2 NO GUARANTEE OF SUCCESS\par \par \uc2 Japan may act alone if the yen plunges to 80 per dollar or the Nikkei stock average breaks below 9,000, Scicluna said. The Nikkei on Wednesday closed at 9,240.54.\par \par \uc2 In that case, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  would likely be paired with more monetary easing, possibly via extending funding \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  announced at an emergency December meeting.\par \par \uc2 And still, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may not work, said \uc2 UBS\uc2  strategist Geoffrey Yu. Switzerland has spent the past 18 months selling francs against the euro, and while deflation risks have receded, Yu said that has more to do with productivity gains that have helped Swiss companies retain export competitiveness.\par \par \uc2 "The SNB is now sitting on \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  losses and a far stronger franc in real terms," Yu said. (Additional reporting by Stanley White in Tokyo and Krista Hughes in Frankfurt; Editing by Leslie Adler)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /YENINTERVENTION|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J|UKI|Z|CANT|AUF  \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100818e68i0015w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
