\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ BOJ WATCH: Not Ready To Ease, But Yen, Politicians Could Force A Move\b0\par\par\uc2 By Megumi Fujikawa and Tomoyuki Tachikawa  \par \uc2 A Dow Jones Newswires Column  \par \uc2 1,063 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2010\par 11:56\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's central bankers don't think the yen's rise poses an immediate threat to the economy but they are ready to consider additional monetary steps should the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  surge higher or political pressure to act increase, people familiar with the situation say.\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's climb to 15-year highs has alarmed Japan's government, which worries it could throttle a fragile recovery by reducing exporters' earnings and intensifying deflation. The government last week reiterated it will keep working closely with the Bank of Japan to fight the yen's strength, and BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa issued a rare statement expressing concern about volatility in the yen and Japanese share prices.\par \par \uc2 But some half a dozen people familiar with the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's thinking told Dow Jones Newswires the BOJ considers the yen's recent gains relatively mild compared to its sharp moves late last year. The threat to the overall economy--so far--appears limited, as the yen's rise hasn't been excessively fast and hasn't dealt a significant blow to business sentiment, they said.\par \par \uc2 "The current yen appreciation is different in terms of speed, substance and degree" from last year's surge, said one of the people. Still, "it would be regarded as risky if (the yen started to rise) rapidly and in an erratic manner."\par \par \uc2 Another person familiar with the situation said if political pressure on the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to curb the yen should increase, the BOJ might have to take some additional steps.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Naoto Kan is to meet Shirakawa Monday to discuss how to deal with the yen, local media say. The BOJ preempted a similar meeting between Shirakawa and the then-prime minister in December by announcing a three-month emergency loan program amid pressure to curb the yen.\par \par \uc2 The dollar, which traded at nearly Y95 in early May, fell Wednesday to Y84.72, the lowest since July 1995. Tuesday at 0951 GMT, the dollar was at Y85.30.\par \par \uc2 Importantly for the economy and policymakers, the yen's real, trade-weighted exchange rate is significantly weaker than when the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was at current levels against the dollar 15 years ago, given years of consumer price deflation in Japan and a change in the composition of its trading partners.\par \par \uc2 But such considerations haven't eased the government's angst.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials have steady ratcheted up their rhetoric against the yen's relentless ascent. Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda threatened Thursday to take "appropriate steps" in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market--code for yen-selling \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 . The ministry, which runs \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy, hasn't ordered the BOJ to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004.\par \par \uc2 Under current conditions, traders doubt the authorities will return to the market. For one thing, the U.S. Federal Reserve's super-loose monetary policy is driving the dollar's fall and would likely overpower any effort by Japan to weaken the yen. What's more, Tokyo would have a hard time justifying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  when it has expressed its displeasure with China's bid to depress the yuan.\par \par \uc2 With Japan's public debt fast approaching 200% of its gross domestic product, there's little scope for government spending to cushion the economic impact of the strong yen on Japanese industry. That leaves monetary policy as the key policy lever, and analysts say pressure on the BOJ to ease policy could increase.\par \par \uc2 If the yen keeps rising and the economy weakens, "the end of deflation could become further off and political pressure will likely intensify on the BOJ to do more," said Takeshi Minami, chief economist at Norinchukin Research Institute. The Kan administration is now aiming to eradicate deflation in the fiscal year starting next April.\par \par \uc2 Still, few BOJ watchers expect the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take anything other than token measures if it does act.\par \par \uc2 One possibility, they say, would be to expand December's emergency loans. The BOJ initially set up a Y10 trillion facility, doubling that in March. It offers three-month loans at 0.1% with government bonds and corporate debt as collateral.\par \par \uc2 "Although it may not have real effects on stimulating economic activity, it would likely help push down the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  by lowering longer term-fund rates" such as interbank offered rates, said Mari Iwashita, chief market economist at Nikko Cordial Securities. She expects the BOJ to add a further Y10 trillion to that scheme if either the yen surges more or political pressure intensifies.\par \par \uc2 Five of nine BOJ watchers in a Dow Jones survey last week forecast the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would loosen policy if the yen rises further, either by increasing the amount of cheap three-month loans or extending their duration to six months.\par \par \uc2 A more aggressive step would be for the BOJ to increase the amount of Japanese government bonds it buys outright from the current pace of Y1.8 trillion a month, said Susumu Kato, chief Japan economist at Credit Agricole.\par \par \uc2 He expects the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to increase the size of JGB buying \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  to Y2.6 trillion a month in October. Doing so would have a greater economic impact than expanding the loan facility as it would push down bond yields and significantly improve funding conditions for Japanese banks, which hold large amounts of JGBs. Bank lending might then rise and, some of the funds could flow into foreign-\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  assets, taking some pressure off the yen, Kato said.\par \par \uc2 Yet, BOJ officials have opposed the idea because of worries that it could increase market concerns about Japan's fiscal discipline and that the anti-deflation drug could prove too effective--causing prices to rise out of control.\par \par \uc2 -By Megumi Fujikawa and Tomoyuki Tachikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2786; megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com; tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verification purposes. We reserve the right to edit and publish your comments along with your name; we reserve the right not to publish reader comments.\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 17, 2010 05:56 ET (09:56 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100817e68h001nx\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
