\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: The No and Da Of Yen Action\b0\par\par\uc2 By James Simms   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES COLUMN   \par \uc2 570 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2010\par 13:25\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 The yen is as strong as it's been against the dollar in the past 15 years. That has \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders watching closely for signs that Tokyo might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to relieve pressure on an already struggling export-oriented economy.\par \par \uc2 But when it comes to \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  action by Japan, where you stand depends on where you sit.\par \par \uc2 Consider the big picture: when factoring Japan's trading partners and changing prices at home, the yen sits nearly 28% below its 15-year-old highs. China -- whose \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  grew substantially stronger between 2005 and 2008 -- has overtaken the U.S. to be come Japan's largest two-way trading partner. Japan has also suffered a decade of deflation.\par \par \uc2 This real-effective exchange rate index was up 3.6% in the 7 months through the end of July. Against the dollar, meanwhile, the yen is up 7.5% in nominal terms. It means the macroeconomic shock won't be as severe as the exchange rate against the dollar implies. That's a good part of the argument, so far, against \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's finance minister, Yoshihiko Noda, has practicality to consider as well. Geopolitics and the size of global \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets mean that Tokyo will find effective \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  difficult without support from the U.S. and Europe. With politicians in both hoping their own weak \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  will bolster exports, approval -- let alone assistance -- from those two economies remains unlikely.\par \par \uc2 When it last \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2 , six years ago, \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  sold 35 trillion yen in 15 months. A repeat would quickly deflate arguments put forth by Washington, Brussels and Tokyo for Beijing to allow the yuan to strengthen.\par \par \uc2 Standing on principle, of course, will do little for already suffering Japanese exporters -- and their tense employees and shareholders. At \uc2 Toyota Motor\uc2 , for example, every time the dollar weakens by a single yen, $350 million is wiped out of operating profit. More broadly, Japan's economic recovery is already threatened by a slowing rebound in exports and weakening domestic demand. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves that make Japanese products more expensive overseas is the last thing the economy needs.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo is currently addressing the issue with talk, rather than action. In the short run, this has kept yen bulls at bay. For example, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  weakened slightly Tuesday on reports that Prime Minister Naoto Kan and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  chief Masaaki Shirakawa are due to meet next week, as traders speculated that the Bank of Japan might yield to pressure to take steps to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 From where both the government and the BOJ sit, things are looking less comfortable by the day.\par \par \uc2 (James Simms, a writer on the Heard on the Street team, has been a correspondent in Japan for 14 years, including over ten years at Dow Jones covering economics, politics and regulatory issues. He can be reached at 81-(0)3-6269-2799 or by e-mail: james.simms@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkbackAsia@dowjones.com. 