\par\par \b  \highlight22\uc2 Forex \highlight\uc2  Focus: Why The Dollar Should Rise Some More\b0\par\par\uc2 By Nicholas Hastings   \par \uc2 A DOW JONES NEWSWIRES COLUMN   \par \uc2 889 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2010\par 09:15\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--Confidence in the dollar should be growing.\par \par \uc2 Over the last week, the risks of U.S. deflation have receded.\par \par \uc2 On the other hand, the outlook for the Japanese economy has deteriorated and hopes for an early end to the euro zone's debt crisis appear to have been too high.\par \par \uc2 In other words, the attraction of the U.S. \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  should continue to rise.\par \par \uc2 See how the euro has started to slide against the dollar this month:\par \par {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4433"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://www.dowjoneswebservices.com/chart/view/4433}}}\par \par \uc2 Let's look at the U.S. itself.\par \par \uc2 The economic recovery there remains fragile, as reflected in the Fed's decision to sustain market liquidity levels for now and remain on guard to extend quantitative easing if needed.\par \par \uc2 However, gentle improvements in consumer confidence, a rebound in retail sales and even the latest consumer price figures suggest that the recovery may not be stalling quite as badly as many feared.\par \par \uc2 In fact, inflation numbers last Friday suggested there has been little increase in deflation pressure over the last month and the need for more quantitative easing is that much less likely.\par \par \uc2 This slightly more robust view of the U.S. contrasts with developments in both Japan and the euro zone.\par \par \uc2 News on Monday that Japan had achieved annualized growth of only 0.4% in the second quarter came as a shock, given forecasts for growth of 2.3%.\par \par \uc2 This will only increase political pressure on the Bank of Japan to pour even more liquidity into financial markets, not only to help the economy by easing policy but by helping to cut off the yen's recent rise.\par \par \uc2 Japan has been suffering of late from the yen's safe haven role, that has pushed the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  higher and poses a threat to the country's export-led upturn.\par \par \uc2 Developments in the euro zone may have been different but are no less negative for the euro.\par \par \uc2 The cost of debt funding in peripheral euro-zone debtor countries has started to rise once again, making it more expensive for these countries to refinance their borrowing and increasing the risk of default.\par \par \uc2 This has combined with data showing that growth in Germany is soaring not only ahead of expectations but well ahead of most of its euro-zone neighbors.\par \par \uc2 For the euro, however, this of little consequence. Global investors are well aware that the risks for the single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  are all associated with the problems of the peripheral countries.\par \par \uc2 They will be more focused on the results of the latest bond auctions in Ireland and Portugal, later Tuesday and Wednesday, respectively, for any evidence that funding costs are still rising.\par \par \uc2 However, there is another reason why investors will steer clear of the euro. The diverging growth patterns between core euro-zone members, such as Germany, and the rest of the region, suggest the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  will face an even greater difficulty setting monetary policy at an appropriate level for all members.\par \par \uc2 So while interest rates are likely to remain low in most countries for the time being, those in the U.S. could well be increased first in a move that would leave Japan and the euro zone lagging far behind.\par \par \uc2 Early Tuesday, the euro was finding a little support on hopes that the Irish bond auction, which should raise about EUR1.5 billion, proves successful. The market will not only be looking at overall demand for bonds but also the price Ireland has to pay.\par \par \uc2 More good news could come from Germany too, in the form of the latest ZEW business sentiment survey, especially if the current conditions index comes in up at 21 from 14.6 as forecast.\par \par \uc2 By 0645 GMT, the euro had risen to $1.2834 from $1.2818 late on Monday in New York, according to EBS. The single \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  was also up at Y109.49 from Y109.35.\par \par \uc2 The dollar ticked up a little to Y85.35 from Y85.30 as speculation over Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to halt the yen's rise increased after former finance ministry official Hiroshi Watanabe said Tokyo may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if the yen rises too rapidly, but that there is no need to act on the yen for now.\par \par \uc2 Investors are also looking ahead to a meeting between Prime Minister Naoto Kan and BOJ Governor Masaaki Shirakawa next Monday to see if they decide to introduce any further monetary easing.\par \par \uc2 Bloomberg TNI FRX POV\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par    Reuters   USD/DJ \par    Thomson   P/1066 or P/1074 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 (Nick Hastings has covered the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets and industry for over 20 years. Apart from his written commentary and analysis, he also appears on Fox Business News and CNBC television in Europe, Asia and the U.S. He can be contacted on +44-20-7842-9493 or by email: nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 TALK BACK: We invite readers to send us comments on this or other financial news topics. Please email us at TalkBackEurope@dowjones.com. Readers should include their full names, work or home addresses and telephone numbers for verif