\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan Structural Woes To Support JGBs-MONEY TALKS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,480 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2010\par 08:55\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0655 GMT [Dow Jones] Japanese government bonds are on a roll, and demand is likely to remain solid for the foreseeable future as structural problems in country's economy keep cash flowing into safe-haven JGBs. JGBs provide the best bet for investors in an economy confronting structural problems, deflation and anemic growth. See: =MONEY TALKS: Japan's Structural Woes Set To Keep JGBs Solid (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0651 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW sharply lower at 1,176.2 late vs 1,187.2 late Monday in Seoul, but pair ends domestic session off of day's low at 1,175.1 on suspected USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities in afternoon trade; BOK likely bought USD near 1,175 to curb KRW's sharp gains, according to four Seoul-based traders, with one of those traders estimating buying amount at more than $500 million. "With the successive failures to hold on to 1,190 support as well as 1,180 support (so far this week), it seems about right to anticipate lower levels from here," says local bank trader. Tips pair in 1,170-1,180 range tomorrow. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0650 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stock market remains sluggish as participants wary of taking bigger positions given lingering economic concerns. Overall volume paltry at under 850 million shares vs yesterday's entire 1.85 billion. STI flat at 2934.36, expected to end above 2900, with minimal upside beyond morning high of 2938. "Eyes will be on the U.S. job market that is still recovering at a snail's pace, as well as potential weakness in the euro zone. While Asia remains the favoured region and is expected to avoid a 'double dip', global uncertainties should still see the STI moving in a broad range in coming months," says DBS Vickers. Top 10 most active stocks in market comprise good mix of large, small caps, including Genting Singapore (G13.SG), +3.8% at S$1.64, Informatics (I03.SG), off 3.1% at S$0.155, Genting Hong Kong (S21.SG), +1.8% at US$0.29, Armstrong Industrial (A14.SG), +7.1% at S$0.455, Noble Group (N21.SG), +1.3% at S$1.62. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI near flat at 21,118.28 vs 21,078.40 midday, with much-eyed HK land auction starting. Results keenly watched given HK government's fresh measures aimed at reining in soaring property prices; results could dictate HK market direction for rest of day. Developers so far mostly rebounding gingerly after big fall yesterday; \uc2 SHK Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) +0.6% at HK$110.60, \uc2 Sino Land\uc2  (0083.HK) +1.7% at HK$13.50. Blue chips overall about half up, half down, but no index members rising or falling by more than 2%. Market volume light at HK$28.38 billion, suggesting investors not sure which way market heading next. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0640 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/SEK is seen trending lower Tuesday after the market had no desire to shoot for 9.5525 Monday and the marginally fresh high earned after a bullish print last week was not sustained, says SEB. "Under 9.4470 would be increasingly near-term bearish." EUR/SEK trades at 9.4320. (dominic.chopping@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are little changed Tuesday after surging to new highs Monday on the back of worries over peripheral euro zone countries and concerns over global growth. European equities are tipped to open a shade higher tracking modest gains in Asia. Yield premiums on peripheral bonds vs bunds have widened recently and Tuesday will be a key test of investor appetite with Ireland selling bonds for up to EUR1.5 Bln. The German ZEW index is also out at 0900 GMT. Over in the U.S., PPI and industrial production data are out at 1230 GMT and 1315 GMT, respectively. September bunds are up 0.03 at 132.08 and the 10-year bund is slightly lower at 105.88 to yield 2.326%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish bonds performed well in the front end of the curve Monday, supported by the idea that the Riksbank might eventually ease the pace of hiking rates, says Handelsbanken. The bank says: "A bit early maybe; two-year tightened by 3bp vs euro. The rest of the curve was unchanged vs core." The bank notes mortgages lagged government bonds as always in a strong rate environment. Adds: "We don't think this will last and stay bullish on mortgages, both vs swaps and govies." Sweden's two-year bond yield is 0.5bps higher at 1.225% while the 10-year bond yield is flat at 2.370%. (dominic.chopping@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude futures rise slightly as both upside, downside appear limited; while weaker dollar may offer support, buying interest low on weak macroeconomic signals in Japan, U.S. "Traders and investors saw a weakening economy and plentiful oil supplies as reasons not to be aggressive buyers when the euro rallied" recently, Cameron Hanover says in research note. "Oil has traded between $70 (and) $80/bbl, and there seems no reach to move beyond this range," says Ken Hasegawa, analyst at Newedge Japan. September Nymex oil last up 16 cents at $75.40/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD down as Singapore, U.K. and local banks sell take profit, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7680-7.7720 band near-term. "Trading volume is quite thin and this could mean the pair may fall sharply even on a small amount of sell orders," trader says. Pair last at 7.7699 vs 7.7730 this morning, compared with 7.7718 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bond yields settle lower following cues from international bond markets, prices boosted by demand for long-end government debt amid abundant liquidity. Actively-traded 20-year Taiwanese government bond yield ends down at 1.6800% vs last close at 1.6965%; local dealer says it could test 1.60% medium term. Also actively-traded 5-year government bond ends down at 0.9019% vs 0.9075%; dealer says short-end staying in range, will be tough to sustain below 0.9000%, but market has priced in likely chance of rate hike at Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next quarterly monetary policy meeting (which several economists say will be done as part of normalization of very low interest rates rather than concerns about tame inflation.) (jr.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY, USD/JPY tick up in reaction to media report that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  former MOF official Watanabe, says Tokyo may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if JPY's moves become rapid, and that there's no need to act on JPY's rise for now. \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer Mitsuru Sahara says short-term players "used his remarks as a good reason to sell back the yen after players failed to break (USD/JPY) support" of 85.00, EUR/JPY's 109.00 floor earlier. Says Watanabe's comments bolster expectations there could be \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if USD/JPY falls more sharply. Tips USD/JPY's resistance at 86.00 vs last 85.32. \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  senior dealer Jun Kitazawa says EUR/JPY may rise to 111.00 vs last at 109.70; EUR/USD gains in tandem, has upward bias with climb toward 1.2930 possible vs last at 1.2855. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0627 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end slightly lower as profit-taking in financial companies, weak demand for technology stocks weighed on market; Taiwan Composite Index down 0.1% at 7931.09, tipped to consolidate in 7880-7980 band tomorrow. "Non-tech companies had risen quite a bit lately, so it's no surprise that some people would want to take profit," says Nancy Wu, a trader at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "Meanwhile, technology companies generally didn't perform so well because third-quarter demand is expected to be weak." Adds, legislature's final vote on economic trade pact with China, or ECFA, widely expected to be approved later today, to have little effect on market as impact on agreement widely priced into market. Among actives, China Airlines (2610.TW) +1.9% at NT$21.90. \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) down 1.5% at NT$28.95. \uc2 Chang Hwa Commercial Bank\uc2  (2801.TW) down 1.6% at NT$19.00. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com) [ 08-17-10 0255ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100817e68h00044\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
