\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Bunds Near Flat; Irish Bond Supply, ZEW Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 1,612 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2010\par 08:36\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Bunds are little changed Tuesday after surging to new highs Monday on the back of worries over peripheral euro zone countries and concerns over global growth. European equities are tipped to open a shade higher tracking modest gains in Asia. Yield premiums on peripheral bonds vs bunds have widened recently and Tuesday will be a key test of investor appetite with Ireland selling bonds for up to EUR1.5 Bln. The German ZEW index is also out at 0900 GMT. Over in the U.S., PPI and industrial production data are out at 1230 GMT and 1315 GMT, respectively. September bunds are up 0.03 at 132.08 and the 10-year bund is slightly lower at 105.88 to yield 2.326%. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0636 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish bonds performed well in the front end of the curve Monday, supported by the idea that the Riksbank might eventually ease the pace of hiking rates, says Handelsbanken. The bank says: "A bit early maybe; two-year tightened by 3bp vs euro. The rest of the curve was unchanged vs core." The bank notes mortgages lagged government bonds as always in a strong rate environment. Adds: "We don't think this will last and stay bullish on mortgages, both vs swaps and govies." Sweden's two-year bond yield is 0.5bps higher at 1.225% while the 10-year bond yield is flat at 2.370%. (dominic.chopping@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] Crude futures rise slightly as both upside, downside appear limited; while weaker dollar may offer support, buying interest low on weak macroeconomic signals in Japan, U.S. "Traders and investors saw a weakening economy and plentiful oil supplies as reasons not to be aggressive buyers when the euro rallied" recently, Cameron Hanover says in research note. "Oil has traded between $70 (and) $80/bbl, and there seems no reach to move beyond this range," says Ken Hasegawa, analyst at Newedge Japan. September Nymex oil last up 16 cents at $75.40/bbl. (max.lin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0631 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/HKD down as Singapore, U.K. and local banks sell take profit, says senior trader at Chinese bank. Tips pair in 7.7680-7.7720 band near-term. "Trading volume is quite thin and this could mean the pair may fall sharply even on a small amount of sell orders," trader says. Pair last at 7.7699 vs 7.7730 this morning, compared with 7.7718 yesterday. (chester.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bond yields settle lower following cues from international bond markets, prices boosted by demand for long-end government debt amid abundant liquidity. Actively-traded 20-year Taiwanese government bond yield ends down at 1.6800% vs last close at 1.6965%; local dealer says it could test 1.60% medium term. Also actively-traded 5-year government bond ends down at 0.9019% vs 0.9075%; dealer says short-end staying in range, will be tough to sustain below 0.9000%, but market has priced in likely chance of rate hike at Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 's next quarterly monetary policy meeting (which several economists say will be done as part of normalization of very low interest rates rather than concerns about tame inflation.) (jr.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0629 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/JPY, USD/JPY tick up in reaction to media report that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  former MOF official Watanabe, says Tokyo may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  if JPY's moves become rapid, and that there's no need to act on JPY's rise for now. \uc2 Bank of Tokyo-Mitsubishi\uc2  UFJ senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer Mitsuru Sahara says short-term players "used his remarks as a good reason to sell back the yen after players failed to break (USD/JPY) support" of 85.00, EUR/JPY's 109.00 floor earlier. Says Watanabe's comments bolster expectations there could be \b \highlight22\uc2 FX intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  if USD/JPY falls more sharply. Tips USD/JPY's resistance at 86.00 vs last 85.32. \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  senior dealer Jun Kitazawa says EUR/JPY may rise to 111.00 vs last at 109.70; EUR/USD gains in tandem, has upward bias with climb toward 1.2930 possible vs last at 1.2855. (miho.nakauchi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0627 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan shares end slightly lower as profit-taking in financial companies, weak demand for technology stocks weighed on market; Taiwan Composite Index down 0.1% at 7931.09, tipped to consolidate in 7880-7980 band tomorrow. "Non-tech companies had risen quite a bit lately, so it's no surprise that some people would want to take profit," says Nancy Wu, a trader at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "Meanwhile, technology companies generally didn't perform so well because third-quarter demand is expected to be weak." Adds, legislature's final vote on economic trade pact with China, or ECFA, widely expected to be approved later today, to have little effect on market as impact on agreement widely priced into market. Among actives, China Airlines (2610.TW) +1.9% at NT$21.90. \uc2 AU Optronics\uc2  (2409.TW) down 1.5% at NT$28.95. \uc2 Chang Hwa Commercial Bank\uc2  (2801.TW) down 1.6% at NT$19.00. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0627 GMT [Dow Jones] China attracted $9.62 billion of foreign direct investment in July, down from $12.5 billion in June, Ministry of Commerce says. Still, economists play down significance, saying FDI likely to stay strong, driven in part by ample liquidity in U.S., Europe. "It is difficult to conclude the trend of FDI based on just one month's data. I am not worried about the FDI outlook," says \uc2 China International Capital Corp\uc2  economist Ziqiang Xing; expects China FDI to grow 25% this year, accelerating from 20.65% in January-July. (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0623 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are unchanged at 132.05, having hit a record high of 132.17 during Monday's session. While the contract may still rise further from here, technical studies are becoming more and more overbought, and long is definitely the crowded trade, says \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2 . The bank tips resistance at 132.30 and 132.97, while support is pegged at 131.54 and 130.90. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT [Dow Jones] ASIAN SUMMARY: Regional markets mixed; Nikkei down 0.4%, NZX-50 down 0.6%, HSI down 0.2%, STI down 0.1%, Taiex down 0.1%, SET down 0.1%, S&P/ASX +0.9%, Kospi +0.7%, Sensex down 0.1%, Shanghai Composite flat. In \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, JPY weaker vs USD, EUR after \uc2 Fuji TV\uc2  reported Japanese Prime Minister Naoto Kan, BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa will meet next Monday, expected to exchange views on how to deal with JPY's recent rise; USD/JPY at 85.32 vs 85.30 late Monday in New York, EUR/JPY at 109.65 vs 109.35; USD/EUR at 1.2850 vs 1.2818. 10-year JGB yield flat at 0.945% after touching fresh 7-year low at 0.920%. AUD/USD at 0.9012 vs 0.8980 before release of RBA minutes indicating nothing has shaken \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of comfort zone, inflation under control, appears set to leave rates on hold until final months of year. Australian wages expected to grow by around 2.6% over next 12 months vs May survey showing +2.5%, Melbourne Institute of Applied Economic & Social Research says. Bank of Korea will balance need for sound growth with price stability when setting policy rate in coming months, Gov. Kim Choong-soo reiterated; adds Korea's monetary policy stance "still deemed highly accommodative." Singapore's July non-oil exports +18.2% on-year vs June's downwardly revised +28.5%, +20% expected, as pharmaceuticals exports contracted, electronics shipment growth slowed. China July FDI +29.2% on-year at $6.92 billion vs June's +39.5%, January-July FDI +20.65% on-year at $58.36 billion. Spot gold at $1,224.20/oz, down $1.30 vs NY close. September Nymex crude oil futures up 13 cents at $75.37/bbl. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF holds above key technical support at 1.3260, a level which if breached could open to way to a decline to 1.3072, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "A drop through that level would be bearish and lead to the July low of 1.3072, and then the 1.3036 region would be in the pipeline," says technical analyst Axel Rudolph. EUR/CHF at 1.3388. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 60.6% at HK$995 million at interval, marking only 3.8% of market total volume vs 7.7% Monday's half-day, suggesting any further downside in HSI likely limited after index's 0.2% fall to 21,078.40 midday, with psychologically key 21,000 level expected to remain as strong near-term support. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) leads with HK$110.39 million, then \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$62.62 million, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) with HK$61.42 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$46.20 million and Tingyi (0322.HK) with HK$34.78 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com) [ 08-17-10 0236ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100817e68h0003z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
