e release of RBA minutes indicating nothing has shaken \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  out of comfort zone, inflation under control, appears set to leave rates on hold until final months of year. Australian wages expected to grow by around 2.6% over next 12 months vs May survey showing +2.5%, Melbourne Institute of Applied Economic & Social Research says. Bank of Korea will balance need for sound growth with price stability when setting policy rate in coming months, Gov. Kim Choong-soo reiterated; adds Korea's monetary policy stance "still deemed highly accommodative." Singapore's July non-oil exports +18.2% on-year vs June's downwardly revised +28.5%, +20% expected, as pharmaceuticals exports contracted, electronics shipment growth slowed. China July FDI +29.2% on-year at $6.92 billion vs June's +39.5%, January-July FDI +20.65% on-year at $58.36 billion. Spot gold at $1,224.20/oz, down $1.30 vs NY close. September Nymex crude oil futures up 13 cents at $75.37/bbl. (leslie.shaffer@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0613 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF holds above key technical support at 1.3260, a level which if breached could open to way to a decline to 1.3072, according to \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . "A drop through that level would be bearish and lead to the July low of 1.3072, and then the 1.3036 region would be in the pipeline," says technical analyst Axel Rudolph. EUR/CHF at 1.3388. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0557 GMT [Dow Jones] HK short-selling volume down 60.6% at HK$995 million at interval, marking only 3.8% of market total volume vs 7.7% Monday's half-day, suggesting any further downside in HSI likely limited after index's 0.2% fall to 21,078.40 midday, with psychologically key 21,000 level expected to remain as strong near-term support. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) leads with HK$110.39 million, then \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$62.62 million, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) with HK$61.42 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$46.20 million and Tingyi (0322.HK) with HK$34.78 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore non-oil domestic exports likely to moderate in 2H 2010 on concerns about sustainability of global economic recovery, Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew says after island nation reports July exports +18.2% on year vs +20% consensus, +28.5% June. "We could yet see more significant moderation in exports growth in 2H this year. Indeed, it is likely the export recovery may have reached a plateau in 1H-2010, and going forward the recovery momentum will slow and even look anemic for some months" because of concerns about the sustainability global recovery, high unemployment in U.S., EU, Liew says. Also, lingering Europe sovereign debt concerns could negatively impact trade flows. Adds, pharmaceutical exports remains wildcard for Singapore, magnifying peaks, troughs of export performance. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0550 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.7% at 1754.68 with local institutional investors seeking bargains after recent sharp declines, but analysts say anywhere above 1770 for Kospi unlikely at least in short term due to lack of fresh news in market as well as lingering uncertainties over global economic health. "Unless the economic data, mainly from the U.S., show solid improvement, the Kospi isn't likely to rise sharply from the current level (of around 1760)," says \uc2 Kyobo Securities\uc2 ' Kim Dong-ha; adds foreigners would continue make their moves based on U.S. market trend. Foreigners so far net selling around KRW117 billion today. Most tech shares up after recent drops, analysts say, with \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) +2.4% to KRW105,500 while Hynix (000660.SE) +0.2% at KRW22,150. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) off 1.8% at KRW134,500 on lingering uncertainties over \uc2 Hyundai Group\uc2 's planned bid for 35% stake in \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE).