ally key 21,000 level expected to remain as strong near-term support. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) leads with HK$110.39 million, then \uc2 China Construction Bank\uc2  (0939.HK) with HK$62.62 million, \uc2 Sun Hung Kai Properties\uc2  (0016.HK) with HK$61.42 million, China Mobile (0941.HK) with HK$46.20 million and Tingyi (0322.HK) with HK$34.78 million. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0556 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore non-oil domestic exports likely to moderate in 2H 2010 on concerns about sustainability of global economic recovery, Standard Chartered Bank economist Alvin Liew says after island nation reports July exports +18.2% on year vs +20% consensus, +28.5% June. "We could yet see more significant moderation in exports growth in 2H this year. Indeed, it is likely the export recovery may have reached a plateau in 1H-2010, and going forward the recovery momentum will slow and even look anemic for some months" because of concerns about the sustainability global recovery, high unemployment in U.S., EU, Liew says. Also, lingering Europe sovereign debt concerns could negatively impact trade flows. Adds, pharmaceutical exports remains wildcard for Singapore, magnifying peaks, troughs of export performance. (gaurav.raghuvanshi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0550 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi +0.7% at 1754.68 with local institutional investors seeking bargains after recent sharp declines, but analysts say anywhere above 1770 for Kospi unlikely at least in short term due to lack of fresh news in market as well as lingering uncertainties over global economic health. "Unless the economic data, mainly from the U.S., show solid improvement, the Kospi isn't likely to rise sharply from the current level (of around 1760)," says \uc2 Kyobo Securities\uc2 ' Kim Dong-ha; adds foreigners would continue make their moves based on U.S. market trend. Foreigners so far net selling around KRW117 billion today. Most tech shares up after recent drops, analysts say, with \uc2 LG Electronics\uc2  (066570.SE) +2.4% to KRW105,500 while Hynix (000660.SE) +0.2% at KRW22,150. \uc2 Hyundai Motor\uc2  (005380.SE) off 1.8% at KRW134,500 on lingering uncertainties over \uc2 Hyundai Group\uc2 's planned bid for 35% stake in \uc2 Hyundai Engineering\uc2  (000720.SE). (jung-ah.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0548 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.9% at 4477.3, hits 4-day high of 4486.1, albeit on light volume. Gains are broad-based, with industrials, financials and utilities outperforming, while materials are also making handy gains. Traders say strong 2Q results and upgraded FY earnings guidance from Carlsberg had some bullish influence on the Australian share market. S&P/ASX 200 is outperforming other markets in Asia, although U.S. stock index futures have turned up 0.3% and other risk assets have improved, with EUR/USD up 0.3% and AUD/USD up 0.4%. Today's index close in relation to 4480 will be important on the charts, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Close below 4480 would leave some risk of bear flag pattern. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/KRW edging slightly off earlier 1,175.1 low, with 3 Seoul-based traders saying they suspect USD buying \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities; pair now at 1,176.7 vs 1,187.2 late yesterday in Seoul. "I should say that pair will likely remain supported at 1,175 for the rest of the session," says local bank trader. But notes, another bout of USD selling by offshore players may kick in toward session close, weighing on pair. Tips pair in 1,174-1,178 range for rest of day. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0542 GMT [Dow Jones] Thai shares down 0.1% at 860.11 midday in choppy trade; moderate volume. Profit-taking countering selective buying in small, mid-cap stocks on individual cues. Investors reluctant to take major positions amid dearth of fresh leads, while 2Q earnings season already over, says BFIT Securities strategist Aphisit Limsupanark. Investors keeping eyes on second, third reading of 2011 draft fiscal budget by Thai parliament due Wednesday, Thursday. Support put at 856-857, resistance at 865. Among top actives, \uc2 Jasmine International\uc2  (JAS.TH) +5.1% at THB1.23 after hitting THB1.27 -- highest since April 2004 -- on positive earnings expectations for coming quarters, \uc2 True Corp\uc2 . (TRUE.TH) +1.7% at THB6.15, Charoen Pokpgand Foods (CPF.TH) +0.4% at THB25, \uc2 Kasikornbank\uc2  (KBANK.TH) down 0.8% at THB100.50, Banpu (Banpu.th) off 0.3% at THB608. (oranan.paweewun@dowjones.com) [ 08-17-10 0229ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100817e68h0003u\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
