\par\par \b \uc2 HEARD ON THE STREET: Daily Digest\b0\par\par\uc2 2,227 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2010\par 18:23\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Ireland Passes Bond-Market Test \par  \par    By Richard Barley \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Another hurdle surmounted. Fears about Ireland's EUR1.5 billion bond auction proved unfounded. Demand was strong. The sovereign's bonds should now recoup some of the losses racked up since the start of August. Concerns remain, but from a funding and liquidity perspective, Ireland looks strong.\f28 \par \par \uc2 Some investors were worried that recent sharp spread widening in Irish bonds, with 10-year yields peaking at more than three percentage points above those on German debt, presaged new turmoil in the euro-zone government bond market. In particular, a steady flow of poor news from Irish banks, including a fresh capital injection for \uc2 Anglo Irish Bank\uc2 , had raised concerns about the impact on the country.\par \par \uc2 The results proved the fears of turmoil to be overdone. Ireland drew bids of EUR5.1 billion for its auction - giving an aggregate bid-to-cover ratio of 3.4 times, its strongest performance this year. And while wide spreads undoubtedly acted as a lure for investors, the enormous rally in underlying German bonds means that Irish absolute yields have been falling in recent weeks. In fact, the 10-year portion of the sale came in at a yield 15 basis points below that achieved in July -- when risk appetite was relatively strong.\par \par \uc2 If EUR1.6 billion of consumer savings products are included, Ireland has now raised EUR19.9 billion in 2010, or 99% of its target. With a strong cash balance, it is funded into the second quarter of 2011 - and therefore can use September, October and November to make a start on prefunding next year's requirements.\par \par \uc2 Ireland does face strong economic headwinds. Interest payments are set to balloon as a proportion of revenues and savage budget cuts have hit growth. But crucially, the country has a track record both in terms of budget consolidation and creating conditions for strong economic growth. Painful deflation is restoring lost competitiveness. If the government can keep on track, that should pay off for bond investors.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: For Foreigners, China's Solar Market Is Cloudy \par  \par    By Andrew Peaple \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In August, talk of the sun and protection is usually linked to the beach. This year, in China, it's about access to the country's fast-growing solar-power industry.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's National Energy Administration has taken bids from solar-power developers to build out 13 projects around the country that will add 280 megawatts of solar power capacity. This will almost double the current level of solar-power capacity, and it's another step on China's path toward greater reliance on renewable energy. Solar power, it's hoped, will contribute as much as 20 gigawatts of energy by 2020.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It seems like a compelling opportunity for the world's solar-power players, but the light of Chinese favor isn't shining on foreign companies. Of the 135 bids put in by more than 40 companies for the contracts currently on offer, only one came from a non-Chinese outfit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In part, this is down to domestic companies' ability to compete on price, based on their own lower labor costs. Analysts at \f28 \uc2 \f2 HSBC\f28 \uc2 \f2  predict Chinese companies such as Trina, Suntech and Yingli -- all listed on the New York Stock Exchange -- will become ever more prominent as the solar-power market becomes more weighted toward large-scale installations rather than residential sales -- in which case, cost, rather than branding, is the key to success.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But also putting off outsiders is uncertainty about the tariffs China's electricity grids will pay for the power generated at these projects. Without knowing this, it's hard for developers to run the sums on their investment's potential return. Consider the challenges faced by U.S.-based First Solar: A supposed landmark project for it to build the world's largest solar-power plant in Inner Mongolia, signed last year, has stalled over concerns about funding and the tariff First Solar will be paid.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This time around, foreign companies aren't even prepared to put in token bids for Chinese solar projects, even though showing willingness now might pay dividends as the market continues its rapid expansion.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Mutterings that thinly-veiled protectionism would anyway see most of the contracts go to domestic players may not be wholly justified. But the lack of foreign enthusiasm is understandable. For them, the outlook on China's solar-power market is cloudy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: The No And Da Of Yen Action \par  \par    By James Simms \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen is as strong as it's been against the dollar in the past 15 years. That has \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  traders watching closely for signs that Tokyo might \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to relieve pressure on an already struggling export-oriented economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But when it comes to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign-exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  action by Japan, where you stand depends on where you sit.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Consider the big picture: when factoring Japan's trading partners and changing prices at home, the yen sits nearly 28% below its 15-year-old highs. China -- whose \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  grew substantially stronger between 2005 and 2008 -- has overtaken the U.S. to be come Japan's largest two-way trading partner. Japan has also suffered a decade of deflation.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This real-effective exchange rate index was up 3.6% in the 7 months through the end of July. Against the dollar, meanwhile, the yen is up 7.5% in nominal terms. It means the macroeconomic shock won't be as severe as the exchange rate against the dollar implies. That's a good part of the argument, so far, against \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's finance minister, Yoshihiko Noda, has practicality to consider as well. Geopolitics and the size of global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  markets mean that Tokyo will find effective \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  difficult without support from the U.S. and Europe. With politicians in both hoping their own weak \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  will bolster exports, approval -- let alone assistance -- from those two economies remains unlikely.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When it last \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , six years ago, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  sold 35 trillion yen in 15 months. A repeat would quickly deflate arguments put forth by Washington, Brussels and Tokyo for Beijing to allow the yuan to strengthen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Standing on principle, of course, will do little for already suffering Japanese exporters -- and their tense employees and shareholders. At \f28 \uc2 \f2 Toyota Motor\f28 \uc2 \f2 , for example, every time the dollar weakens by a single yen, $350 million is wiped out of operating profit. More broadly, Japan's economic recovery is already threatened by a slowing rebound in exports and weakening domestic demand. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  moves that make Japanese products more expensive overseas is the last thing the economy needs.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Tokyo is currently addressing the issue with talk, rather than action. In the short run, this has kept yen bulls at bay. For example, the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  weakened slightly Tuesday on reports that Prime Minister Naoto Kan and \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  chief Masaaki Shirakawa are due to meet next week, as traders speculated that the Bank of Japan might yield to pressure to take steps to weaken the yen.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 From where both the government and the BOJ sit, things are looking less comfortable by the day.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Blackstone Might Rewire \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Balance Sheet \par  \par    By Liam Denning \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 It isn't often a 62% premium offers reason for grumbling.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Blackstone Group\f28 \uc2 \f2 's takeover offer for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2  effectively gives it two-thirds of the company's generation capacity plus cash for no money down. That is because \f28 \uc2 \f2 NRG Energy\f28 \uc2 \f2  has simultaneously agreed to buy about a third of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2 's megawatts for $1.36 billion, or about $800 million more than the price tag for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2 's equity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Why didn't \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2  sell the assets to NRG and keep the money itself? Probably because it did this a year ago for no discernible gain. In August 2009, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2  sold about a quarter of its capacity to LS Power to boost liquidity. That didn't stop the stock dropping from about $10 then to less than $3 before Blackstone showed up.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 If shareholders are miffed, it is \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2 's bondholders that have real reason to worry. Should Blackstone decide to dividend the entire proceeds of the NRG deal back to itself, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2  will be left carrying its existing debt load on a smaller stream of profits. That would raise the company's already high credit risk. But then Blackstone, sitting on a potential $800 million instant profit and with no other shareholders to protect, need not worry too much about that.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Alternatively, Blackstone could use some of that profit to buy in some of those bonds. Doing so would reduce \f28 \uc2 \f2 Dynegy\f28 \uc2 \f2 's debt burden and, possibly, its cash interest costs, potentially boosting Blackstone's return when it sells the company down the road.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 This likely would only make sense, however, if Blackstone bought the bonds below even today's market values of between 60 and 80 cents on the dollar. Shareholders might wish they got a higher premium. Bondholders may end up scrambling to limit their losses.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 's Fight To Be Upwardly Mobile \par  \par    By Rolfe Winkler \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO \f28 \uc2 \f2 Eric Schmidt\f28 \uc2 \f2  thinks he can build a $10 billion mobile business. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Larry Ellison\f28 \uc2 \f2  is suing to get a piece of the action.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  CEO acquired his way to dominance in enterprise software, and his purchase of \f28 \uc2 \f2 Sun Microsystems\f28 \uc2 \f2  secured him an invite to the mobile party. But while Sun's Java programming language anchors many basic phones, it's at risk of being left behind in smart phones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 That's despite the fact that \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  uses an adapted version of Java for its Android software, the fastest-growing mobile operating system for smartphones. \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  claims the workaround violates Sun's intellectual property. [ 17-08-10 1623GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Of course, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  would like to participate in Android's stunning growth. In May, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  said 100,000 Android devices were being activated worldwide every day. By July, the figure had doubled to 200,000. Android's share of global smart phone sales was 17.2% in the second quarter says Gartner, from only 1.8% last year.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When users search on their phones--and click on paid links--\f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2  profits. It doesn't break out Android revenues, but its contribution is small. Still, Mr. Schmidt's $10 billion estimate underscores mobile's overall potential and shows why Mr. Ellison is fighting to get a foothold.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Java's current licensing revenue is small beer for \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2 --about $200 million estimates IDC, compared to analyst expectations for $34 billion in total \f28 \uc2 \f2 Oracle\f28 \uc2 \f2  sales this fiscal year. But considering that tens of billions of dollars worth of software is written in Java, Sun never managed to maximize its value.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 In seeking to change that, Mr. Ellison may end up targeting others besides \f28 \uc2 \f2 Google\f28 \uc2 \f2 . Next could be Android handset makers like \f28 \uc2 \f2 Motorola\f28 \uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par =HEARD ON THE STREET: Twists Ahead For UK Insurance Battle \par  \par    By Hester Plumridge \par    A DOW JONES COLUMN \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aviva and RSA: just the first chapter?\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. life and general insurer Aviva Monday defended a board decision to decline RSA's GBP5 billion offer for its U.K, Irish and Canadian general insurance businesses. RSA responded by saying the offer remains open. It could yet return with a higher bid. If that happens, Aviva should let shareholders decide its fate.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Aviva's management says its composite life and general insurance model is central to strategy, allowing capital benefits and cost synergies. It argues RSA's offer, at 1.6 times tangible book value and 9.8 times earnings, undervalues the businesses. That is broadly in line with RSA's own valuation, but in the U.K. and Ireland Aviva is market leader, and in Canada it is No. 2. Earnings are recovering from cyclical lows. At their peak, U.K. general insurers changed hands for 17 times earnings.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 But some Aviva shareholders are angry they were not consulted before the offer was declined. They argue Aviva's composite model also undervalues the general insurance division: RSA's offer is equivalent to roughly half Aviva's market capitalization; but its entire general insurance division, including sizeable European businesses, accounts for less than 40% of revenues.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A 2.5% fall in both group's shares Monday indicates the market is not expecting an imminent deal. There is currently no suggestion RSA would team up with life insurance consolidator Resolution to launch a break-up bid. Other big U.K. general insurance players: RBS, AXA and \f28 \uc2 \f2 Zurich Financial\f28 \uc2 \f2 , seem unlikely to bid for Aviva's assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For RSA, with its GBP4.4 billion market capitalization, a GBP5 billion rights issue was already a huge ask. Yet the three banks fully underwriting the deal would be prepared to support a higher bid, according to someone familiar with their thinking. RSA argues it could better manage Aviva's existing book and improve future underwriting discipline. Taxed and capitalized, annual synergies of GBP300 million could allow RSA to pay a premium of up to GBP2.1 billion. That could mean it returns to the table.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 For Aviva, accepting an offer would mean a reversal of current strategy; potentially undermining management. But if RSA raises the stakes, it should let investors write the next chapter. [ 17-08-10 1623GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 4257   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100817e68h0015z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
