\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,199 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 17 August 2010\par 12:02\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE JUNE CURRENT ACCOUNT DEFICIT NARROWS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The current account deficit of the euro area narrowed slightly in June, the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday, amid a rise in exports. (published 0819 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN ZEW AUGUST INDICATOR POINTS TO 2H SLOWDOWN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Expectations for Germany's economy fell in August, according to a closely-watched survey published Tuesday by the ZEW economic research institute. (published 0936 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK INFLATION SLOWS BUT TRIGGERS LETTER FROM BOE'S KING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. inflation slowed as expected in July, but not by enough to prevent the Bank of England Governor Mervyn King from having to write another explanatory letter to the government about the rate of price growth, official data showed Tuesday. (published 0939 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOE KING: STAND READY TO EXPAND OR REDUCE MONETARY STIMULUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of England Governor Mervyn King said Tuesday the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  stands ready to either expand or reduce monetary stimulus depending on the outlook. (published 0939 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA FDI GROWTH DROPS; SHARP SLOWDOWN SEEN TEMPORARY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's foreign direct investment growth slowed sharply in July, data issued Tuesday show, but economists played down the single-month fluctuation, saying the country's vast market will continue to make it a hot investment destination despite the global economic slowdown. (published 0543 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA: EXPECT EXPORT GROWTH TO CONTINUE TO SLOW IN NEXT FEW MONTHS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's Ministry of Commerce said Tuesday it expects the country's export growth to continue to slow in the coming few months. (published 0225 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ THINKS RECENT YEN RISE DOESN'T POSE IMMEDIATE THREAT TO REAL ECONOMY-SOURCES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  believes the yen's recent rise doesn't pose an immediate threat to the economy but is ready to consider additional monetary steps should the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surge higher or political pressure to act increase, said people familiar with the situation. (published 0708 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HUNGARY, OTHER EU MEMBERS DEMAND PENSION REFORM IN BUDGET GAP \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary is one of nine European Union member states demanding that the costs of earlier pension system reforms are reflected in their budget data and therefore contribute to lowering budget deficits, the Economy Ministry said in a release Tuesday. (published 0959 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par UK OSBORNE: PLAN TO CUT DEFICIT HAS STABILIZED UK ECONOMY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.K. Chancellor of the Exchequer George Osborne Tuesday said the government's austerity measures have begun to stabilize the economy and have placed the country on the road to recovery. (published 0736 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN ARAI: YEN'S RISE MAY BE IN "FINAL PHASE" - NIKKEI \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese Minister of Economy and Fiscal Policy Satoshi Arai said Tuesday that the yen's appreciation against the dollar may have entered its final phase, the Nikkei reports. (published 0524 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 JAPAN\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  EX-MOF WATANABE: NO NEED TO \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 INTERVENE\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  ON YEN NOW -REUTERS \f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  doesn't need to \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervene\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  now to curb the yen's rise but may do so if the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's moves become rapid, a former senior Finance Ministry official said, Reuters reported Tuesday. (published 0551 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SINGAPORE JULY NON-OIL EXPORTS UP 18.2% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Singapore's key non-oil exports rose by a slower-than-expected 18.2% in July from a year earlier, as pharmaceuticals exports contracted while electronics shipment growth slowed. (published 0501 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par RBA COMFORTABLE TO REMAIN ON POLICY SIDELINES FOR NOW \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  looks set to leave interest rates on hold until the final months of the year at least after telling financial markets Tuesday nothing has happened of late to shake it out of its comfort zone, with inflation under control. (published 0352 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN PAY HIKE EXPECTATIONS MODEST FOR NEXT 12 MONTHS -SURVEY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australian wages are expected to grow by around 2.6% over the next 12 months, in line with recent indicators suggesting the local economy is slowing and giving the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Reserve Bank of Australia\f28 \uc2 \f2  further scope to slow the pace of interest rate increases, the Melbourne Institute of Applied Economic & Social Research said Tuesday. (published 0100 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN PROPERTY BUBBLE EXISTS BUT NO NEAR-TERM THREAT OF BUST - MS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's sharply overpriced stock of housing remains a major risk to the economy, but the danger of hefty price declines over the near term seems low, \f28 \uc2 \f2 Morgan Stanley\f28 \uc2 \f2  said Tuesday in a research report. (published 0545 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par JAPAN, INDIA TO AGREE ON FREE TRADE PACT BY YEAR END -KYODO \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan and India are expected to reach a bilateral free trade agreement by the end of this year, Kyodo News reported Tuesday citing sources close to the matter. (published 0910 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA COMMERCE MINISTRY: 2010 TRADE SURPLUS WILL "SURELY" FALL \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's full-year trade surplus will "surely" fall this year, Ministry of Commerce spokesman Yao Jian said Tuesday. (published 0325 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA AIMS FOR MORE BALANCED TRADE - COMMERCE MINISTRY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's current recovery in exports and imports has been uneven because of a stronger increase in exports, China Commerce Ministry spokesman Yao Jian said Tuesday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC OPENS NEW INVESTMENT CHANNEL FOR YUAN-SETTLEMENT BANKS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  said Tuesday it will begin allowing some overseas financial institutions to invest in China's interbank bond market as part of a program to promote the use of yuan in settling cross-border trades. (published 0912 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOK GOVERNOR: TO ENSURE SOUND GROWTH, PRICE STABILITY WHEN SETTING RATES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Bank of Korea will balance the need for sound growth with price stability when setting its policy rate in the months to come, Governor Kim Choong-soo reiterated Tuesday. (published 2300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    ======== DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======== \par  \par BOJ WATCH: NOT READY TO EASE, BUT YEN, POLITICIANS COULD FORCE A MOVE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japan's central bankers don't think the yen's rise poses an immediate threat to the economy but they are ready to consider additional monetary steps should the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  surge higher or political pressure to act increase, people familiar with the situation say. (published 0956 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHARTING EUROPE: LONG-TERM BULL TREND IN BUNDS EYES 134.40 \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Government bonds are clearly a highly sought-after asset following fresh contract highs in gilts and U.S. Treasury-Note futures this week, and the record highs seen in the German bund suggest the 134.40 upside target is still achievable. (published 0831 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: WHY THE DOLLAR SHOULD RISE SOME MORE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Confidence in the dollar should be growing. (published 0715 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SNB WATCH: FRANC GAINS DAMP OUTLOOK FOR SEPTEMBER RATE HIKE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Swiss franc's strengthening in August and unexpectedly weak inflation data may dissuade the \f28 \uc2 \f2 Swiss National Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  from raising interest rates in September for the first time in three years, according to analysts. (published 0920 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: JAPAN'S STRUCTURAL WOES SET TO KEEP JGBS SOLID \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds are on a roll, and demand is likely to remain solid for the foreseeable future. (published 0634 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FED WATCH: BOSTON FED: ECONOMICS CAN'T REVEAL HOUSING BUBBLE \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Should economists and policy makers have identified the housing market bubble before it burst? The answer is most likely no, says the Federal Reserve Bank of Boston, because economic theory was not up to the challenge. (published 2108 GMT) [ 17-08-10 1002GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100817e68h000q6\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
