\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-GLOBAL MARKETS-Asian shares fall on bleak Japan data, yen up\b0\par\par\uc2 833 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2010\par 09:06\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 (Repeats to additional subscribers)\par \par \uc2 * Asian shares slip as Japan posts anaemic Q2 growth\par \par \uc2 * Weak Japan data underscores worries about global slowdown\par \par \uc2 * Yen inches up as investors reduce risk, look to havens\par \par \uc2 * Dollar retains strength vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 By Kevin Yao\par \par \uc2 SINGAPORE, Aug 16 (Reuters) - Japanese shares fell on Monday after data painted a gloomy picture for the economy, fuelling worries that a global recovery is rapidly faltering and spurring investors to shed risky assets.\par \par \uc2 A key index of 300 leading European shares <.FTEU3> opened up 0.2 percent, with firmer oil <CLc1> and metal prices providing some support despite weakness in Asian stocks and losses on Wall Street on Friday. [ID:nLDE67C1FZ]\par \par \uc2 Japan's economy expanded just 0.1 percent in the quarter to June, far less than the 0.6 percent analysts had expected, as export growth moderated and a recovery in consumption driven by government stimulus ran out of steam. [ID:nTOE67901S]\par \par \uc2 Slowing growth in the country's key export destinations, such as the United States and China, an uneven recovery in Europe and a strong yen are clouding the outlook further.\par \par \uc2 Japan's Nikkei <.N225> fell 0.6 percent, after an early drop of as much as 1.7 percent, weighing on the rest of the region, though technical charts showed it was approaching oversold levels, suggesting it may be due for a rebound.\par \par \uc2 The MSCI share index for Asia excluding Japan <.MIAPJ0000PUS> fell 1 percent at one point but later clawed back most of the losses as investors snapped up Chinese stocks on optimism that the economy remains on strong growth track.\par \par \uc2 Economists are confident China's economic growth in 2010 will beat last year's outcome of 9.1 percent and catapult China ahead of Japan as the world's second-largest economy. [ID:nTOE67A01A]\par \par \uc2 The MSCI Asia ex-Japan index lost nearly 2.9 percent last week on growing concerns about the global recovery, its worst performance in six weeks.\par \par \uc2 "The yen's rise may begin to hurt export growth in the latter half of the current fiscal year. I think the Bank of Japan and the government need to take decisive action against \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves. Solo \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is possible if the yen approaches 80 to the dollar," said Takeshi Minami, chief economist at Norinchukin Research Institute.\par \par \uc2 "If that is accompanied by monetary easing by the Bank of Japan, it may have a certain effect."\par \par \uc2 But most investors doubted a direct market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  was on the cards and argued instead the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may opt for more quantitative easing to support growth.\par \par \uc2 Economists polled by Reuters expect Japan's economy to slow further in coming quarters, with some predicting a double-dip recession, and the yen's recent rise to 15-year highs against the dollar is likely to add pressure on exports. [ID:nSLAAJE6A1]\par \par \uc2 South Korean shares <.KS11> fell 0.2 percent, led by technology exporters and banking stocks including \uc2 LG Electronics Inc\uc2  and \uc2 KB Financial Group Inc\uc2  as traders awaited data on U.S. industrial production and earnings from key U.S. retailers for further clues on corporate and consumer demand.\par \par \uc2 "Concern about the economy is fueling foreign selling. We are also seeing substantial programme selling as the economic and stock market outlooks grow more uncertain," said Lee Sun-yeb, a market analyst at Shinhan Investment Corporation in Seoul.\par \par \uc2 YEN GAINS\par \par \uc2 The yen firmed broadly as high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  faltered, with talk of possible demand from Japanese exporters and investor fund repatriation lending it additional support.\par \par \uc2 The flight from risky assets sent more buyers into government bonds as investors looked for safe havens.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell 0.4 percent to 85.81 yen <JPY=> and the euro shed 0.1 percent to 109.81 yen <EURJPY=R>.\par \par \uc2 The New Zealand dollar fell 0.7 percent against the yen to 60.43 yen <NZDJPY=R> and the Australian dollar slipped 0.6 percent to 76.44 yen <AUDJPY=R>.\par \par \uc2 The dollar fell a third of a percent against a basket of \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  <.DXY> after briefly touching its highest since July 23.\par \par \uc2 Japanese government bond futures hit their highest in more than seven years, with September 10-year futures rising 0.19 point at 142.58 <2JGBv1>, after advancing as high as 142.62, their highest since June 2003.\par \par \uc2 Gold <XAU=> gained $4 to $1,218.65 an ounce, its strongest in more than a month, as pessimism about the global economic recovery pushed equities down, though a firmer U.S. dollar could cap further gains.\par \par \uc2 U.S. crude <CLc1> gained 0.49 cents to $75.88 a parrel, after suffering its worst week weekly performance in six weeks. (Additional reporting by Elaine Lies in TOKYO and Jungyoun Park in SEOUL; Editing by Kim Coghill)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-GLOBAL (WRAPUP 2, REPEAT)|LANGEN|SI|AUF|Z|IF|SNS|J|ABN|E|U|ND|PHD|SNI|GNN|DNP|PCO   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100816e68g000dm\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
