\par\par \b \uc2 RPT-TAKE A LOOK-Five world markets themes in the coming week\b0\par\par\uc2 834 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 16 August 2010\par 07:55\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 (Repeats, without changes, story first published on Friday)\par \par \uc2 LONDON, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Following are five big themes likely to dominate thinking of investors and traders in the coming week and the Reuters stories related to them.\par \par \uc2 1/ PATH OF LEAST RESISTANCE\par \par \uc2 Broad-based yen strength and a euro retreat that is becoming almost as broad points to euro/yen as the path of least resistance for those looking for a way to play trends without fretting as much about the risk of Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  as they might on dollar/yen. The SNB has shown how to lean against the prevailing \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  trend without drawing international criticism for engaging in competitive devaluations -- though its experience also shows the limits of solo action. Watching what non-Japanese officials say about the pace of yen moves and their attitude to the possibility of solo Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may give some insight into how close Tokyo is to the limits of its tolerance. > Round up of policy and market stories on yen [ID:nECONJP] > Q+A on why the yen is so strong [ID:nTOE67B078] > Europe doesn't want \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 -source [ID:nTST002429]\par \par \uc2 2/ PERIPHERAL SIGNALS\par \par \uc2 Risk premia on peripheral euro zone government debt are rising again, with the 10-year Irish/German spread widening back to levels last seen before the announcement of the European bank stress test results on July 23 and Greek CDS underperforming southern European peers. Trigger points may initially have been country-specific and holiday-thinned markets have exaggerated some moves, but the trend is starting to look broad enough to risk becoming a drag on the euro. The real test will come in September when liquidity picks up and governments start issuing more debt again. The push up in yields at an Irish treasury bill auction suggests scope for setbacks. > Rolling euro sovereign debt report [GVD/EUR] > Euro zone sovereign debt issuance outlook [EURODEBT/O] > Irish \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  warning on yield premium [ID:nLDE67B0HX]\par \par \uc2 3/ RELATIVE VALUE\par \par \uc2 The Federal Reserve's and the Bank of England's gloomy assessments of the economic outlook, and Fed measures that have been dubbed QE2, should accentuate recent relative performance trends between U.S., UK, and German sovereign debt. With the ECB showing no inclination to reverse course on the monetary policy path it has charted, German benchmarks are expected to keep underperforming U.S. Treasuries and Gilts. BOE policy meeting minutes, which will be released in the coming week, will determine how much scope Gilts have to outperform while any resurgence of euro zone sovereign debt is likely to show up in a further narrowing of the U.S. 10-year T-note/Bund spread from recent eight-month lows. > Rolling reports on: Treasuries [US/] Gilts [GB/] > Analysis on European monetary policy [ID:nLDE67A11H] > European inflation-linked debt takes a hit [ID:nLDE67B1PX] > Analysis on UK inflation targeting [ID:nLDE67B1PX]\par \par \uc2 4/ LOOKING FOR RETURN\par \par \uc2 Europe tends to lag the U.S. economic cycle by six to nine months, and after the Fed's gloomier assessment, investors could start questioning how long export performance alone can ensure Germany's DAX keeps outperforming other major stock indexes. The German benchmark still looks cheap against the S&P 500 and Japan's Topix on a one-year forward P/E basis but big emerging equity markets may appear an even more attractive investment destination given relative growth prospects and that the MSCI emerging market index has a similar valuation to the DAX. Inflows into emerging market equities and bonds will be a trend to watch given a marked breakdown in the traditional linkage between the performance of developed stock markets and the relative performance of developed versus developing markets. > Interactive graphic on cross-asset correlations {\field{\*\fldinst{HYPERLINK "http://r.reuters.com/nuk64n"}}{\fldrslt{\cf2 \uc2 http://r.reuters.com/nuk64n}}}\uc2  > Crisis-tested emerging banks have attractions [ID:nLDE67A0HM] > Banks loath to jettison cash ballast [ID:nLDE67C0PT]\par \par \uc2 5/ SHIFTING OUTLOOK\par \par \uc2 The Fed's downgrade of the U.S. economic outlook and China's efforts to ensure a soft landing for its economy also pose risks for high-yielding \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the Australian/New Zealand/Canadian dollars, and these countries' stock markets, which had already discounted a pretty solid economic recovery. The risks are all the greater given speculative positioning has been increasingly short of U.S. dollars and long of dollar-bloc \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  in recent weeks. Anything that pushes back expectations of when the RBA, RBNZ or \uc2 Bank of Canada\uc2  will next raise rates can only compound such risks. > Analysis on U.S. economic outlook [ID:nN11217117] > Analysis on outlook for Canadian dollar [ID:nN11262027] > Live updates and consensus for G7 economic data <ECON>\par \par \uc2 The weekly item on key financial market themes for next week can be retrieved by using MKT/THEMES code on a news search. (Compiled by Swaha Pattanaik; Editing by Ruth Pitchford)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS/THEMES (REPEAT, TAKEALOOK)|LANGEN|C|D|E|M|O|U|MTL|GRO|SOF|OIL|J    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100819e68g000am\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
