\par\par \b \uc2 =DJ UPDATE: Japan Ruling Party Group Seeks 'Immediate' Steps Vs Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 658 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 12:24\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds comments from a DPJ lawmaker, more background on Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  policy.) \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--A group of lawmakers from Japan's ruling party urged the nation's government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to take "immediate and extensive action" to fight the rising yen Friday, in a fresh indication of growing concern over the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 The group of more than a hundred Democratic Party of Japan lawmakers-informally known as the "anti-deflation federation"--said in a statement that the government needs to "consider undertaking \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 " to retain appropriate exchange rate levels. The \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  also ought to implement additional "large-scale monetary easing immediately," the statement said.\par \par \uc2 Although it is unclear how seriously Tokyo policymakers will listen to the group, whose influence over policy decisions appears limited, the statement underscores the level of concern among Japanese politicians over the yen's recent rise to a 15-year high versus the dollar. A strong yen hurts Japanese exports, the main engine of the country's economic recovery over recent quarters.\par \par \uc2 "Even if the impact of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is small, we still find it necessary to take action to show the stance" that Japan wouldn't tolerate the ever-rising yen, Yoichi Kaneko, a DPJ Upper House lawmaker and a key member of the federation, told Dow Jones Newswires. "If possible, we would like to submit this statement to Finance Minister (Yoshihiko) Noda" in the days ahead, said Kaneko, formerly a Cabinet Office official.\par \par \uc2 The move by the lawmakers came as the dollar changed gands around Y85.80 Friday, not too far from the 15-year-low of Y84.72 marked Wednesday. The group's lobbying on the yen reflects their view that the government and the BOJ aren't doing as much as other advanced nations to combat economic stagnation, exacerbating the ongoing slowdown in Japan's export-driven economy.\par \par \uc2 "If nothing is done..it could become possible for Japan to suffer a double-dip recession," the statement said. "If we allow the yen's strength to remain untouched, causing the export industry's business performance to worsen, it will be inevitable for the Japanese economy ... to fall into doldrums and deflation to become more serious."\par \par \uc2 The government and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  have mostly relied on public comments over recent weeks to discourage investors from pushing the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  up too much. Thursday, Noda threatened "appropriate action" against the yen for the first time since he took office in June, while the BOJ's governor Masaaki Shirakawa issued an emergency statement warning against "substantial fluctuations" in exchange rates. But their verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  have had only a limited impact so far.\par \par \uc2 Noda did not act on a similar set of policy proposals handed to him by the same lawmaker group late last month, including a request for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to target certain levels of annual inflation. Prime Minister Naoto Kan has shown some sympathy toward the group's aims in a recent parliamentary session, but he hasn't gone beyond that.\par \par \uc2 Many of the group's members are young, rank-and-file lawmakers, a fact analysts say may make it difficult for them to influence government policy. While Noda has hinted at action, he has kept mum on whether he would order market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen. Speculation remains that conducting \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is probably difficult given signs that \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's trade partners, including the U.S., are comfortable with falls in their \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  versus the yen.\par \par \uc2 But speculation could grow that the BOJ may bend under political pressure to take additional monetary easing to combat the effects of the strong yen if the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  gains further.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 13, 2010 06:24 ET (10:24 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100813e68d00215\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
