\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Japan "Has Hit Yen Alarm" - RBS\b0\par\par\uc2 1,360 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 09:18\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in London. +44-20-7842-9464 \par Markettalk.eu@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0718 GMT [Dow Jones] "Someone in Japan appears to have hit the alarm on the yen rise. Policymakers have woken and the lights are being turned on," says Greg Gibbs at RBS. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  has "nothing to lose" from \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervening\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , and the apparent lack (so far) of G7 support is a "lame excuse" for doing nothing, he says. The country needs to show markets that it's serious about tackling deflation, Gibbs adds. USD/JPY trades at 85.94. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0718 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/CHF weakened as market liquidity thinned out, but as long as it holds above support at 1.3460, it still has upside potential, according to \uc2 Zuercher Kantonalbank\uc2 . "In the short term an upward correction to the 1.3605 region is overdue," says technical analyst Christian Eugster. The next support on the downside is at 1.3380, he notes. EUR/CHF at 1.3523. (neil.maclucas@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0715 GMT [Dow Jones] EUR/SEK's recoil down to 9.45 "was deeper than ideally wished for, but even though passing a micro-term equality point, buyers response to the move seems firm," says SEB. A move above 9.5020 would target fresh highs, then with 9.5140 and later 9.5525 in mind. Key near-term support is at 9.4350/250. EUR/SEK trades at 9.4756. (dominic.chopping@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0712 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI +0.3% at 21,166.76, with index managing to stay well above 21,000 level (intraday low of 21,012.51), mild rebound after falling 3.2% over past 3 sessions, but trading remains cautious amid uncertain economic outlook after weaker-than-expected U.S. jobs data, says Peter Lai, director at DBS. Expects index to remain in 19,800-22,200 range medium-term. "It's likely that the index will stay in range, as there are a lack of catalysts, while the overall economic recovery pace remains questionable in the U.S., which is likely to dampen interest in equity markets." Suggests looking for specific market leads for investment strategies on individual stocks. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) +1.5% at HK$62.15 after news chairman raises stake; MTRC (0066.HK) +0.7% at HK$28.40 after results. Volume modest at HK$39.86 billion. (susanna.tai@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0709 GMT [Dow Jones] Swedish bonds outperformed Thursday across the curve and markets are scaling back expectations on Riksbank rate hikes during the autumn, says Handelsbanken. Notes that currently a little more than two rate hikes are priced over the next three meetings. "We continue to be bullish on bonds but prefer to go further out as the front end is vulnerable to Riksbank stubbornness." Sweden's two-year bond yield is 1bps higher at 1.26% while the 10-year bond yield trades 2bps higher at 2.48%. (dominic.chopping@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0704 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian bonds price slightly higher in line with rupiah's appreciation and modest rebound in local stocks, dealers say. "There's a bit of buying interest in the government bonds," a dealer says; adds investors, however, still a bit cautious after risk aversion resurfaced earlier this week. 5-year yield down at 7.52% vs 7.58% yesterday; 10-year yield down at 8.01% vs 8.09%; 15-year down at 8.54% vs 8.60%; 20-year yield down at 9.05% vs 9.08% and 28-year yield edges down at 9.34% vs 9.36%.; elsewhere USD/IDR down at 8,970 vs 9,015 late yesterday; and stocks +0.4%. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0659 GMT [Dow Jones] GBP may be one of the ugly sisters, but it isn't the ugliest, says Nicholas Hastings in \b \highlight22\uc2 Forex\b0 \highlight\uc2  Focus. So, while it may now have seen the best of its recent gains against safe haven \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as the USD and the JPY, it will still do better than others, such as the EUR. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0657 GMT [Dow Jones] After several months of strong European peripheral performance, this week has seen a tempering of the optimistic investor sentiment, with deficit concerns clouding Ireland and denting confidence in the other peripheral bonds. And the outlook does not improve going into September, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  strategists. "An accumulation of risk factors is likely to impact the performance of peripherals in the coming weeks: maturing ECB liquidity, redemption of Irish GG [government guaranteed] bonds, resumption of EUR govt bond supply, and the 3-6 month lag between the turning point in US data and then that in EMU data." Peripheral yield spreads to Germany are unchanged on the day in early Friday trading with the 10-year Italian/German at 141.5 bps, Spanish/German at +163.5 bps, Portuguese/German at 269.5 bps, Irish/German at 288 bps, and Greek/German at 798.5 bps. (nick.andrews@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0654 GMT [Dow Jones] September bunds are down 0.14 at 131.08. \uc2 DZ Bank\uc2  says while the overall outlook remains positive, it recommends using current strength to take some profits rather than add to longs. Support is at 130.90 and 130.49. Resistance is pegged at 131.40 and 132.00. (neelabh.chaturvedi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.6% at 1357.04 on bargain-buying after 3-session losing streak; dealers say upbeat corporate earnings also lifting sentiment with Genting (3182.KU) +5.7 at MYR8.14 after its 51.7%-owned unit Genting Singapore (G13.SG) posts 2Q net profit of S$396.5 million vs loss of S$50.7 million year earlier. "The KLCI is holding well above the 1350 support. This bodes well for the outlook for next week," dealer says; tips KLCI to test 10-day moving average of 1360 in near term. Among gainers, Pacific & Orient (6009.KU) +1.6% at MYR1.26, Axiata (6888.KU) +1.6% at MYR4.32. Among decliners, PPB (4065.KU) down 0.8% at MYR17.22. (benjamin.low@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0645 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan bonds end mostly down on profit-taking triggered by U.S. Treasurys' fall overnight; large brokerage seen selling, driving benchmark 5-year bond's yield up to 0.9161% vs 0.8950%. 20-year bond's yield also up to 1.7120% vs 1.7110%. But 10-year bonds rise in price as demand outweighs tenor's limited supply circulating in market, says trader at local bank; 10-year bond's yield falls to fresh record low of 1.2730% intraday before rebounding slightly to end at 1.2835% vs 1.2880% yesterday. Taiwan \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 's announcement to cancel NT$20 billion 182-day T-bills auction scheduled for Aug. 20 because it has sufficient funds in the treasury had no impact on market, traders say. 5-year bond yield tipped 0.90%-0.95% next week. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0635 GMT [Dow Jones] Firm U.S. stock futures, gains across Asian markets suggest Singapore shares likely to close up, enabling STI to recoup part of its 2.3% loss so far this week. Benchmark +0.7% at 2948.83, with immediate resistance at 2950, then 2969 (Aug. 11 high). Gainers still outnumbering decliners by almost 2 to 1 in broad market. "The gains are still modest as all it takes is just another set of weak economic data for sentiment to turn sour. But there's liquidity in the market, which is a good sign, as it shows there's still a healthy level of participation," says trader at local house. Overall volume encouraging at over 1.7 billion shares worth almost same as whole of yesterday's S$1.49 billion, with Genting Singapore (G13.SG) accounting for about 30% of total shares, +10.9% at S$1.42. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com) [ 08-13-10 0318ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Doku