\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: STI +0.3%, 2950 Cap; Genting Singapore Sharply Up\b0\par\par\uc2 1,482 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 03:39\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0139 GMT [Dow Jones] Singapore stocks tad higher as investors start to nibble after hefty losses this week. STI +0.3% at 2936.79, with initial resistance at 2950. Top gainer among component stocks led by Genting Singapore (G13.SG), +9.4% at S$1.40, as investors cheer gaming group's return to profitability in 2Q10. Market breadth at almost 2 gainers for every decliner. "For the blue chips, be selective. Take the opportunity to buy those offering dividends, but I will not be aggressive because of developments overseas," says dealer at foreign brokerage, referring to slower economic growth in U.S., China. (frankie.ho@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Philippine shares may fall, tracking overnight losses on Wall Street; recent outperformers like property, banking stocks expected to lead losses on profit-taking. Benchmark PSEi may head towards 3450 support after closing 1.1% lower at 3483.02 Thursday; resistance tipped at 3500. "The bearish mood might linger today and continue to drag down local equities. A round of bargain-hunting cannot be discounted, which could limit the decline or even lift the market," says PCCI Securities Brokers. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Big EUR/USD buy-stops stand at 1.2875-1.2885 area, strong offers above 1.2910; buying interest at 1.2790, staggered down to 1.2760; large sell-stops below 1.2750, says Singapore-based dealer. EUR/USD last 1.2830. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0129 GMT [Dow Jones] Korea treasury bonds, bond futures little moved in early trade, taking a breather following yesterday's sharp gains on BOK's decision to keep key policy rate on hold at 2.25%; 3-year yield +1 bp at 3.77%, 5-year yield +1 bp at 4.34%, September futures flat at 111.31. Bonds likely to seesaw around current levels in quiet trade, with futures eyed moving in 111.20-111.45 range, says bond manager at local asset management firm. Notes, "I'm not sure how the market sees it at this point, but if participants are looking at the next rate hike coming in either October or November, they should buy bonds." 7 out of 12 economists, fixed-income analysts polled by Dow Jones shortly after BOK's rate decision yesterday forecast \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to raise policy rate by another 25 bps in September. (min-jeong.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0126 GMT [Dow Jones] KLCI +0.4% at 1355.12 with gains boosted by heavyweights like Genting (3182.KU) on expectations of stronger 2Q after its Singapore unit swings to black. "After three days of losses, it looks like some buyers are emerging to pick up cheap stocks," says dealer; but notes volume remains thin, index may slip back later in day. Tips 1350-1360 range. HwangDBS says market likely "relatively resilient" today despite Wall street's losses following a rebound late yesterday from low of 1342 to close at 1349. Advancers Genting +5.1% at MYR8.09, BAT (4162.KU) +1.0% at MYR44.00, Axiata (6888.KU) +0.9% at MYR4.29. Decliners \uc2 Malaysia Airports\uc2  (5014.KU) down 1.9% at MYR5.30, E&O (3417.KU) down 0.9% at MYR1.14. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] September Nikkei 225 futures up 0.1% at 9200; upside capped as yen strengthening concerns linger, despite comments by Japanese government and monetary officials hinting of action against stronger yen, says Investrust CEO Hiroyuki Fukunaga. Says expects futures to move in 9100-9250 range for rest of session; futures more likely to fall (vs rise), as traders think even further monetary easing measures wouldn't be enough to eliminate long-term stronger yen concerns, unless U.S. and other governments agree to coordinate to make Japan's steps more effective--which seems unlikely. (juro.osawa@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0121 GMT [Dow Jones] Any near-term rebound for HSI after shares set to open tad lower on Wall Street cue, should be taken as chance to sell, says futures trader; HSI already down 3.2% Tuesday through Thursday, after having risen 14 out of 15 sessions previously. Trader especially uneasy with both August, September futures ending yesterday at much wider-than-fair discount to cash (139 points, 231 points respectively), which coupled with USD gains, VIX spike, oil prices fall, suggest "risk aversion is back in vogue." Likely to outperform though are \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK), supported by big shares buy by \uc2 Li Ka-shing\uc2 ; \uc2 Li & Fung\uc2  (0494.HK) on strong interim results. HSI ended down 0.9% at 21,105.71 yesterday. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0114 GMT [Dow Jones] S&P/ASX 200 up 0.5% at 4420.7 on what looks like weekend short-covering, or a buy portfolio, despite a lack of bullish catalysts and risk factors tonight including U.S. retail sales and European GDP data. Volume is fair for a Friday, although inflated by heavy trading in \uc2 Telstra\uc2  (TLS.AU), down 1.7% on earnings downgrades. Materials accounting for most of the strength, with BHP Billiton (BHP.AU) up 1.0%, \uc2 Rio Tinto\uc2  (RIO.AU) up 1.3%, \uc2 Incitec Pivot\uc2  (IPL.AU) up 5.0%, though CBA (CBA.AU) also strong, up 0.9%. "We've seen some risk appetite trimmed off the market this week," says Shaw Stockbroking's head of trading Jamie Spiteri. "Some of the economic data out of the U.S., China and Europe just showed that global economic recovery will only be moderate. Markets were to some degree expecting that, but some of the good corporate results we've seen out of the U.S. and Australia have not been able to generate ongoing supportive momentum across the market." S&P/ASX 200 faces 50-day moving average resistance near 4444, according to Dow Jones Newswires technical analysis. Minor support at Tuesday's low of 4382.1 ahead of July 19 low at 4345.2. (david.rogers1@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0112 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong AUD/USD option-related offers stand at 0.9000, says Singapore-based dealer. AUD/USD last 0.8965 vs today's high of 0.8976. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0109 GMT [Dow Jones] Strong USD/JPY sellers this morning at 86.00 still have large sell orders at 86.20-86.30 area, says Singapore-based dealer; adds good-sized bids stand at 85.80, large buy-stops above 86.50; though USD/JPY up on speculative short-covering amid \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  fears (Asahi Shimbun reported PM Kan and BOJ Gov. Shirakawa may meet next week to exchange views on yen's recent appreciation and ways to deal with it), genuine USD sellers would be happy for opportunity to unload USD at higher levels. USD/JPY last 86.07 vs 86.19 morning high.(jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0103 GMT [Dow Jones] Base metals to hold around current levels for next few months, before re-stocking activity in Asia and a more robust demand recovery in developed world lead to stronger prices in 4Q, through 2011, \uc2 National Australia Bank\uc2  forecasts in monthly market update issued late yesterday. Bank says doesn't view recent slowing in Chinese production activity as a slump, rather, more a phase in economic cycle that won't hamper China's medium-term growth prospects. Chinese IP growth eased in 2Q, but current pace remains just below average over past decade, with weak zinc, nickel imports by China in accord with this, copper imports remain strong, it says. For all metals other than zinc, average stocks in LME warehouses fell over July, signalling some tightening in market, notes data for LME cancelled warrants suggest copper inventories set to fall further in coming months. Early in Asia today, LME 3-month copper eases $3 to $7,252/metric ton, zinc slips $6 at $2,050, lead loses $25 at $2,075, aluminum falls $11.50 at $2,152. (ray.brindal@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0100 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY likely to extend gains on further USD strength vs major \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  overnight; dollar-yuan central parity rate eyed around 6.8025, which would be highest since June 24, vs 7-week high of 6.8015 yesterday. On OTC, USD/CNY tipped at 6.7800-6.8000 vs 6.7851 close yesterday, already highest settlement level since June 29. "We expect the fixing will be set higher again," says Shanghai-based trader at Asian bank. "But exporters will come to sell the dollar because of the attractive level and an expectation that the yuan will appreciate further sooner or later." (joy.shaw@dowjones.com) [ 08-12-10 2139ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100813e68d0001b\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
