\par\par \b \uc2 News Highlights: Top Economic Stories Of The Day\b0\par\par\uc2 1,168 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 13:09\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE GROWS AT STRONGEST RATE IN FOUR YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro-zone economy grew at the fastest pace in four years in the second quarter, driven by an unexpectedly strong surge in Germany, preliminary data showed Friday. (published 1004 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EU: 2Q GDP GROWTH FIGURE CONFIRMS RECOVERY ON TRACK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Strong European Union economic growth in the second quarter confirms that the region's economic recovery is on track, but national governments must continue to cut their budget deficits, a European Commission spokesman said Friday. (published 1055 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par OPEC UPS 2010 WORLD OIL DEMAND GROWTH FORECAST ON US RECOVERY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Organization of Petroleum Exporting Countries Friday revised up its forecast for global oil demand growth this year as a small U.S. recovery drives a crude consumption rebound. (published 1041 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par CHINA MULLS ALLOWING FOREIGN INVESTORS TO TRADE YUAN BONDS - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The People's Bank of China\f28 \uc2 \f2  is considering allowing foreign investors to trade yuan-denominated bonds on the domestic interbank market as part of efforts to boost the yuan's global status, the Economy & Nation Weekly reported Friday, citing unnamed sources. (published 0923 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EURO-ZONE TRADE BALANCE SWINGS BACK TO SURPLUS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro zone's trade balance swung back into surplus in June from a deficit the previous month, as both exports and imports surged to their strongest level for more than one and half years, official data showed Friday. (published 1032 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN IMK INSTITUTE RAISES 2010 GDP GROWTH FORECAST TO 2.5% TO 3% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany's economy will grow by 2.5% to 3% rather than the 2.0% previously forecast due to the "sensationally good" second quarter growth figures, German economics research institute IMK said Friday. (published 0900 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMAN BGA EXPORT GROUP: GERMAN 2010 GDP MIGHT EXCEED 3% \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 German gross domestic product growth may top 3% this year if the pace of expansion continues, the head of the BGA Federation of German Exporters and Wholesalers said Friday. (published 1002 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par GERMANY CAN'T MAINTAIN 2Q GROWTH PACE - DIHK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Germany won't be able to keep up the surprisingly high pace of economic growth it registered in the second quarter, when the economy expanded at its fastest rate in 20 years, Germany's DIHK chambers of commerce said Friday. (published 0809 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH 2Q GDP BEATS EXPECTATIONS BUT LAGS GERMANY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French economic growth was stronger than expected in the second quarter of the year, but lagged Germany and the outlook for the French economy remains uncertain. (published 0958 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FRENCH CONSUMER PRICES DECLINE 0.3% IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 French consumer prices declined slightly in July after remaining stable in June, the country's statistics office said Friday. (published 0651 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par HUNGARIAN ECONOMY STAGNATES IN SECOND QUARTER \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Hungary's economy returned to stagnation in the second quarter as a recovery that started in the first quarter slowed and a rise in exports was offset by a plunge in domestic demand, data released Friday by the central statistics office, or KSH showed. (published 0841 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par SPANISH 2Q GDP +0.2% ON QUARTER, -0.2% ON YEAR \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The Spanish economy grew 0.2% in the second quarter from the first quarter, extending modest gains after a long period of economic contraction, according to preliminary data from the National Statistics Institute released Friday. (published 0700 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par BOJ BOARD CONCERNED ABOUT YEN RISE, STOCK FALL IN JULY \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bank of Japan policy board members were already concerned about the steep rise in the yen and weakness in Tokyo shares at their July meeting, according to minutes released Friday. This has added to market speculation that the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  may further ease monetary policy to curb the yen's strength. (published 0232 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par AUSTRALIAN 2Q SMALL, MEDIUM SIZED-BUSINESS CONDITIONS DECLINE -NAB \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Business conditions for small to medium-sized business in Australia fell broadly in the second quarter, according to an industry survey released Friday. (published 2348 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par PBOC ADVISER: CHINA, TAIWAN SHOULD CONSIDER \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 CURRENCY\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  SWAP PACT - REPORT \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 People's Bank of China adviser Xia Bin said China and Taiwan should use the Chinese yuan as the settlement \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  for cross-strait trade, and central banks on both sides should consider signing a \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  swap agreement, the Commercial Times reported Friday. (published 0040 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par S KOREA 2Q ACTUAL MONTHLY HOUSEHOLD INCOME RISES FASTEST IN 3.5 YEARS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 South Korea's actual household income in the second quarter grew at its fastest pace in three and a half years, as better economic conditions and robust exports more than offset a fall in asset income, Statistics Korea said Friday. (published 0300 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par     ======= DOW JONES NEWSWIRES ANALYSIS AND COMMENTARY ======= \par  \par CHARTING EUROPE: USD/CAD CONSOLIDATION SEEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 LONDON --Consolidation seems to be on the cards Friday for USD/CAD after the U.S. dollar slid against its Canadian counterpart Thursday. (published 0931 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  FOCUS: STERLING HAS UGLIER SISTERS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Sterling may be one of the ugly sisters, but it isn't the ugliest. (published 0815 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par TAKING STOCK: THINK AGAIN ABOUT JAPAN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 How often have you read in a broker's report that Japanese exporters' shares fell in response to a stronger yen? Well, maybe it's time to think again about just how strong the yen has been and what the answer means for Japanese stocks. (published 0826 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par WORLD \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : YEN DOWN VS DOLLAR, BUT MAY RISE AGAIN IF US DATA WEAK \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The yen fell against the dollar in Asia Friday after Japanese officials hardened the tone of their comments on the \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  market, but dealers said the safe-haven yen could rise sharply later in the global day if upcoming U.S. data deepen concerns about the health of the country's economy. (published 0601 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN EQUITY INDEX TECHNICALS: SEEN REBOUNDING \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Intraday DAX: The false breakout of the 6125 mark calls for a tentative rebound. Suggest long positions above 6065 with 6216 and 6260 in sight. The downside breakout of 6065 will open the way to 6000 and 5975. (published 0642 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par EUROPEAN INTEREST RATE TECHNICALS: UP MOVES EYED \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The contracts remain on the upside but should pull back before further up moves. (published 0749 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par FOCUS: AUSTRALIA OPPOSITION BLOC VOWS TO REPAIR CHINA TIES \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Australia's main opposition bloc is vowing to re-engage key Asian trade partners, especially China--upset by a series of diplomatic dust-ups last year--if it wins next weekend's federal election. (published 0558 GMT)\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par MONEY TALKS: US EYES ARE ON CHINA, SO JAPAN'S ALONE OVER YEN \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The last time the dollar dropped below the levels it reached Wednesday against the yen, the Bank of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  and the Federal Reserve jointly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervened\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  to push it higher. That was in July 1995. (published 2112 GMT)\f28 \par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  OPTIONS:DOLLAR/YEN OPTIONS DOWN;SPOT RISES TO Y86-LEVELS \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Dollar/yen \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  options declined slightly in Asia Friday as the underlying exchange rate rose to around the Y86.00 level, reducing demand for hedges against the dollar's declines. (published 0438 GMT) [ 13-08-10 1109GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1030 1804 4180   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100813e68d000w2\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
