\par\par \b \uc2 EUROPEAN MORNING BRIEFING: Stocks Seen Recovering Slightly\b0\par\par\uc2 3,890 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 07:00\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 SNAPSHOT:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 European bourses seen up, government debt mixed; euro, spot gold, oil futures up\par \par \uc2 -Germany Weighs Limits On \uc2 Google\par \par \uc2 -WSJ: Germany Probing Allegations H-P Execs Paid Bribes In Russia - Sources\par \par \uc2 -Greek, Portuguese Banks Raised Borrowings From ECB In July\par \par \uc2 -Merkel, Slovak PM To Discuss Slovak Rejection Of Greek Aid-Report\par \par \uc2 -German Govt Fines \uc2 Alstom\uc2  EUR91M For Price Fixing\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Infineon\uc2 , IBM Sell JV Altis Semiconductor\par \par \uc2 -US Authorities Investigating Pharmaceutical Companies - FT\par \par \uc2 -US Government: BP May Skip 'Bottom Kill' If Tests Show Gulf Well Sealed\par \par \uc2 -GM Chief To Step Down Ahead Of Listing\par \par \uc2 -\uc2 Oracle\uc2  Sues \uc2 Google\uc2  Over Android\par \par \uc2 -Caterpillar to Build Plant in Texas\par \par \uc2 -BOJ Board Concerned About Yen Rise, Stock Fall In July\par \par \uc2 Watch for: 0600 GMT GER Q2 GDP - 1st release; 0900 GMT EU Q2 Flash Estimate GDP; US retail sales and CPI are at 1230 GMT, US University of Michigan sentiment index and business inventories are at 1355 GMT and 1400 GMT.\par \par \uc2 EUROPEAN OUTLOOK & US/ASIAN SUMMARIES:\par \par \uc2 ========\par \par \uc2 Asian bourses are a bit higher after the week's losses on fresh worries about the longevity of the global economic recovery, especially in the U.S. and China. European stock markets are tipped to start marginally higher with government bonds mixed. The euro and sterling are trying for a rebound, while spot gold and oil futures are higher.\par \par \uc2 STOCKS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 European bourses are likely to start a bit higher on Friday as investors digest the week's losses on economic worries but also test the opportunities to pick up some bargains.\par \par \uc2 For Friday's opening, IG Markets is calling FTSE up 17 at 5283, the DAX up 8 at 6143 and the CAC up 8 at 3629.\par \par \uc2 "With a pretty decent earnings season not having the impetus to push stocks beyond their current trading range, many investors are now questioning what will be the catalyst to see stocks trade higher over the remainder of the year," said Cameron Peacock of IG Markets.\par \par \uc2 "With no obvious answer to this question, investors seem happy to lighten equity positions and wait things out in safer asset classes where capital preservation is more assured.\par \par \uc2 "Given it's a Friday and investors are a bit bruised and battered after a tough week we're not expecting too much opportunistic bargain hunting today," he added.\par \par \uc2 "Economic indicators are sending conflicting messages at the moment, suggesting uncertain times ahead," said asset managers at Gartmore.\par \par \uc2 But strategists at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  said they are still positive on European equities, which they believe are severely undervalued.\par \par \uc2 "European equities trade at multi-decade valuation lows relative to cash, government bonds, investment grade and high-yield credit and U.S. equities," they noted.\par \par \uc2 U.S. "retail sales are...going to be closely watched to see if that can counter the pessimism that has hit sentiment in the last couple of days," said \uc2 IG Index\uc2  of figures due Friday.\par \par \uc2 U.S. stock futures are higher on Friday, after markets fell for a third-straight day Thursday as a revenue warning from Cisco Systems weighed on other technology stocks including \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , while disappointing U.S. jobs data also sent jitters across the market.\par \par \uc2 U.S. weekly jobless claims reported Thursday unexpectedly climbed to their highest level in six months, contributing further to the somber tone in the markets.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Cisco\uc2 's such a big bellwether for the whole sector, especially for enterprise spending, that any comments they make are very relevant to the whole market," said Noman Ali, senior portfolio manager for MFC Global Investment Management. "They were cautious for the next quarter and that's not a good sign. Even for the quarter that just ended...organic growth was weaker. It kind of confirms the (Federal Reserve's) view on the overall economy slowing down and potentially is a risk to earnings estimates for the rest of the year."\par \par \uc2 Most Asian share markets are moderately higher on Friday after recent losses, but uncertainty over whether Japanese authorities will act to curb the yen's recent strength curtailed gains on the Tokyo market.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 :\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 The euro and sterling are rebounding against the dollar and yen on Friday, although tentatively so, given the continuing doubts about economic endurance.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials Thursday publicly aired their concerns about yen strength.\par \par \uc2 Still, some said the yen could soon resume its climb. "Taken together, Japan's official claims that it will take appropriate action and the fact they have done nothing underscores that the bar to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  is high," said \uc2 Brown Brothers Harriman\uc2  analysts in a note. "If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  were to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , it would, no doubt, prefer a multilateral exercise, as that would increase the chances of success. However, Japan looks out on the limb by itself," they added.\par \par \uc2 On Thursday, the euro and some other \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  tied to growth, such as the New Zealand dollar, were hit by "the negative news flow," said Andrew Busch, global \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago.\par \par \uc2 "Slower economic growth is a reality and this is being priced into the markets this week," he said.\par \par \uc2 When the yen is on the verge of appreciating too far too fast, "\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  tries to ratchet up the threat level of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Sebastien Galy, \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2  in New York. Galy said that while investors are watching \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  closely, they still believe direct \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets is a long way away.\par \par \uc2 Sterling is likely to keep to narrow ranges on Friday against the euro and dollar, at least until there is greater clarity as to the strength of the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 BONDS:\par \par \uc2 =====\par \par \uc2 Bunds and gilts may start mixed on Friday while investors mull over the need for more safety.\par \par \uc2 "We are now in a period of consolidation, after the fears raised by the FOMC (Federal Open Market Committee) earlier in the week," noted analysts at \uc2 Societe Generale\uc2 . "If we can continue to stabilize, recent moves look more like a clear-out of positions, and less driven by fear of a double dip."\par \par \uc2 The euro zone will grow at very subdued rates for the next two years despite recent signs of a German-led pickup in activity, according to a survey of forecasters released Thursday by the European \b \highlight22\uc2 Central Bank\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 In its Inflation Report Wednesday, the Bank of England cut its GDP forecasts and added that it expects inflation to fall below its target level by early 2012. "Both the forecasts and the tone of the document has led to a further scaling back of rate hike expectations in the market, with the balance of risks having shifted to further policy loosening rather than tightening in the shorter term," said Philip Tyson at \uc2 MF Global\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 U.S. Treasury bond prices are little changed on Friday, after markets declined Thursday, taking a break from their recent rally, after demand on a $16 billion sale of 30-year bonds was relatively weak compared with other sales earlier in the week.\par \par \uc2 "Money managers and insurance companies are forced to the 30-year bonds to grab yields. You have a slowing economy, no inflation, the Federal Reserve buying Treasurys, which all pushed bond yields lower," said Tom di Galoma, head of U.S. rates trading at \uc2 Guggenheim Partners\uc2  in New York. "The jump in jobless claims is another nail in the coffin for the economy."\par \par \uc2 Kevin Giddis, president of fixed-income capital markets at Morgan Keegan Inc., said that many investors sold shorter-dated Treasurys and moved into the 30-year bonds, helping the 30-year maturity to reduce its yield premium.\par \par \uc2 In Japan on Friday, Japanese government bonds rose in price as market speculation that the Bank of Japan will ease its monetary policy prodded investors to buy JGBs.\par \par \uc2 ENERGY:\par \par \uc2 =======\par \par \uc2 Oil prices are rebounding Friday on bargain-hunting but analysts said the fragile state of the global economic recovery continues to weigh on the market.\par \par \uc2 New York's main contract, light sweet crude for delivery in September, was up 57 cents to $76.31 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 London's Brent North Sea crude for delivery in September climbed 36 cents to $75.88 a barrel.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said prices remained under pressure from the faltering global economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 "The past week has been tough for shares (and) commodities...as investors succumbed to a new bout of worries about a double dip back into global recession in response to weak economic data in the U.S., China and Europe," said Shane Oliver, chief economist at \uc2 AMP Capital Investors\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "U.S. economic data was generally poor with a blowout in the trade deficit in June, coming on the back of weak data for construction and inventories," he said in a market commentary.\par \par \uc2 "European data was also weak, with the Bank of England revising down its growth forecasts and euro-zone industrial production falling in June."\par \par \uc2 METALS:\par \par \uc2 ======\par \par \uc2 Spot gold is at $1,214.60 a troy ounce, up $1.10, as bulls seem to be heartened that gold is rallying on weak economic data, a dealer said.\par \par \uc2 Copper futures are little changed. Some uncertainty remains about the direction for base metal prices, though some support can be found around current levels, in part buoyed by the appetite for resilient copper, said Triland Metals.\par \par \uc2 -By Dennis Baker; Dow Jones Newswires; dennis.baker@dowjones.com [ 13-08-10 0500GMT ]\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10319.95  -58.88  -0.57% \par Nasdaq     2190.27  -18.36  -0.83% \par S&P 500    1083.61   -5.86  -0.54% \par FTSE 100   5266.06  +20.85  +0.40% \par Xetra DAX  6135.17  -18.90  -0.31% \par CAC40      3621.07  -7.22   -0.20% \par Above are closing prices \par  \par Nikkei 225   9229.46   16.87  0.18% \par Hang Seng    21106.96   1.25  0.01% \par S&P/ASX 200   4429.30  28.41  0.65% \par Taiwan Index  7884.77  54.98  0.70% \par S.Korea Kospi 1740.29  18.54  1.08% \par Shanghai Comp 2585.39   9.91  0.38% \par Bombay SenSex 18144.09 70.19  0.39% \par Dow Future    10324.00 +50.00  +0.5% \par NASDAQ Future 1837.75  +10.50  +0.6% \par S&P Future    1085.80  +6.60   +0.6% \par Above are as of 0425 GMT \par  \par USD/JPY      86.10-14      +0.27% \par Range        86.21   85.85 \par EUR/USD      1.2864-69    +0.28% \par Range        1.2870  1.2825 \par AUD/USD      0.9013-16    +0.63% \par Range        0.9016  0.8958 \par G