\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: BOJ Board Concerned About Yen Rise, Stock Fall In July\b0\par\par\uc2 590 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 04:32\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments from BOJ minutes, background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Megumi Fujikawa \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Bank of Japan policy board members were already concerned about the steep rise in the yen and weakness in Tokyo shares at their July meeting, according to minutes released Friday. This has added to market speculation that the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may further ease monetary policy to curb the yen's strength.\par \par \uc2 "With regard to the recent appreciation of the yen and the fall in Japanese stock prices, some members said the effect on Japan's economic activity should be examined closely," the minutes for the BOJ's July 14-15 meeting showed.\par \par \uc2 The dollar plunged Wednesday to a 15-year low of Y84.72, stoking concern that a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take more steam out of Japan's already decelerating export-led growth. That sent the Nikkei Stock Average to a 13-month low of 9,065.94 Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Following the volatile market moves, BOJ Gov. Masaaki Shirakawa released an emergency statement Thursday expressing stronger concerns over "substantial fluctuations" in exchange rates and stock markets.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ will carefully monitor developments and any effects they may have on the Japanese economy," he said.\par \par \uc2 In a coordinated move, Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda also threatened to act against the rising yen for the first time in his tenure, saying the government "will take appropriate action while closely monitoring economic developments." Japanese officials in the past have often used the term, "appropriate action" as an indirect reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet traders are skeptical about whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will be able to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell the yen, given that the U.S. and Europe are seemingly comfortable with the yen's rises. Instead, analysts think the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be pressured for more aggressive easing to curve the yen's gains.\par \par \uc2 In the July meeting, a representative of the MOF repeated its request for the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to steer policy "in an appropriate and flexible manner," and to closely exchange views with the government, the minutes showed.\par \par \uc2 Still, it remains uncertain whether alarm over the rising yen has risen enough among policy board members to lead to immediate loosening. The BOJ on Tuesday decided to maintain its policy interest-rate target at a super-low 0.1% and leave special lending programs in place.\par \par \uc2 One policy board member said it was important to "comprehensively" assess the effects of the strong yen and stock falls, taking into account economic and market conditions at the time, according to the minutes.\par \par \uc2 The member added that when making such an evaluation, the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  should also keep in mind "the fact that domestic and overseas economies were recovering and that, compared with the situation in the United States and Europe, money and credit markets in Japan were more stable and Japan's financial system was considerably more robust."\par \par \uc2 Indeed, the policy board generally stuck to its optimistic views on the domestic economy, although many board members said both upside and downside risks were increasing compared with April, when it issued the latest outlook report on the economy and prices, the minutes showed.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ has kept its economic assessment unchanged since upgrading it in May. It said the nation's economy "shows further signs of moderate recovery" in August, thanks to improvements in overseas economies.\par \par \uc2 -By Megumi Fujikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2786; megumi.fujikawa@dowjones.com [ 13-08-10 0232GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100813e68d0005t\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
