\par\par \b \uc2 Tokyo Shares End Up As Govt Action To Slow Yen Rise Eyed\b0\par\par\uc2 By Kana Inagaki   \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES   \par \uc2 562 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 09:11\par \uc2 Dow Jones Asian Equities Report\par \uc2 AER\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)-- Tokyo shares ended marginally higher on Friday, with the Nikkei snapping a five-day losing streak on dip-buying in select blue-chips such as \uc2 Sony\uc2  and Panasonic, but skepticism over Japanese government resolve to tackle the strong yen capped gains.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei 225 Stock Average rose 40.87 points, or 0.4%, to 9253.46 following a 4.6% decline from last Friday through Thursday. The Topix index of all the Tokyo Stock Exchange First Section issues also rose 3.46 points, or 0.4%, to 831.24, with 26 of 33 subindexes closing in positive territory.\par \par \uc2 Trading volume was relatively low at 1.6 billion shares with the seasonal "Obon" holiday in full swing. Investors also await further cues on the health of the U.S. economy, such as retail sales data for July, following grim signs in the job market.\par \par \uc2 Stocks remained sluggish mostly throughout the morning as investors watched closely for further hints on whether the Japanese government or the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will take steps via additional monetary easing or \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  following comments made Thursday by Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda to "take appropriate action."\par \par \uc2 "There is no way markets will be satisfied with yesterday's developments," said Hideyuki Ishiguro, strategist at \uc2 Okasan Securities\uc2 . "The most important issue is whether any action is taken at all."\par \par \uc2 Masayoshi Yano, senior market analyst at Meiwa Securities, projected the Nikkei to trade between 9000 and 9300 next week, adding that the index may rise to 9500 if the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  takes extra steps to expand liquidity.\par \par \uc2 "If it turns out that the government is just giving lip service (regarding the strong yen), markets will test critical 9000 support," he cautioned.\par \par \uc2 Some dip-buying was seen after the Nikkei plunged to a 13-month intraday low on Thursday, making many blue-chip shares look extremely cheap. \uc2 Sony\uc2  closed up 1.9% at Y2,613, while TDK rose 1.6% to Y4,695.\par \par \uc2 Panasonic closed up 2.0% at Y1,086 after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  raised its rating to Buy, citing medium-term growth prospects from the company's integration with \uc2 Panasonic Electric Works\uc2  and \uc2 Sanyo Electric\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Other bellwether exporters closed with losses, however. \uc2 Tokyo Electron\uc2  lost 0.7% at Y4,255 while \uc2 Honda Motor\uc2  fell 0.3% to Y2,789 as strong yen worries continued to sour sentiment.\par \par \uc2 Dai-ichi Life Insurance closed down 4.5% to Y103,100 after hitting yet another all-time low, following a \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2  downgrade to Neutral. "Under current market conditions, we think it is very risky to invest in the stock," wrote analyst Natsumu Tsujino in a report, citing the company's high sensitivity to stock prices and interest rates.\par \par \uc2 Information technology services firm CSK Holdings was one of the heaviest-weighted drags on the Nikkei, closing down 7.3% at Y304 after Deutsche Securities cut its target price by 20% following the company's grim fiscal first-quarter earnings.\par \par \uc2 September Nikkei 225 futures closed up 80 points, or 0.9%, at 9270 on the Osaka Securities Exchange.\par \par \uc2 For the week, the Nikkei dropped 4.0% and remains down 12% year-to-date.\par \par \uc2 -By Kana Inagaki, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2795; kana.inagaki@dowjones.com [ 13-08-10 0711GMT ]\par \par \uc2 4159 5011 55117   \par \par \uc2 Dokument AER0000020100813e68d0000j\par }\page {\par\fs20{\