\par\par \b \uc2 UPDATE: Japan Finance Minister Steps Up Rhetoric Against Rising Yen\b0\par\par\uc2 469 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2010\par 11:53\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 (Adds more comments from finance minister; background)\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par    By Takashi Nakamichi and Tomoyuki Tachikawa \par    Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister escalated his warnings against the yen's rise Thursday, threatening to take "appropriate action" against the persistent rises for the first time since he took office in June.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda's remarks showed a heightened sense of caution among Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  officials over the yen's recent rise to a 15-year high versus the dollar. Japanese officials in the past have often used the term, "appropriate action" as an indirect reference to \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 "We will take appropriate action while closely monitoring economic developments," Noda said during an emergency news conference at the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2 . "I will extremely closely watch \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market movements with great interest."\par \par \uc2 If \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  does \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  to sell the yen, it would be the first such action in more than six years, and would likely surprise investors.\par \par \uc2 Many \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign-exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders doubt Tokyo will storm into the market anytime soon, partly because the U.S. and Europe don't seem uncomfortable with the rising yen, which makes their exports more competitive versus Japanese products.\par \par \uc2 But Noda's remarks failed to impress market participants, who pushed the dollar down more than a third of a yen to Y85.18 immediately after the press conference.\par \par \uc2 Noda's latest comments came after the dollar fell Wednesday to a 15-year low of Y84.72, stoking worries that a strong domestic \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  may take more steam out of the nation's already decelerating export-led growth.\par \par \uc2 Rises in the yen make Japan's products more expensive on global markets and cut the yen value of profits earned in foreign \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 . Around 0946 GMT the dollar stood at Y85.39.\par \par \uc2 Noda diverged from his remarks of the past few days. Previously, he had called the yen's rises "a little one-sided," and vowed to watch exchange rates "extremely carefully," but didn't threaten actual action.\par \par \uc2 While Noda made no mention of the possibility of joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  with other Group of Seven nations, he said finance ministry bureaucrats have kept in touch "not only with the U.S., but other countries to exchange information."\par \par \uc2 He added that he has also been reporting exchange rate movements to Prime Minister Naoto Kan and Chief Cabinet Secretary Yoshito Sengoku.\par \par \uc2 Asked about whether Tokyo has plans to call an emergency G-7 teleconference, Noda said: "I'm not thinking of going that far at this stage." Nor did he make direct comments about the likelihood of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  undertaking \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi and Tomoyuki Tachikawa, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com; tomoyuki.tachikawa@dowjones.com [ 12-08-10 0953GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100812e68c000y4\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
