\par\par \b \uc2 Yen  \highlight22\uc2 Intervention \highlight\uc2  Viewed As Unlikely Despite Strained Nerves\b0\par\par\uc2 By Katie Martin  \par \uc2 Of DOW JONES NEWSWIRES  \par \uc2 565 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2010\par 13:28\par \uc2 Dow Jones Global Equities News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 LONDON (Dow Jones)--\b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is extremely unlikely to try and weaken the super-strong yen any time soon, despite its clear unease with the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's recent climb, several market watchers said.\par \par \uc2 The chance of an \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  to pull the yen down from its recent 15-year high against the dollar has become an obsession in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets over the past two days. Thursday, the yen sank swiftly as the Bank of Japan called Tokyo traders to check their rates for the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 --a move seen in the market as a possible precursor to an imminent sting.\par \par \uc2 However, the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise is still too slow, and Japan still lacks the international backing needed to press ahead with such a move, according to many observers. \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  for the first time since 2004 remains a possibility, but it is unlikely to happen in the coming days.\par \par \uc2 "It is only a slim chance," said Yunosuke Ikeda, a senior yen strategist at Nomura in Tokyo. "Real, meaningful actions are still far away from these levels."\par \par \uc2 The \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  wouldn't request a dollar-buying move by the Bank of Japan unless the BOJ had already started easing monetary conditions further, because that would "break a gentlemen's rule," Ikeda said.\par \par \uc2 Japanese officials, particularly Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda, have made no secret of their unease as the shrinking chance of U.S. interest-rate rises and broad-based nerves among investors have shoved the yen some 10% higher against the dollar since the middle of May. Wednesday, the dollar sank to Y84.72--its lowest level since July 1995.\par \par \uc2 However, \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  now seems to remain unlikely. In an emergency press conference Thursday, Noda reiterated that he is watching yen movements closely, but he also said that there wasn't any plan to hold a special meeting of the Group of Seven leading economies to discuss the yen's ascent--a move seen as an important step to agree on a framework for a yen-weakening exercise.\par \par \uc2 "This press conference has disappointed those expecting an extraordinary G7 statement," said Ikeda at Nomura. In any case, while the yen's climb over the past two months or so has been fast enough to justify \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the shorter-term climb over the past week has been relatively slow, Ikeda added. The dollar's rapid decline to the Y82 area would probably be needed to justify a move, he said.\par \par \uc2 Other market watchers said real \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  can't be ruled out at this point. Still, they agree that the lack of international consensus over the need for a yen-denting move is a deal-breaker, particularly as major diplomatic powers have worked hard to convince China to allow even modest climbs in the Chinese yuan.\par \par \uc2 "I don't think they are going to do anything yet," said Neil Mellor, a \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  analyst at \uc2 The Bank of New York\uc2  Mellon in London. "It would jar with the G7, it would upset China, and it probably wouldn't work."\par \par \uc2 At 1100 GMT, the dollar was trading at Y85.33, roughly in the middle of its range for the day, according to trading system EBS.\par \par \uc2 -By Katie Martin, Dow Jones Newswires; 44 20 7842 9346; katie.martin@dowjones.com [ 12-08-10 1128GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100812e68c0012z\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
