\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: Sensex +0.1%; Rebounds On Strong SBI Earnings\b0\par\par\uc2 1,336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2010\par 10:21\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 0821 GMT [Dow Jones] Sensex turns up, +0.1% at 18,079.46 vs session-low of 17,914.78, on strong results from heavyweight \uc2 State Bank of India\uc2  (500112.BY), which +4.7% at INR2,725.25. SBI's stock hits all-time high of INR2,735.85 after lender posts 1Q net profit +25.1% on-year at INR29.14 billion vs INR25.07 billion average forecast in Dow Jones poll of 12 analysts. "This is another indication that there is nothing fundamentally wrong with the Indian markets. Given the rally we have seen lately (Sensex up over 5% in 3 months), there might be some correction, but nothing worrisome," says Kunal Dalal, head of research at KRC Research. Adds valuations aren't unduly expensive as even at 18,500-levels, Sensex would trade at about 18X on FY11 earnings estimate of INR1,030. other major gainers include Oil & Natural Gas (500312.BY), +2.4% at INR1,266, and \uc2 Hindustan Unilever\uc2  (500696.BY), +2.1% at INR265.70. S&P CNX Nifty flat at 5422.35. (shikhar.balwani@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesian government bond prices continue sliding on resurfacing risk aversion around the globe, dealers say. "The rupiah's slide against the dollar triggered selling on the government bonds," a dealer says; adds foreigners likely to continue trimming bond holdings Friday if risk appetite continues deteriorating; 10-year yield up at 8.09% vs 7.97% yesterday; 15-year yield at 8.61% vs 8.45%; 20-year yield up at 9.07% vs 8.90%. USD/IDR up at 9,000 vs 8,975 late yesterday. (i-made.sentana@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0819 GMT [Dow Jones] More verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  and price checking by the Bank of \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has driven the USD a little higher against the JPY. However, the USD is mostly lower elsewhere as investors appear to take profits on the sharp rally staged after the FOMC decided to preserve liquidity levels Tuesday. The improvement in general sentiment helped European stocks push into positive territory even though Asian stocks were lower. The USD is up at Y85.63 after dipping down to Y84.94 earlier. The EUR is up at $1.2921 as the market now waits for the latest euro zone industrial production data. The GBP is also up a little at $1.5694. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0816 GMT [Dow Jones] China's efforts to hold down the yuan are making life even tougher for the Japanese authorities, which are squaring up to painful yen strength. China's yuan-management program is indirectly pushing even greater inflows into the yen, as it's the only regional heavy-hitter left to take the strain, says Lutz Karpowitz at \uc2 Commerzbank\uc2 . \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  has already stepped up the verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 interventions\b0 \highlight\uc2  Thursday, and Karpowitz reckons a real \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  "cannot be ruled out" at this point. USD/JPY trades higher on these concerns, at Y85.62. (katie.martin@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0809 GMT [Dow Jones] The AUD has been a major casualty of the USD rally, and it, as well as other commodity \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , looks set to suffer more as global growth concerns rise. "The AUD/USD has broken below its 200-day moving average which is likely to generate selling interest among portfolio managers, with AUD/JPY already breaking lower from its recent trading range," the bank notes. AUD/USD trades at 0.8987, AUD/JPY at 76.97. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0807 GMT [Dow Jones] A worrying aspect of the global growth slowdown is that the recent improvement in labor markets is stalling worldwide, says \uc2 BNP Paribas\uc2 . "While this is most evident in the U.S. labour market, conditions seen recently in Canada, UK and even the Australian labour market report overnight, point to some worrying trends," the bank says. (nick.hastings@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0759 GMT [Dow Jones] The Turkish lira and bonds are flat in line with mixed global stock markets and a flat Istanbul Stock Exchange. USD/TRY trades at 1.5190, unchanged from Wednesday. The benchmark April 25, 2012 bond trades at 8.44%, unchanged from Wednesday. Analysts forecast TRY to trade between 1.5130 and 1.5260, and the benchmark bond to trade between 8.30% and 8.50%, in the short term.\par \par \uc2 0754 GMT [Dow Jones] August HSI down 1.5% at 20,989, falling much more than cash market down 1.1%, marking third consecutive session of market decline. Contract trading at steep discount to cash, around 70 points now, and same for September HSI futures, which now trading at 170-point discount to cash; both much wider than fair basis, roughly 40 points, 100 points respectively, pointing to negative bias in market's near-term direction. August futures' volume heavy at 79,999 lots. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0752 GMT [Dow Jones] Number of traders in Tokyo say BOJ conducted \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  rate check earlier in day, in move seen as form of verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  that has in past at times been followed by actual \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ; MOF makes decision on whether or not to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with BOJ conducting any actual \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market transactions. In rate check, BOJ asks commercial banks questions about \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  transaction plans, possible market moves; while \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  conducts regular dialogue on market with many banks, rate check would involve more detailed questions than usual. Trader at major foreign bank in Tokyo says bank was contacted by BOJ for rate check. Other \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealers at both Japanese, non-Japanese bank also confirm rate checks were conducted. Another senior \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer in Tokyo says "This was only the second time in my twenty years (working in this industry)" to have experienced a rate check. Other \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  dealer at major Japanese bank says heard BOJ was asking how much USD/JPY rate could be expected to move at that time if it were to buy $10 million worth of USD/JPY. USD/JPY last up at 85.70, off intraday low at 84.94. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0751 GMT [Dow Jones] Australia's markets are slowly awakening to risk of a hung parliament outcome in general election August 21, something which would almost certainly be negative for AUD, create volatility generally. If opposition Liberals win office, mining stocks will rally as a planned government tax is scrapped. Carbon tax will be next major policy area where Labor differs with opposition, while planned national broadband network could also be dumped. All will be clear next week - or, maybe it won't. See "=FOCUS: Australian Markets Vulnerable To Hung Parliament Risk." (enda.curran@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0750 GMT [Dow Jones] Hungarian forint hardly changes as some profit-taking is seen after the sharp gains of the past two weeks. EUR/HUF is at 281.40 versus 281.69 Wednesday afternoon. The next HUF support levels at 282.50 and 285, says Raiffeisen Bank, which expects the cross to trade in the 280.75-284.84 range for the rest of Thursday. Equilor brokerage expects no dramatic HUF weakening but notes that HUF volatility will remain high, and advises against opening long HUF positions. Adds that EUR/HUF, however, may return to below 270 after the October municipal elections if the government calms foreign investors' nerves. (margit.feher@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0749 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/MYR higher late at 3.1780 vs 3.1700 Wednesday; local dealer says pair tracking USD strength vs Asians as concerns over global economic recovery curbs risk appetite. "Until early yesterday, many investors were still looking at a ringgit strengthening trend in the near term but the confluence of negative news from the U.S., China and U.K. has changed everything," dealer says. Tips 3.1650-3.1900 range Friday. (kwan-por.lee@dowjones.com) [ 08-12-10 0421ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100812e68c0005w\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
