\par\par \b \uc2 Nikkei inches up as yen pulls back a tad\b0\par\par\uc2 839 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 01:24\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * Nikkei on downtrend; caution continues on yen -fund manager\par \par \uc2 * Technicals mixed; slow stochastic in oversold territory\par \par \uc2 * Nikkei falls 4 pct on the week, worst in a month\par \par \uc2 By Aiko Hayashi and Elaine Lies\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 13 (Reuters) - Japan's Nikkei rose 0.4 percent on Friday, drawing support from an apparent halt in the yen's advance against the dollar after Japanese officials stepped up their campaign against the yen's rise with a series of comments.\par \par \uc2 But the benchmark lacked clear direction, moving in and out of negative territory, as investors remained doubtful that dollar/yen moves will stabilise anytime soon. They also grew cautious after weak jobs data underscored persistent worries about the sluggish pace of the U.S. economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 On the week, the Nikkei shed 4 percent, its worst weekly decline in about a month, with shares of exporters hit hard by worries about the impact of the strong yen and a weakening outlook for global demand on their earnings.\par \par \uc2 "The market is still clearly on a downtrend. The yen's advance, which has been the main cause of recent market slides, is coming to a halt and that's why investors have stopped selling for now," said Mitsushige Akino, chief fund manager at Ichiyoshi Investment Management.\par \par \uc2 "But a majority of market participants doubt yen appreciation will end here, unless more substantial measures are taken in \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 , such as an easing in monetary policy or \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 ."\par \par \uc2 The yen rose to a 15-year high against the dollar of 84.72 yen this week on trading platform EBS, and market players say \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  at some point cannot be ruled out.\par \par \uc2 But on Friday the yen was weaker at 86.06 yen, helped by a report Japan's prime minister and the head of its \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  would meet to discuss ways to deal with the Japanese \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's strength.\par \par \uc2 The Nikkei ended up 40.87 points at 9,253.46. It touched a 13-month low on Thursday before paring losses.\par \par \uc2 The index is down 3 percent so far this month and has lost about 12 percent in the year to date.\par \par \uc2 The broader Topix rose 0.4 percent to 831.24.\par \par \uc2 If the dollar falls back below 85 yen, market players say the Nikkei could break below 9,000, although they add there are support levels at 9,091, a low hit last month, and 9,076, a trough marked in November 2009.\par \par \uc2 A fall below the Nikkei's support in the 8,600-8,800 range could open the way for a slide to 8,000 or 7,000, seriously alarming Japanese authorities, \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  traders in Tokyo said.\par \par \uc2 If that happens, authorities are likely to take firm action. It could be buying of more government bonds by the Bank of Japan, tweaking of the BOJ's special \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2  or \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  market, some said.\par \par \uc2 Wall Street ended lower for a third straight day as jobless claims rose unexpectedly to a nearly six-month high, the second straight week of increases.\par \par \uc2 A slew of U.S. indicators are due out later on Friday, including consumer price data as well as July retail sales, with already holiday-thinned trade likely to slow further as investors take a wait-and-see stance.\par \par \uc2 "Certainly there's concern about the U.S. economy, but there's also growing concern about various sectors going forward, both in Japan and overseas, as the result of things like rising inventories," said Hiroaki Osakabe, fund manager at Chibagin Asset Management.\par \par \uc2 "Whether the Nikkei breaks 9,000 or not has now become a key issue."\par \par \uc2 REBOUND OR DOWNTREND?\par \par \uc2 Charts suggest the Nikkei could be due for a bit of a rebound, with the benchmark on Thursday breaking under its lower Bollinger Band. The Nikkei's slow stochastic, a measure of how oversold the market is, has also fallen into oversold territory.\par \par \uc2 But its MACD, a measure of market momentum, is still pointing downward after a bearish cross.\par \par \uc2 Some exporters recouped ground after sharp falls, with \uc2 Sony Corp\uc2  gaining 1.9 percent to 2,613 yen.\par \par \uc2 Panasonic Corp\uc2  climbed 2 percent to 1,086 yen after \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  raised its rating on the stock to "buy" from "neutral", citing a good medium-term buying opportunity.\par \par \uc2 "We think equity finance is the last negative catalyst and think the market will focus on medium-term growth," \uc2 Goldman Sachs\uc2  analysts wrote in a client note.\par \par \uc2 But CSK Holdings, an information services company, tumbled 7.3 percent to 304 yen after Deutsche Securities cut its target price for the stock to 280 yen from 350 yen. It maintained its "sell" rating.\par \par \uc2 Some 1.60 billion shares changed hands on the Tokyo exchange's first section, after it hit the lowest volume since late December on Monday at 1.25 billion.\par \par \uc2 Advancing shares outnumbered declining ones by 2 to 1. (Editing by Edwina Gibbs)\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-JAPAN-STOCKS (UPDATE 6)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100812e68c001uh\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
