\par\par \b \uc2 WRAPUP 4-Japan officials step up campaign against yen rise\b0\par\par\uc2 850 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2010\par 07:01\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited    \par \par \uc2 * FinMin Noda says to deal with yen's rise appropriately\par \par \uc2 * Yen up after Noda says no plans for G7 talks on \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * BOJ checks dollar/yen rates with banks\par \par \uc2 * PM Kan decries yen gains as "rough" - Jiji\par \par \uc2 * MOF's Tamaki held talks with BOJ on markets\par \par \uc2 By Tetsushi Kajimoto and Shinji Kitamura\par \par \uc2 TOKYO, Aug 12 (Reuters) - Japan policymakers fired off a volley of comments on Thursday to curb yen strength and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  checked rates with banks as officials stepped up efforts to prevent the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  from harming a fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Finance Minister Yoshihiko Noda said, however, Group of Seven countries have no plans to hold a conference call about \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , sparking a brief yen rally as traders interpreted the remarks to mean \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely for now.\par \par \uc2 The yen has risen steadily against the dollar from around 95 yen in early May to a 15-year high on Wednesday of 84.72 yen, prompting \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets to speculate that authorities might \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 The dollar was trading at 85.35 yen on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Government officials have shown increasing alarm in recent weeks at the yen's rise against the dollar. On Thursday, both the finance minister and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  governor Masaaki Shirakawa issued comments simultaneously.\par \par \uc2 Noda declined to comment on \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , saying instead: "I'm closely in touch with the prime minister and the chief cabinet secretary with regard to the series of market moves. We will respond appropriately while carefully examining economic trends."\par \par \uc2 Shirakawa said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  and stock markets are showing big fluctuations caused by uncertainty over the U.S. economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 "The BOJ will closely watch market fluctuations and their impact on the Japanese economy," he said in a statement.\par \par \uc2 Earlier, market sources said the BOJ had asked banks for dollar/yen exchange rates saying it wanted to do a trade, a step beyond its usual contact. But it did not conduct any transactions, they said.\par \par \uc2 A senior BOJ official later confirmed it had conducted the checks as a part of its vigilance on markets.\par \par \uc2 Many traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  was unlikely for now.\par \par \uc2 "Finance Minister Noda's comments on not planning a G7 phone meeting disappointed the market as it took his comment to mean that Japan has not prepared anything to stem the yen's rise," said Yuji Saito, director for \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  at Credit Agricole.\par \par \uc2 "He did not indicate anything concrete and the market took it that the finance ministry will not do anything."\par \par \uc2 Indeed, a euro zone official on Wednesday had said European authorities would not welcome such a move by Tokyo and that joint \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by major central banks was not on the cards.\par \par \uc2 The market sees a monetary policy response from the BOJ as more likely than \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , but that would only come if the yen were to rise at a pace of 2-3 yen per day towards its 1995 record high of 79.75 per dollar, analysts say.\par \par \uc2 The BOJ checked rates in a similar manner when the yen surged in late November, just before it held an emergency board meeting and decided to launch a fixed-rate fund supply \b \highlight22\uc2 operation\b0 \highlight\uc2  that market players say was aimed at curbing the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2 "Whether \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  will \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  depends on how much global shares and U.S. bond yields will fall. But if the dollar falls to around 82-83 yen, the chance of \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  will rise," said Masafumi Yamamoto, chief \b \highlight22\uc2 FX\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist at \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 POLICYMAKERS STEP UP WARNING\par \par \uc2 Behind policymakers' rhetoric is concern that a stronger yen could threaten Japan's exporters and derail a feeble recovery from the global crisis.\par \par \uc2 Prime Minister Naoto Kan decried the yen rise as "rough", Jiji news agency reported.\par \par \uc2 Rintaro Tamaki, the finance ministry's top \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  official, said he had discussed the financial markets' situation with a Bank of Japan official in charge of international affairs.\par \par \uc2 Asked if he had discussed \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Tamaki said: "I exchanged opinions with Executive Director (Hiroshi) Nakaso on financial markets in and outside of Japan."\par \par \uc2 "We only talked about the markets as we are not in charge of monetary policy," Tamaki told reporters at the finance ministry.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's authorities haven't \b \highlight22\uc2 intervened\b0 \highlight\uc2  since March 2004, when a 15-month long yen selling spree came to an end. During that period, they sold 35 trillion yen to try to stop a rising yen from harming all-important exports and to fight deflation.\par \par \uc2 Even a rare G7 statement between meetings in October 2008, which singled out yen volatility and was seen as giving \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  approval to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2 , did not tempt Tokyo to step in.\par \par \uc2 Keisuke Tsumura, a parliamentary secretary at the Cabinet Office, told reporters the yen's strength poses a risk to Japan's economy and rapid rises in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  could call for some "measures". (Additional reporting by Tokyo bureau, Koh Guiqing in Sydney; Writing by Kazunori Takada; Editing by Neil Fullick)\par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY (WRAPUP 4)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN|AFA|CSA|LBY|RWSA|RWS|REULB|GNS|RNA|SXNA    \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100812e68c0009n\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
