\par\par \b \uc2 ASIAN MORNING BRIEFING: US Stocks End Lower; \uc2 Cisco\uc2 , Jobs Data Weigh\b0\par\par\uc2 2,510 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 13 August 2010\par 00:07\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 Thai markets closed for Special Holiday.\par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par DJIA      10319.95  -58.88  -0.57% \par Nasdaq     2190.27  -18.36  -0.83% \par S&P 500    1083.61   -5.86  -0.54% \par FTSE 100   5266.06  +20.85  +0.40% \par Xetra DAX  6135.17  -18.90  -0.31% \par  \par 2Y Tsy  100 5/32 -2/32 0.549% +3.2BP \par 5Y Tsy  101 9/32 -7/32 1.481% +4.5BP \par 10Y Tsy 98 30/32 -7/32 2.745% +2.6BP \par  \par USD/JPY       85.88-94     +0.64% \par Range         86.03-84.94 \par EUR/USD       1.2826-30    -0.33% \par Range         1.2932-1.2782 \par AUD/USD       0.8957-60    -0.16% \par Range         0.9008-0.8916 \par  \par USD/JPY 1M Vol   11.30/11.75% \par EUR/USD 1M Vol   11.95/12.35% \par AUD/USD 1M Vol   13.80/14.50% \par  \par Comex Gold          $1,216.70 +$17.50 \par Comex Gold Range    $1,199.50-$1,218.50 \par Nymex Crude (NY)     $75.74   -$2.28 \par  \par  \par U.S. SUMMARY & ASIAN OUTLOOK: \par STOCKS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 U.S. stocks fell for a third-straight day Thursday as a revenue warning from Cisco Systems weighed on other technology stocks, including \uc2 Hewlett-Packard\uc2  and \uc2 Microsoft\uc2 , while disappointing U.S. jobs data also sent jitters across the market.\par \par \uc2 The Dow Jones Industrial Average dropped 58.88 points, or 0.57%, to 10319.95, its lowest close since July 21. The measure's drop over the past three sessions amounts to its biggest three-day drop since a three-day slump that ended July 1. The Dow is now off 1.04% for the year.\par \par \uc2 Cisco\uc2  was the measure's worst performer Thursday with a drop of 2.37, or 10%, to 21.36, after the maker of networking gear reported quarterly revenue a bit lower than analysts expected and projected a slower pace of revenue growth for the current quarter. In addition, Chief Executive \uc2 John Chambers\uc2  warned that a return to normal economic conditions would take longer than previously expected.\par \par \uc2 Other technology companies also fell. H-P lost 63 cents, or 1.6%, to 40.14, while \uc2 Microsoft\uc2  gave back 37 cents, or 1.5%, to 24.49. \uc2 International Business Machines\uc2  slipped 1.53, or 1.2%, to 128.30.\par \par \uc2 The technology-heavy Nasdaq Composite shed 18.36, or 0.83%, to 2190.27. The Standard & Poor's 500 index lost 5.86, or 0.54%, to 1083.61, with the technology sector leading its decline.\par \par \uc2 "\uc2 Cisco\uc2 's such a big bellwether for the whole sector, especially for enterprise spending, that any comments they make are very relevant to the whole market," said Noman Ali, senior portfolio manager for MFC Global Investment Management. "They were cautious for the next quarter and that's not a good sign. Even for the quarter that just ended...organic growth was weaker. It kind of confirms the [Federal Reserve's] view on the overall economy slowing down and potentially is a risk to earnings estimates for the rest of the year."\par \par \uc2 China's shares fell to their lowest level in more than two weeks Thursday, because of uncertainties over the government's next policy move and a gloomy global economic outlook.\par \par \uc2 Analysts said the Shanghai index will likely consolidate around the psychologically important 2600 level this week, as the market is awaiting policy clues from Beijing following July's lackluster economic data.\par \par \uc2 "Under the current economic conditions, the possibility of either further tightening measures or accelerated easing are slight. The third quarter remains largely a policy watching period," said Li Xiaoxuan, an analyst at Shenyin and Wanguo Securities.\par \par \uc2 Tokyo stocks fell Thursday, with the Nikkei Stock Average hitting a 13-month low as the yen's relentless rise and jitters over a global economic slowdown hurt \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 -sensitive shares. Trading volume didn't necessarily reflect the volatility in the broader market, however. It totaled about 1.9 billion shares, due mainly to the onset of the seasonal Obon holiday.\par \par \uc2 "It's hard to see any sign that stocks will stop falling unless the Japanese government starts moving," said Masatoshi Sato, senior strategist at Mizuho Investors Securities.\par \par \uc2 "We seem to be falling into another global spiral of declining equities prices," said Yoshihiro Ito, chief strategist at Okasan Online Securities. He noted the near-term possibility of selling momentum building to push the Nikkei below 9000, pressuring the Japanese authorities to act to stem the strong yen's impact on the market.\par \par \uc2 Crude futures fell for the third-consecutive session Thursday in U.S. trading, settling below $76 as a weakening outlook on the economic recovery continues to stoke fears of dropping oil demand.\par \par \uc2 Gold rose as investors turned to the metal Thursday as a refuge after weaker-than-expected U.S. jobless data added to anxiety about the economic recovery.\par \par \uc2 Base metals on the London Metal Exchange recouped early losses to end mostly higher Thursday, buoyed by a bottoming in equities and the euro.\par \par \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 FOREX\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 : \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar advanced Thursday as rising fears of a global economic slowdown weighed on the euro and boosted the appeal of the U.S. \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  as a safe harbor.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The dollar also rose against the yen after Japanese officials ramped up their rhetoric against their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's recent strength.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The greenback's upward move was an extension of its strong gains Wednesday against most major \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , said Vassili Serebriakov, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Wells Fargo\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. Wednesday saw the largest single-day rise since December 2008 for the ICE Dollar Index, which tracks the greenback against a trade-weighted basket of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 .\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Concerns about global growth are back in the forefront, and the dollar is enjoying safe-haven demand," Serebriakov said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Late Thursday in New York, the euro was at $1.2833 from $1.2882 late Wednesday, according to EBS via CQG. The dollar was at Y85.90 from Y85.37, while the euro was at Y110.23 from Y109.99. The U.K. pound was at $1.5581 from $1.5683. The dollar was at CHF1.0499 from CHF1.0576. The ICE Dollar Index was at 82.569 from 82.276.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 A string of recent soft data out of Europe, the U.S. and Asia sent investors out of most growth- and risk-sensitive assets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Industrial production in the 16 countries that share the euro fell unexpectedly in June following declines in output in France and Germany, according to data from the European Union's Eurostat statistics office.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro was also hurt by reports the European \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Central Bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  bought short-dated Irish government bonds Wednesday and early Thursday in an attempt to calm market volatility stemming from concerns of the creditworthiness of Irish banks.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 U.S. weekly jobless claims reported Thursday unexpectedly climbed to their highest level in six months, contributing a bit more to the somber tone in the markets.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Finally, the Greek economy contracted sharply in the second quarter as government austerity measures bit deeper into incomes, according to government data released Thursday. The dismal figures reinforced concerns about the negative effects austerity measures will have on the economies of peripheral euro-zone nations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The euro and some other \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currencies\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tied to growth, such as the New Zealand dollar, were hit by "the negative news flow," said Andrew Busch, global \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at BMO Capital Markets in Chicago.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Slower economic growth is a reality and this is being priced into the markets this week," he said.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The flight to safety has Japanese officials concerned that yen strength will hurt Japan's export-driven economy. Japan's finance minister threatened to act against the rising yen Thursday, while the Bank of Japan governor issued an emergency statement warning against "substantial fluctuations" in exchange rates following the dollar's drop Wednesday to a 15-year low of Y84.72.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Reports that the Bank of Japan checked rates in Tokyo hours, which involves asking commercial banks for details of their \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  transactions, drove home the message that the Japanese government isn't happy with the yen's appreciation. Checking rates is considered a type of verbal \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , but a step more serious than "jawboning."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 When the yen is on the verge of appreciating too far too fast, "\f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  tries to ratchet up the threat level of \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 ," said Sebastien Galy, \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 currency\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  strategist at \f28 \uc2 \f2 BNP Paribas\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. Galy said that while investors are watching \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Japan\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 's \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 central bank\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  closely, they still believe direct \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 intervention\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  in markets is a long way away.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 ANZ Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2  Senior Dealer Alex Sinton said that while euro/dollar had been hurt by fears of a global economic slowdown, it might find support in Asia with people reasonably short euro. He tips the pair in a range of $1.2762 to $1.2897 in Asia.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par BONDS: \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Treasurys were lower Thursday, taking a break from their recent rally, after demand on a $16 billion sale of 30-year bonds was relatively weak compared with other sales earlier in the week.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Bond prices had gained over the past two sessions, pushing the benchmark 10-year note's yield to the lowest level since April 2009, as worries over the economic outlook spurred demand for safe assets. The Federal Reserve has acknowledged downside risks to the economy and moved to buy Treasurys to push down long-term interest rates to prevent a lapse in the economic recovery.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond auction is the last leg of this week's $74 billion new government debt supply. The securities were offered at a yield of 3.954%, the lowest level since March 2009. But it was higher than 3.937% on the auctioned notes traded just right before the sale, signaling the demand trailed market expectations.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Yet the 30-year bond posted a smaller rise in yield compared to shorter maturities as investors took heart from a positive sign of the auction. The indirect bid--a proxy of buying from foreign investors including central banks--jumped to 46%, the highest level since September 2009, up from the average of 32.3% for the previous four auctions. [ 12-08-10 2207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "That was indicative of the broad-based buying that has been taking place in Treasurys," said Richard Bryant, head of Treasury trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 MF Global Holdings Ltd\f28 \uc2 \f2 . in New York. "In this volatile but low return environment and in the absence of inflation, 4% yield long bonds will continue to draw cash off the sidelines."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 As of 1945 GMT, the benchmark 10-year note was 7/32 lower to yield 2.745%, and the 30-year bond was 13/32 lower to yield 3.949%. Bond prices move inversely to yields.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The 30-year bond had done better during the morning session. It rallied after weak jobless claims data further bolstered the argument that there is no risk of inflation, a main threat to bonds' value, as the economy is weakening. That has spurred many investors to buy the longest-maturity Treasurys still offering relatively higher yields than shorter maturities at a time when bond yields have dropped across the board.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Money managers and insurance companies are forced to the 30-year bonds to grab yields. You have a slowing economy, no inflation, the Federal Reserve buying Treasurys, which all pushed bond yields lower," said Tom di Galoma, head of U.S. rates trading at \f28 \uc2 \f2 Guggenheim Partners\f28 \uc2 \f2  in New York. "The jump in jobless claims is another nail in the coffin for the economy."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China government bonds ended higher Thursday amid safe-haven play, with the Shanghai exchange government bond index likely to extend gains in near term due to excess liquidity.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 China Everbright Bank\f28 \uc2 \f2 's IPO subscription only attracted CNY307.9 billion in funds, far below market expectations of CNY500 billion, a Beijing-based trader with a local insurer noted. "The figure (of subscription funds) highlights investors' pessimistic outlook on the stock market, helping support bond prices."\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Japanese government bonds finished higher Thursday, pushing yields across the curve down sharply to multi-year lows, as investors flocked to safe-haven assets on concern the soaring yen and weaker economic recoveries overseas could trip Japan's export-dependent economy.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 "Price swings in the super-long zone may become more volatile than other zones amid overall market rallying," said Akito Fukunaga, chief rates strategist at RBS Securities.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Supply and demand conditions have been tight at the superlong end of the curve recently, as there are no long-dated JGB sales planned until the next 20-year auction on August 24. Domestic banks, which used to be the main players in the five-year sector, are also now more active buyers of JGBs with longer maturities, leading to further pressure on supply.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 Among the data due Friday, Hong Kong second-quarter gross domestic product likely rose 5.7% on the year compared with an 8.2% rise in the first quarter, according to the median forecast of 9 economists polled by Dow Jones.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 The weaker reading is likely due to a higher base of comparison along with weaker exports and slower retail sales growth, economists said, adding that growth is likely to fall below 5% in the second half.\f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par  \par TOP HEADLINES: \par US Jobless Claims Rise 2K In Week \par GM Posts $1.3B Profit; Whitacre Stepping Down \par Junk-bond Issuance Sets Weekly Record \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Harrah's Entertainment\f28 \uc2 \f2  Files For IPO \par BP To Pay $50m Fine Over Texas City Safety \par Court Rejects Burkle Suit Vs \f28 \uc2 \f2 Barnes & Noble\f28 \uc2 \f2  \par Documents Sought In H-P Bribery Probe \par \f28 \uc2 \f2 Facebook\f28 \uc2 \f2  To See Over $1B In Sales In 2010 \par Blagojevich Jurors Agree On Just 2 Counts \par BOJ Governor Shirakawa: See Large Movement In \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 Forex\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2 , Share Markets \par Japan Steps Up Rhetoric Against Rising Yen \par HK 2nd-Quarter GDP Up Over 6% On Year - Source \par  \par  \par SCHEDULE OF MAIN EVENTS (times in GMT): \par 2245  New Zealand  2Q  Retail Trade Survey \par 2350  Japan        Jul Bank of Japan Monetary Policy Meeting Minutes \par 0200  Japan        Jul Electric Power Output \par 0300  New Zealand  Jul NZ Government bonds held for non-residents \par 0300  S Korea      Jul Import price index \par 0300  S Korea      Jul Export price index \par 0300  S Korea      2Q  Net barter terms of trade \par 0500  Singapore    Jun Retail Sales \par 0600  Germany      2Q  GDP - 1st release \par 0645  France       2Q  Flash estimate of job creation \par 0645  France       2Q  GDP - preliminary figures \par 0645  France       Jul CPI \par 0730  Thailand     Weekly International Reserves \par 0830  Hong Kong    2Q  GDP \par 0900  EU           2Q  Flash Estimate GDP \par 0900  EU           Jun Foreign trade \par 1130  India        Weekly \f28 \b \highlight22\uc2 \f2 foreign exchange\f28 \b0 \highlight\uc2 \f2  reserves \par 1230  US           Jul Advance Monthly Sales for Retail & Food Services \par 1230  US           Jul Real Earnings \par 1230  US           Jul CPI \par 1355  US           August Thomson Reuters / University of Michigan Survey of \par                    Consumers - preliminary \par 1400  US           June Manufacturing & Trade: Inventories & Sales \par N/A   New Zealand  Jul REINZ Monthly Housing Price Index \par N/A   UK           Office for Budget Responsibility - Sir Alan Budd steps down \par                    as interim chair of the Office for Budget Responsibility \par  \par -By Helen Ozolins, Dow Jones Newswires; +64 4 471 5990; helen.ozolins@dowjones.com \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 [ 12-08-10 2207GMT ]\f28 \par \par \uc2 1160 4119   \par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100812e68c001ic\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
