\par\par \b \uc2 *DJ Japan Fin Min Noda: Watching Yen Very Closely\b0\par\par\uc2 194 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2010\par 10:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones Institutional News\par \uc2 DJDN\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 Copyright \u169\'00\'A9 2010, Dow Jones & Company, Inc.  \par \par \uc2 11 Aug 2010 04:43 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fin Min Noda: No Comment On Whether To \b \highlight22\uc2 Intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 11 Aug 2010 04:43 EDT *DJ  \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  Fin Min Noda Was Speaking To Reporters In Tokyo\par \par \uc2 11 Aug 2010 04:55 EDT DJ  Japan Finance Minister Noda: Watching Yen Very Closely\par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's finance minister repeated Wednesday that he is watching the yen's moves very closely, but he declined to comment on whether he intends to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to arrest the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise.\par \par \uc2 "I am watching this development extremely closely," Yoshihiko Noda told reporters in the \b \highlight22\uc2 Ministry of Finance\b0 \highlight\uc2  after the dollar fell below the psychologically important Y85.00 mark to Y84.98 earlier in the day.\par \par \uc2 The consistent tone of his remarks over recent days suggests \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  is still a few steps away from \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market to sell the yen, a step not taken since spring 2004.\par \par \uc2 -By Takashi Nakamichi, Dow Jones Newswires; 813-6269-2781; takashi.nakamichi@dowjones.com\par \par \uc2 (END) Dow Jones Newswires\par \par \uc2 August 11, 2010 04:55 ET (08:55 GMT)\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJDN000020100811e68b001v5\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw205\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal3075\pichgoal600
