\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK: U.S. Investors Neutral On Asian ETFs-TrimTabs\b0\par\par\uc2 1,341 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2010\par 04:33\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0233 GMT [Dow Jones] U.S. investors are becoming neutral on Asian equities, according to TrimTabs. Six of 16 Asian ETFs tracked by TrimTabs posted inflows in the past two weeks while five saw outflows. Investors were least enthusiastic on developed markets - Japan and Australia. The two that saw big inflows were the iShares MSCI Hong Kong and iShares MSCI Singapore funds. (ellen.sheng@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0231 GMT [Dow Jones] Nikkei down 2.3% at 9333.15 midday after hitting fresh 3-week low of 9307.18 due to weak U.S. stock market, weaker USD/JPY, EUR/JPY, weaker-than-expected June machinery orders data; Nikkei may fall below 9300 and could hover between 9200-9300 for rest of session, says Yutaka Miura, senior technical analyst at Mizuho Securities, noting uncertainty over both in U.S. and Japanese economies also weighs on index. \b \highlight22\uc2 Currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves biggest market determinant at moment, vis-a-vis exporters. All 33 Topix subsectors in negative territory; \uc2 Nissan Motor\uc2  (7201.TO) off 2.4% at Y646, \uc2 Toshiba\uc2  (6502.TO) down 2.8% at Y423, Fanuc (6954.TO) off 2.8% at Y9,830, leading Topix machinery subindex down 2.4%; Makino Milling Machine (6135.TO) also off 5.3% at Y464. (atsuko.fukase@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] PSEi last +0.2% to 3532.30 in moderate volume, lifted by advancing conglomerates with \uc2 First Philippine Holdings\uc2  (FPH.PH) +2.4% at PHP56.30, Lopez Holdings (LPZ.PH) +6.7% at PHP4.48, \uc2 Ayala Corp\uc2 . (AC.PH) +0.2% at PHP332. Market breadth bearish, with profit-taking nudging losers ahead of gainers, 50 to 41. Decliners led by Metropolitan Bank & Trust (MBT.PH) down 0.5% to PHP60.10, \uc2 Aboitiz Power\uc2  (AP.PH) down 0.3% to PHP19.66. PCCI Securities Brokers says while falls in U.S. stocks overnight undermine investor confidence, "majority of the trending and volume oscillators are (still) bullish for the near-term." PSEi resistance at 3550, support at 3500. (cris.larano@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] CBOT wheat futures rise above $7/bushel in electronic trade on fresh buying amid concerns over defaults after recent surge in prices, new demand in physical market, traders say. Buyers in South Korea, Japan, Bangladesh have started to test market again with physical tenders, which is supportive, says executive at global trading company. Profit-taking is expected at regular intervals, says Singapore-based analyst. Most traders put immediate support at $7.0/bushel. Nearby September contract trading 11.50 cents higher at $7.0625/bushel. (sameer.mohindru@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +0.8% at 21,643.24, quickly resuming uptrend after 1.5% retreat yesterday, which "timely profit-taking" according to Taifook Securities; says economic outlook remains uncertain but new round of government stimulus in U.S., and probably in China as well, should extend liquidity-driven runs. HSI now rising for 15 sessions out of 17; volume solid at HK$7.43 billion. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) best-performing blue chip, +3.2% at HK$60.20 after exchange filing shows managing director, CFO bought company shares one day after interim results; HKEx (0388.HK), Tencent (0700.HK) both tad lower ahead of 1H results later today. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] China's new yuan loans in July at CNY532.8 billion, down vs CNY603.4 billion in June, also lower than CNY600 billion expected; data indicate credit growth already slowing and likely to slow further in coming months if Beijing sticks to annual CNY7.5 trillion new yuan loans target. PBOC data also show M2 +17.6% at end July from year earlier, growth also slowing vs end-June's +18.5%; market had expected 18.5% growth in M2 in July. (yajun.zhang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  raises China 2H export growth forecast to 16.4% from 10%, full-year 2010 growth forecast to 24.6% from 20.5%, citing strong shipping orders for U.S., European routes, solid bookings by export manufacturers, stronger-than-expected July exports. However, expects 2H import growth to rise by only 6.1% due to slowdown in infrastructure projects, housing construction. Raises full-year trade surplus forecast to $230.5 billion, an increase of 18% from 2009. "Despite the looming concerns about a global 'double-dip', we believe that Chinese exports will remain resilient." (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely lower, dragged down by weak IDR, falls in many Asian markets, traders say; tip support at 3030 after ending down 0.8% yesterday at 3057.161. "I expect bargain buying by foreign funds to prevent the main index from falling more than 0.5%," says trader at \uc2 Kresna Securities\uc2 . Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK), \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) may lead losers, on profit-taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi off 0.6% at 1767.07 as foreigners' selling weighs. Shinhan Investment analyst Han Beom-ho says jury's still out on ramifications of U.S. FOMC statement overnight: "they made clear that they won't stand by and watch the economy slip into deflation, but their comments also highlighted growth concerns going forward." Says no sharp selling likely, as likelihood of continued low interest rates in U.S. will attract carry-trade inflows to Asian markets; expects index to be supported around 1763. Tech plays seeing sharp declines as foreigners had bought into sector heavily so far this year; Hynix (000660.SE) 