\par\par \b \uc2 MARKET TALK:CBOT Wheat Futures Up On Defaults, Physical Demand\b0\par\par\uc2 1,300 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2010\par 04:30\par \uc2 Dow Jones Capital Markets Report\par \uc2 CM\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 \f2 \par  \par Contact us in Singapore. 65 64154 140; \par MarketTalk@dowjones.com \par  \par  \f28 \par \par \uc2 \f2 0230 GMT [Dow Jones] CBOT wheat futures rise above $7/bushel in electronic trade on fresh buying amid concerns over defaults after recent surge in prices, new demand in physical market, traders say. Buyers in South Korea, Japan, Bangladesh have started to test market again with physical tenders, which is supportive, says executive at global trading company. Profit-taking is expected at regular intervals, says Singapore-based analyst. Most traders put immediate support at $7.0/bushel. Nearby September contract trading 11.50 cents higher at $7.0625/bushel. (sameer.mohindru@dowjones.com)\f28 \par \par \uc2 0225 GMT [Dow Jones] HSI last +0.8% at 21,643.24, quickly resuming uptrend after 1.5% retreat yesterday, which "timely profit-taking" according to Taifook Securities; says economic outlook remains uncertain but new round of government stimulus in U.S., and probably in China as well, should extend liquidity-driven runs. HSI now rising for 15 sessions out of 17; volume solid at HK$7.43 billion. \uc2 Hutchison Whampoa\uc2  (0013.HK) best-performing blue chip, +3.2% at HK$60.20 after exchange filing shows managing director, CFO bought company shares one day after interim results; HKEx (0388.HK), Tencent (0700.HK) both tad lower ahead of 1H results later today. (robert.li@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0221 GMT [Dow Jones] China's new yuan loans in July at CNY532.8 billion, down vs CNY603.4 billion in June, also lower than CNY600 billion expected; data indicate credit growth already slowing and likely to slow further in coming months if Beijing sticks to annual CNY7.5 trillion new yuan loans target. PBOC data also show M2 +17.6% at end July from year earlier, growth also slowing vs end-June's +18.5%; market had expected 18.5% growth in M2 in July. (yajun.zhang@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] \uc2 Credit Suisse\uc2  raises China 2H export growth forecast to 16.4% from 10%, full-year 2010 growth forecast to 24.6% from 20.5%, citing strong shipping orders for U.S., European routes, solid bookings by export manufacturers, stronger-than-expected July exports. However, expects 2H import growth to rise by only 6.1% due to slowdown in infrastructure projects, housing construction. Raises full-year trade surplus forecast to $230.5 billion, an increase of 18% from 2009. "Despite the looming concerns about a global 'double-dip', we believe that Chinese exports will remain resilient." (jean.yung@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0215 GMT [Dow Jones] Indonesia shares likely lower, dragged down by weak IDR, falls in many Asian markets, traders say; tip support at 3030 after ending down 0.8% yesterday at 3057.161. "I expect bargain buying by foreign funds to prevent the main index from falling more than 0.5%," says trader at \uc2 Kresna Securities\uc2 . Bank Rakyat (BBRI.JK), \uc2 Bank Mandiri\uc2  (BMRI.JK) may lead losers, on profit-taking. (edhi.pranasidhi@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0214 GMT [Dow Jones] Kospi off 0.6% at 1767.07 as foreigners' selling weighs. Shinhan Investment analyst Han Beom-ho says jury's still out on ramifications of U.S. FOMC statement overnight: "they made clear that they won't stand by and watch the economy slip into deflation, but their comments also highlighted growth concerns going forward." Says no sharp selling likely, as likelihood of continued low interest rates in U.S. will attract carry-trade inflows to Asian markets; expects index to be supported around 1763. Tech plays seeing sharp declines as foreigners had bought into sector heavily so far this year; Hynix (000660.SE) off 4.7% at KRW21,550, LG Display (034220.SE) 3.8% lower at KRW36,660, \uc2 Samsung Electronics\uc2  1.1% lower at KRW784,000; \uc2 Hyundai Heavy\uc2  (009540.SE) +0.9% at KRW291,000 after announcing earlier Wednesday it won $700 million solar power plant order, reaching agreement with \uc2 International Petroleum Investment Co\uc2 . to buy 70% stake in Hyundai Oilbank for KRW2.573 trillion. (vincent.lee@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0211 GMT [Dow Jones] Taiwan government bonds mostly higher, with 20-year yield down 1.71 bps at 1.7330% as insurers buy long term papers, says local brokerage trader. Notes insurers were spotted buying after 20-year yield rose above 1.7500%. Still, gains in short-end bonds limited as "yields are still hovering near their multi-month lows, and the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  is expected to hike policy rate in the third quarter," he says. 5-year yield last at 0.9170% vs 0.9208% yesterday, tipped in 0.9100%-0.9300% band today. 20-year yield eyed in 1.7300%-1.7500% band. 10-year yield higher at 1.3215% vs 1.3208% yesterday. (mason.wu@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0207 GMT [Dow Jones] Large AUD/USD sell-stops stand below 0.9060 and 0.9020, says Singapore-based dealer. AUD/USD last 0.9091. (jerry.tan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0205 GMT [Dow Jones] Despite JPY's persistent strength, Japanese government likely still reluctant to push back \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  through \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , with such move remaining "a last resort," say \uc2 Barclays Capital\uc2  in research report. For this reason, says after FOMC decision overnight to reinvest proceeds from expiring mortgage-backed securities into long-term Treasurys, house thinks "the cleanest way to play for further USD weakness on the back of the FOMC announcement is positioning for a lower USD/JPY." But notes risk of at least further verbal \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  from \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities if Nikkei breaks 9,000, which says could "intensify politicians' angst about JPY strength." Nikkei last down 2.39% at 9,322.39. Earlier Tuesday, Japan Finance Minister Noda said "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves after the Fed's decision appear a little one-sided," but did not go beyond recent line that he "will keep closely monitoring the market," suggesting still not ready to order any action. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] China July CPI +3.3% from year earlier, up from June's +2.9% and slightly below expectations for +3.4%, according to Dow Jones poll of economists. Spike in July prices not likely cause for concern as due to one-off factors such as higher food prices following severe flooding; economists expect inflation pressures to subside in 2H. Underscoring receding inflation pressures, producer price index slows considerably, +4.8% in July from June's +6.4%, and well below expectations for +5.4%. Other data point to continued slowing growth: industrial production +13.4% in July, vs +13.7% in June; January-July urban fixed asset investment +24.9%, vs +25.5% in January-June. (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY slightly up after higher USD/CNY central parity rate set, but continued hopes for CNY appreciation limits gains; USD/CNY at 6.7739 vs 6.7720 last close; tipped in 6.7700-6.7800 range for session. Market also waiting for July CPI release for cues. "If the CPI is higher than expectations, it could add more pressure for yuan appreciation expectation," says Guangzhou-based trader at foreign bank. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to continue falling with investors selling to leave market after many companies announced plans to issue new shares to raise capital as banks keep interest rates high. "Fears over price dilution because of possible new shares issued are driving investors from the market, and weak inflows are also discouraging others from entering to buy today," says trader. Tips key index in 455-460 range after ending down 2.3% at 461.67 yesterday. (phammuoi.nguyen@dowjones.com) [ 08-10-10 2230ET ]\par \par \uc2 4022   \par \par \uc2 Dokument CM00000020100811e68b00029\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
