 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  authorities if Nikkei breaks 9,000, which says could "intensify politicians' angst about JPY strength." Nikkei last down 2.39% at 9,322.39. Earlier Tuesday, Japan Finance Minister Noda said "\b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves after the Fed's decision appear a little one-sided," but did not go beyond recent line that he "will keep closely monitoring the market," suggesting still not ready to order any action. (andrew.monahan@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0200 GMT [Dow Jones] China July CPI +3.3% from year earlier, up from June's +2.9% and slightly below expectations for +3.4%, according to Dow Jones poll of economists. Spike in July prices not likely cause for concern as due to one-off factors such as higher food prices following severe flooding; economists expect inflation pressures to subside in 2H. Underscoring receding inflation pressures, producer price index slows considerably, +4.8% in July from June's +6.4%, and well below expectations for +5.4%. Other data point to continued slowing growth: industrial production +13.4% in July, vs +13.7% in June; January-July urban fixed asset investment +24.9%, vs +25.5% in January-June. (aaron.back@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0158 GMT [Dow Jones] USD/CNY slightly up after higher USD/CNY central parity rate set, but continued hopes for CNY appreciation limits gains; USD/CNY at 6.7739 vs 6.7720 last close; tipped in 6.7700-6.7800 range for session. Market also waiting for July CPI release for cues. "If the CPI is higher than expectations, it could add more pressure for yuan appreciation expectation," says Guangzhou-based trader at foreign bank. (joy.shaw@dowjones.com)\par \par \uc2 0154 GMT [Dow Jones] Vietnam shares likely to continue falling with investors selling to leave market after many companies announced plans to issue new shares to raise capital as banks keep interest rates high. "Fears over price dilution because of possible new shares issued are driving investors from the