\par\par \b \uc2 Aussie, NZ dlrs pare large losses on yen speculation\b0\par\par\uc2 831 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 12 August 2010\par 00:20\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2 * Aussie, kiwi bounces on yen on talk BoJ may \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2 * Gains against yen help pare losses against U.S. dollar\par \par \uc2 * Rise in Australia unemployment lessens risk of rate hike\par \par \uc2 By Gyles Beckford and Koh Gui Qing\par \par \uc2 SYDNEY/WELLINGTON, Aug 12 (Reuters) - The Australian and New Zealand dollars bounced on the yen on Thursday amid speculation \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2 's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be close to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervening\b0 \highlight\uc2  in markets to curb the super-strong \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Yet the gains only helped them partially recoup heavy losses from Wednesday, when they both fell over 2 percent against the U.S. dollar amid unease over global growth.\par \par \uc2 The Australian dollar climbed as far as 76.83 yen <AUDJPY=R>, from the day's low of 75.79, after market sources told Reuters the Bank of Japan had checked exchange rates with banks in the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  market on Thursday. [ID:nTOE67B04X]\par \par \uc2 Traders said that, alongside remarks from Japan's Prime Minister Naoto Kan who had decried gains in the yen as "rough", fuelled talk the authorities may step in to weaken the yen.\par \par \uc2 "The remarks from the Japanese officials that they are unhappy with the yen strength have helped risk \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2 ," said Philip Burke, a lead \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  trader at \uc2 JPMorgan\uc2 .\par \par \uc2 Traders said \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  speculators were among the most active investors on Thursday.\par \par \uc2 Likewise, the New Zealand dollar jumped to as far as 61.17, from the day's low of 60.30 <NZDJPY=R>.\par \par \uc2 By early evening, the pair had pulled back somewhat, with the Aussie dollar at 76.61 and the New Zealand dollar at 60.98.\par \par \uc2 Their gains on the yen helped to pull them up modestly against the U.S. dollar. The Aussie dollar was up at $0.8975 <AUD=D4>, off the day's low of $0.8918 but still down from $0.9037 late Wednesday and last week's peak of $0.9221.\par \par \uc2 It had taken a brief spill on Thursday after a domestic jobs report was not as strong as some had hoped. Unemployment unexpectedly rose to 5.3 percent, compared to forecasts for 5.1 percent, while full-time employment dipped. [ID:nSGE67A0LI]\par \par \uc2 But if anything, some economists argued the modest rise in unemployment was a boon as it meant wage pressures would not fuel inflation anytime soon. Many believe wage pressures only start to threaten inflation when unemployment is under 5 percent.\par \par \uc2 They said it also gives the \uc2 Reserve Bank of Australia\uc2  (RBA) more room to keep rates unchanged at 4.5 percent while it monitors the health of the global economy.\par \par \uc2 "Today's data should comfort the RBA that the Australian economy is ticking over nicely at the moment," said Amber Rabinov, an analyst at ANZ.\par \par \uc2 "Employment growth is arguably running at a 'Goldilocks' pace - not too fast as to rapidly push the unemployment rate down and risk a broad-based surge in wages, and not too slow that the unemployment rate rises."\par \par \uc2 Investors seemed to agree. They cut their bets for Australian rates to rise again this year, with November interbank futures <0#YIB:> rising 0.015 points to 95.475.\par \par \uc2 The kiwi steadied around $0.7130, just above a three-week low of $0.7114 touched in early local trade, but was largely a hostage to broader market moves.\par \par \uc2 "In the absence of a big surprise, we suspect NZ dollar rallies will be limited to $0.7220 given headwinds from deteriorating global risk appetite," said BNZ \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  strategist Mike Jones.\par \par \uc2 Initial Support for the kiwi was seen at the 200-day moving average of $0.7105, with the first line of resistance around $0.7166, the week's previous daily low, ahead of $0.7238, the 21-day moving average.\par \par \uc2 Another sign of the patchy New Zealand economy came in the form of a survey showing manufacturing activity contracted for the first time in 11 months in July. See [ID:nSGE67A0KV]\par \par \uc2 The data added to the view that the Reserve Bank of NZ may pause its timetable of gradual rate rises. The next RBNZ review is on Sept 16 with market pricing for a 25 basis point rise to 3.25 percent cut back to 50 percent from 61 percent on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 New Zealand second quarter retail sales is due on Friday, with a Reuters poll picking a modest 0.2 percent rise in quarterly sales volumes, with June month sales seen rising 0.4 percent. <ECONNZ>\par \par \uc2 NZ real estate industry sales and prices data is also due.\par \par \uc2 NZ debt <0#NZBMK=> gained on the safety bid, with government bond yields, which move inversely to prices, a couple of basis points lower. ((Australia/New Zealand bureaux; +61 2 9373 1800/+64 4 471 4234)) ((If you have a query or comment on this story, send an email to news.feedback.asia@thomsonreuters.com))\par \par \uc2 MARKETS-AUSTRALIA-NEWZEALAND-\b \highlight22\uc2 FOREX\b0 \highlight\uc2 /BONDS (UPDATE1)|LANGEN|Table|ND|ABN|D|M|RBN|AUF|E|RNP|DNP|PTD|PMF   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100814e68b000di\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw172\pich40\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2580\pichgoal600
