\par\par \b \uc2 INTERVIEW-UPDATE 1-Japan's Otsuka:  \highlight22\uc2 forex \highlight\uc2  rates at critical point\b0\par\par\uc2 625 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2010\par 08:22\par \uc2 Reuters News\par \uc2 LBA\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Reuters Limited   \par \par \uc2  * Vice bank minister doubts effect of solo \b \highlight22\uc2 fx intervention\b0 \highlight\par \par \uc2  * Says govt, BOJ must act together against rapid yen rise\par \par \uc2  * Warns rapid yen rise could deepen deflation\par \par \uc2  * Sees no need for BOJ to set rigid inflation targets  (Adds quotes, details)\par \par \uc2  By Tetsushi Kajimoto\par \par \uc2  TOKYO, Aug 11 (Reuters) - \b \highlight22\uc2 Foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  rates are at a critical juncture and if the yen rises rapidly Japan's government and \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  must work together to keep it from damaging the nation's economy, the vice banking minister said on Wednesday.\par \par \uc2  But Kohei Otsuka, a key policy-maker in Prime Minister Naoto Kan's Democratic Party, questioned if there would be any benefit from solo \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  by Tokyo in \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  markets, saying it would have little effect on the yen's rise.\par \par \uc2  Japan's finance minister has voiced concerns about one-sided and disorderly market moves as the yen hovers near a 15-year high against the dollar, threatening to harm Japan's exporters and derail a feeble recovery from the global crisis.\par \par \uc2  "There's no predicting any action beyond those (comments)," Otsuka told Reuters in an interview, referring to the possibility of \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 .\par \par \uc2  "I'm not in a position to comment on that, but \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  (rates) seem to have come to something of a critical juncture."\par \par \uc2  The dollar remained within sight of a 15-year low versus the yen on Wednesday, dipping 0.2 percent from late U.S. trading on Tuesday to 85.32 yen.\par \par \uc2  The greenback hit an eight-month low of 85.02 yen on trading platform EBS last week.\par \par \uc2  On \b \highlight22\uc2 currency intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2 , Otsuka said \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  would need to win agreement on coordinated action from Europe and the United States to stem a rapid rise in the yen as a move by Tokyo alone would hardly help.\par \par \uc2  He voiced concern about the effects of a rapid rise in the yen on deflation, which has plagued Japan for much of the past decade.\par \par \uc2  "A rapid yen rise would boost deflationary factors, so the government and the BOJ must act as one in considering what to do given our commitment to act against deflation," Otsuka said.\par \par \uc2  Japan's \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  held off on new policy steps to combat a stronger yen at a board meeting that ended on Tuesday, saving its limited firepower in case the \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2 's rise accelerates and threatens the country's fragile economic recovery.\par \par \uc2  Otsuka, a former BOJ official well-versed in monetary policy, underscored that point, saying the BOJ will need to act if \b \highlight22\uc2 currency\b0 \highlight\uc2  moves have a big impact on the economy.\par \par \uc2  While praising the BOJ's response to the financial crisis in the wake of the collapse of \uc2 Lehman Brothers\uc2  in 2008, Otsuka said the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  could do more depending on changes in the economy.\par \par \uc2  "Considering the common sense that prevailed in monetary policy 10 to 15 years ago ... I can highly evaluate the BOJ's efforts in meeting the challenges in a (new) policy frontier," he said.\par \par \uc2  "The economy including \b \highlight22\uc2 currencies\b0 \highlight\uc2  is ever-changing, it's a living thing, so it's impossible for central banks to say they cannot do more."\par \par \uc2  Otsuka did not elaborate on what the should be done by the BOJ, which has jurisdiction over monetary policy, but he saw no need for the bank to adopt a rigid inflation target to beat deflation.\par \par \uc2  "The BOJ said it would not tolerate negative inflation, and that means it has virtually set inflation targeting ... While it has not achieved positive inflation, it would be meaningless to aim for a 2-3 percent inflation."  (Additional reporting by Sumio Ito; Editing by Michael Watson)     \par \par \uc2 JAPAN-ECONOMY/OTSUKA (INTERVIEW, TV, UDATE 1)|LANGEN|ABX|BNX|FUN   \par \par \uc2 Dokument LBA0000020100814e68b00032\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
