\par\par \b \uc2 Japan Trade Ministry Announces Emergency Strong Yen Survey-Nikkei\b0\par\par\uc2 336 \uc2 W\u246\'00\'F6rter\par \uc2 11 August 2010\par 16:42\par \uc2 Dow Jones International News\par \uc2 DJI\par \uc2 Englisch\par \uc2 (c) 2010 Dow Jones & Company, Inc.   \par \par \uc2 TOKYO (Dow Jones)--Japan's Ministry of Economy, Trade and Industry announced Wednesday it will conduct an emergency survey of approximately 200 companies to assess how the recently strong yen has affected their \b \highlight22\uc2 operations\b0 \highlight\uc2 , the Nikkei business daily reported on its online edition Wednesday.\par \par \uc2 The news comes as the dollar dropped to a 15-year low against the yen Wednesday at Y84.72, a development that is likely to increase concerns among Japanese exporters. A strong yen makes Japanese products more expensive overseas and eats into revenue sent back to Japan.\par \par \uc2 The trade ministry's survey will question firms on how the recently strong yen has impacted upon management issues related to exports, as well as sounding out how companies intend to deal with the effects of the strong yen going forward, according to the Nikkei.\par \par \uc2 Meanwhile, Minister of Economy, Trade and Industry Masayuki Naoshima said Wednesday that while the government and Bank of Japan are of the same mind on the strong yen problem, they have a difference of perspective on the issue, according to the business daily. The remarks come after the \b \highlight22\uc2 central bank\b0 \highlight\uc2  concluded a regular policy meeting Tuesday at which it refrained from taking any additional easing measures that analysts say would be one way to try to curb yen strength.\par \par \uc2 "The recent yen strength may exert a big impact on the Japanese economy, even in the medium- to long-term," the Nikkei reported Naoshima as saying. Japanese companies are subject to major \b \highlight22\uc2 foreign exchange\b0 \highlight\uc2  risk, necessitating consideration of whether present conditions are fair, Naoshima said, according to the Nikkei.\par \par \uc2 But echoing analysts' comments, Naoshima suggested unilateral \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  may be difficult for now. "While there could be a big effect if international coordinated \b \highlight22\uc2 intervention\b0 \highlight\uc2  were possible, it would be rather difficult for \b \highlight22\uc2 Japan\b0 \highlight\uc2  to \b \highlight22\uc2 intervene\b0 \highlight\uc2  on its own," Naoshima said, according to the Nikkei.\par \par \uc2 -By Andrew Monahan, Dow Jones Newswires; 81-3-6269-2783; andrew.monahan@dowjones.com [ 11-08-10 1442GMT ]\par \par \uc2 Dokument DJI0000020100811e68b001az\par }\page {\par\fs20{\*\shppict{\pict\pngblip\picw184\pich22\picscaley100\picscalex100\picwgoal2760\pichgoal330
